const Phase4Data = {"source":"phase-4-spatial-databases.md","sourceSha256":"58c209ed4eb6950a0f9758a97bf3f774bc2a3102a4a4b0a0f63771867a4d35bd","counts":{"sections":60,"tables":25,"codeBlocks":37,"diagrams":5,"equations":0},"sections":[{"key":"overview","id":"overview","title":"Phase 4 — Spatial Databases: PostGIS, Spatial SQL, pgRouting, Alternatives, and Data Modeling","anchor":"phase-4--spatial-databases-postgis-spatial-sql-pgrouting-alternatives-and-data-modeling","depth":1,"start":0,"topics":["foundations"],"end":914,"markdown":"# Phase 4 — Spatial Databases: PostGIS, Spatial SQL, pgRouting, Alternatives, and Data Modeling\n\n> **Study note · GIS Development · Level: developer new to spatial databases, comfortable with SQL basics**\n> **Version assumptions:** PostgreSQL 14–17, PostGIS 3.3+, GEOS 3.11+, pgRouting 3.x/4.x, DuckDB `spatial` extension 1.x. Function availability and output column names change between versions; when a section says *verify*, check the documentation for the version you installed.\n> **Conventions:** `EPSG:4326` = WGS 84 longitude/latitude in degrees (axis order used by PostGIS: **x = longitude, y = latitude**). `EPSG:28992` = Amersfoort / RD New (metres; used for the synthetic examples). `EPSG:3857` = Web Mercator (metres, strongly distorted away from the equator). All example coordinates are **synthetic** unless stated otherwise, and all ASCII drawings are **schematics, not scale-accurate maps**.\n\n---\n\n","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":8,"html":"<h2>Phase 4 — Spatial Databases: PostGIS, Spatial SQL, pgRouting, Alternatives, and Data Modeling</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Study note · GIS Development · Level: developer new to spatial databases, comfortable with SQL basics</strong>\n<strong>Version assumptions:</strong> PostgreSQL 14–17, PostGIS 3.3+, GEOS 3.11+, pgRouting 3.x/4.x, DuckDB <code>spatial</code> extension 1.x. Function availability and output column names change between versions; when a section says <em>verify</em>, check the documentation for the version you installed.\n<strong>Conventions:</strong> <code>EPSG:4326</code> = WGS 84 longitude/latitude in degrees (axis order used by PostGIS: <strong>x = longitude, y = latitude</strong>). <code>EPSG:28992</code> = Amersfoort / RD New (metres; used for the synthetic examples). <code>EPSG:3857</code> = Web Mercator (metres, strongly distorted away from the equator). All example coordinates are <strong>synthetic</strong> unless stated otherwise, and all ASCII drawings are <strong>schematics, not scale-accurate maps</strong>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr>\n","sha256":"4102c1562ab815a82997f30baac57427c70ebfaa1124d562eab44f3013bb8d52"},{"key":"1","id":"1","title":"1. Title, Learning Objectives, Prerequisites","anchor":"1-title-learning-objectives-prerequisites","depth":2,"start":914,"topics":["foundations"],"end":963,"markdown":"## 1. Title, Learning Objectives, Prerequisites\n\n","lineStart":9,"lineEnd":10,"html":"<h2>1. Title, Learning Objectives, Prerequisites</h2>\n","sha256":"2fb647d8f8ea4ab764a0b83e52f6b0ccf4554a987ea355333a2cb946507cf00c"},{"key":"1.1","id":"1-1","title":"1.1 Learning objectives","anchor":"11-learning-objectives","depth":3,"start":963,"topics":["foundations"],"end":2010,"markdown":"### 1.1 Learning objectives\n\nAfter completing this note you should be able to:\n\n1. Choose between PostGIS `geometry`, `geography`, `raster`, and `topology` models and justify the choice (units, accuracy, function coverage, performance).\n2. Create and tune **GiST** (and recognise **SP-GiST / BRIN**) spatial indexes, and explain why `ST_Distance(a,b) < d` cannot use them while `ST_DWithin(a,b,d)` can.\n3. Read `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` output for spatial queries and explain the **filter-and-refine** execution model.\n4. Write correct spatial SQL for **k-nearest-neighbour (KNN)**, **distance-bounded search**, **clustering**, and **vector-tile generation**.\n5. Build a small routable graph, run **pgRouting** shortest-path, many-to-many cost, and service-area queries, and verify the results by hand.\n6. Compare PostGIS with **SpatiaLite/GeoPackage**, **DuckDB spatial**, **Oracle Spatial**, and **SQL Server spatial**.\n7. Design schemas that handle **versioning**, **valid-time data**, **topology rules**, and **data-quality constraints**.\n\n","lineStart":11,"lineEnd":22,"html":"<h2>1.1 Learning objectives</h2>\n<p>After completing this note you should be able to:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Choose between PostGIS <code>geometry</code>, <code>geography</code>, <code>raster</code>, and <code>topology</code> models and justify the choice (units, accuracy, function coverage, performance).</li>\n<li>Create and tune <strong>GiST</strong> (and recognise <strong>SP-GiST / BRIN</strong>) spatial indexes, and explain why <code>ST_Distance(a,b) &lt; d</code> cannot use them while <code>ST_DWithin(a,b,d)</code> can.</li>\n<li>Read <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code> output for spatial queries and explain the <strong>filter-and-refine</strong> execution model.</li>\n<li>Write correct spatial SQL for <strong>k-nearest-neighbour (KNN)</strong>, <strong>distance-bounded search</strong>, <strong>clustering</strong>, and <strong>vector-tile generation</strong>.</li>\n<li>Build a small routable graph, run <strong>pgRouting</strong> shortest-path, many-to-many cost, and service-area queries, and verify the results by hand.</li>\n<li>Compare PostGIS with <strong>SpatiaLite/GeoPackage</strong>, <strong>DuckDB spatial</strong>, <strong>Oracle Spatial</strong>, and <strong>SQL Server spatial</strong>.</li>\n<li>Design schemas that handle <strong>versioning</strong>, <strong>valid-time data</strong>, <strong>topology rules</strong>, and <strong>data-quality constraints</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n","sha256":"6d9a1ff48afe712fd388c9f0d137d4cac3840eb3ec0d6ae06d761c9f1b4f59f9"},{"key":"1.2","id":"1-2","title":"1.2 Prerequisites","anchor":"12-prerequisites","depth":3,"start":2010,"topics":["foundations"],"end":2447,"markdown":"### 1.2 Prerequisites\n\n| Area | What you need |\n|---|---|\n| SQL | `SELECT`, joins, aggregates, `CREATE TABLE`, indexes, `EXPLAIN` basics |\n| GIS (Phases 1–3) | CRS vs datum, EPSG codes, axis order, OGC Simple Features, geometry validity, GeoJSON/GeoPackage, GDAL/OGR basics |\n| Tooling | A PostgreSQL + PostGIS instance (Docker image `postgis/postgis` or a local install), `psql` or any SQL client, optionally QGIS to eyeball results |\n\n","lineStart":23,"lineEnd":30,"html":"<h2>1.2 Prerequisites</h2>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 1.2 Prerequisites\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Area</th>\n<th>What you need</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>SQL</td>\n<td><code>SELECT</code>, joins, aggregates, <code>CREATE TABLE</code>, indexes, <code>EXPLAIN</code> basics</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>GIS (Phases 1–3)</td>\n<td>CRS vs datum, EPSG codes, axis order, OGC Simple Features, geometry validity, GeoJSON/GeoPackage, GDAL/OGR basics</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tooling</td>\n<td>A PostgreSQL + PostGIS instance (Docker image <code>postgis/postgis</code> or a local install), <code>psql</code> or any SQL client, optionally QGIS to eyeball results</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>","sha256":"4f97c1f599f767fb0b7ceeb29355079c0087b0333d4c906751b9ed6851aaddeb"},{"key":"1.3","id":"1-3","title":"1.3 Suggested 3–4 week learning path","anchor":"13-suggested-34-week-learning-path","depth":3,"start":2447,"topics":["foundations"],"end":2913,"markdown":"### 1.3 Suggested 3–4 week learning path\n\n| Week | Focus | Sections | Checkpoint |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | PostGIS foundations: types, SRIDs, `ST_*`, GiST | §2, §4.1–4.3 | Exercise 1 |\n| 2 | Query planning and spatial SQL patterns (KNN, `ST_DWithin`, clustering, tiling) | §4.4–4.5, §5 | Exercise 2 |\n| 3 | pgRouting and alternative engines | §4.6–4.7 | Exercise 3 |\n| 4 | Data modeling, quality, and the capstone | §4.8, §6 | Exercise 4 (applied challenge) |\n\n---\n\n","lineStart":31,"lineEnd":41,"html":"<h2>1.3 Suggested 3–4 week learning path</h2>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 1.3 Suggested 3–4 week learning path\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Week</th>\n<th>Focus</th>\n<th>Sections</th>\n<th>Checkpoint</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>PostGIS foundations: types, SRIDs, <code>ST_*</code>, GiST</td>\n<td>§2, §4.1–4.3</td>\n<td>Exercise 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Query planning and spatial SQL patterns (KNN, <code>ST_DWithin</code>, clustering, tiling)</td>\n<td>§4.4–4.5, §5</td>\n<td>Exercise 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>pgRouting and alternative engines</td>\n<td>§4.6–4.7</td>\n<td>Exercise 3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Data modeling, quality, and the capstone</td>\n<td>§4.8, §6</td>\n<td>Exercise 4 (applied challenge)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><hr>\n","sha256":"f0b0b09a241b293fe39d073164c09f16a4d1ea32f45fbc2fc181c712dca4a7c7"},{"key":"2","id":"2","title":"2. Conceptual Foundations","anchor":"2-conceptual-foundations","depth":2,"start":2913,"topics":["foundations"],"end":2943,"markdown":"## 2. Conceptual Foundations\n\n","lineStart":42,"lineEnd":43,"html":"<h2>2. Conceptual Foundations</h2>\n","sha256":"669286fc53e957cf2cf515f5158b6124b9d2850f48ee501a04103f8158818ccf"},{"key":"2.1","id":"2-1","title":"2.1 What is a spatial database?","anchor":"21-what-is-a-spatial-database","depth":3,"start":2943,"topics":["foundations"],"end":3733,"markdown":"### 2.1 What is a spatial database?\n\nA **spatial database** is a database management system that stores geometric/geographic values as first-class types and provides (a) **spatial functions** (distance, intersection, buffering, …), (b) **spatial indexes** that make spatial predicates fast, and (c) usually **metadata** about coordinate reference systems. A plain relational database can store coordinates in two `float` columns, but it cannot index \"which rectangles overlap this window\" efficiently, and it knows nothing about CRSs.\n\n**PostGIS** is the spatial extension for PostgreSQL. It adds types (`geometry`, `geography`, `raster`, `box2d`, `box3d`, …), several hundred `ST_*` functions, GiST/SP-GiST/BRIN index support, and the `spatial_ref_sys` table that stores CRS definitions.\n\n","lineStart":44,"lineEnd":49,"html":"<h2>2.1 What is a spatial database?</h2>\n<p>A <strong>spatial database</strong> is a database management system that stores geometric/geographic values as first-class types and provides (a) <strong>spatial functions</strong> (distance, intersection, buffering, …), (b) <strong>spatial indexes</strong> that make spatial predicates fast, and (c) usually <strong>metadata</strong> about coordinate reference systems. A plain relational database can store coordinates in two <code>float</code> columns, but it cannot index &quot;which rectangles overlap this window&quot; efficiently, and it knows nothing about CRSs.</p>\n<p><strong>PostGIS</strong> is the spatial extension for PostgreSQL. It adds types (<code>geometry</code>, <code>geography</code>, <code>raster</code>, <code>box2d</code>, <code>box3d</code>, …), several hundred <code>ST_*</code> functions, GiST/SP-GiST/BRIN index support, and the <code>spatial_ref_sys</code> table that stores CRS definitions.</p>\n","sha256":"9acca81394cbb9f7126f4990fbb0f5d67140d6c57b311a59de1dce694204289c"},{"key":"2.2","id":"2-2","title":"2.2 Key terminology","anchor":"22-key-terminology","depth":3,"start":3733,"topics":["foundations"],"end":5330,"markdown":"### 2.2 Key terminology\n\n| Term | Definition |\n|---|---|\n| **SRID** | Spatial Reference ID stored *inside* each PostGIS geometry; usually an EPSG code. `0` means \"unknown\". |\n| **Simple Features** | OGC/ISO model: `Point`, `LineString`, `Polygon`, `Multi*`, `GeometryCollection`. |\n| **WKT / WKB / EWKT / EWKB** | Text/binary encodings of geometries. The \"E\" variants (PostGIS extensions) embed the SRID, e.g. `SRID=4326;POINT(4.9 52.37)`. |\n| **Bounding box (bbox / MBR)** | Smallest axis-aligned rectangle enclosing a geometry. Indexes store bboxes, not full shapes. |\n| **Typmod** | Column type modifier: `geometry(Point, 28992)` constrains type, SRID, and (optionally) dimensionality. |\n| **GiST** | *Generalized Search Tree*. PostgreSQL's balanced-tree framework; PostGIS uses it to implement an **R-tree-like** index over bboxes. |\n| **Filter-and-refine** | Two-phase evaluation: the index quickly returns *candidates* by bbox (filter); exact geometry tests then discard false positives (refine). |\n| **KNN** | k-nearest neighbours: return the *k* features closest to a query location. |\n| **DE-9IM** | *Dimensionally Extended 9-Intersection Model*: a 3×3 matrix describing how the interior, boundary, and exterior of two geometries intersect. All named predicates (`ST_Contains`, `ST_Touches`, …) are patterns over it. |\n| **Topology (graph sense)** | Explicit nodes/edges/faces with shared boundaries stored once; guarantees no gaps/overlaps by construction. |\n| **Valid time** | The period during which a fact is true *in the real world* (as opposed to when the database recorded it). |\n\n","lineStart":50,"lineEnd":65,"html":"<h2>2.2 Key terminology</h2>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 2.2 Key terminology\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Definition</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>SRID</strong></td>\n<td>Spatial Reference ID stored <em>inside</em> each PostGIS geometry; usually an EPSG code. <code>0</code> means &quot;unknown&quot;.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Simple Features</strong></td>\n<td>OGC/ISO model: <code>Point</code>, <code>LineString</code>, <code>Polygon</code>, <code>Multi*</code>, <code>GeometryCollection</code>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>WKT / WKB / EWKT / EWKB</strong></td>\n<td>Text/binary encodings of geometries. The &quot;E&quot; variants (PostGIS extensions) embed the SRID, e.g. <code>SRID=4326;POINT(4.9 52.37)</code>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Bounding box (bbox / MBR)</strong></td>\n<td>Smallest axis-aligned rectangle enclosing a geometry. Indexes store bboxes, not full shapes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Typmod</strong></td>\n<td>Column type modifier: <code>geometry(Point, 28992)</code> constrains type, SRID, and (optionally) dimensionality.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>GiST</strong></td>\n<td><em>Generalized Search Tree</em>. PostgreSQL&#39;s balanced-tree framework; PostGIS uses it to implement an <strong>R-tree-like</strong> index over bboxes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Filter-and-refine</strong></td>\n<td>Two-phase evaluation: the index quickly returns <em>candidates</em> by bbox (filter); exact geometry tests then discard false positives (refine).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>KNN</strong></td>\n<td>k-nearest neighbours: return the <em>k</em> features closest to a query location.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>DE-9IM</strong></td>\n<td><em>Dimensionally Extended 9-Intersection Model</em>: a 3×3 matrix describing how the interior, boundary, and exterior of two geometries intersect. All named predicates (<code>ST_Contains</code>, <code>ST_Touches</code>, …) are patterns over it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Topology (graph sense)</strong></td>\n<td>Explicit nodes/edges/faces with shared boundaries stored once; guarantees no gaps/overlaps by construction.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Valid time</strong></td>\n<td>The period during which a fact is true <em>in the real world</em> (as opposed to when the database recorded it).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>","sha256":"47f2f75a3779ea3895a0718a0d3d6352d74a8f0dd995d9c45c1a0ba33926159a"},{"key":"2.3","id":"2-3","title":"2.3 `geometry` vs `geography`","anchor":"23-geometry-vs-geography","depth":3,"start":5330,"topics":["foundations"],"end":7659,"markdown":"### 2.3 `geometry` vs `geography`\n\n| Aspect | `geometry` | `geography` |\n|---|---|---|\n| Model | Planar (Cartesian) | Ellipsoidal (angular coordinates on the WGS 84 spheroid by default) |\n| Coordinates | Any CRS (projected or geographic) | Longitude/latitude only (SRID 4326 default) |\n| Distance/length units | CRS units (**degrees** if SRID is 4326!) | **Metres** |\n| Area units | CRS units² | m² |\n| Function coverage | Very large | Smaller subset (e.g. `ST_Distance`, `ST_DWithin`, `ST_Area`, `ST_Length`, `ST_Intersects`, `ST_Buffer`, `ST_Covers`, …) |\n| Speed | Faster (planar math) | Slower (spheroidal math) |\n| Best for | Local/regional work in a suitable projected CRS; heavy analysis; tiling | Global datasets, \"how many metres between two lon/lat points\" with minimal CRS thinking |\n\n**Rule of thumb:** for a regional dataset, store `geometry` in a **local projected CRS** (e.g. EPSG:28992 in the Netherlands, a UTM zone elsewhere) and do metric work there. Use `geography` when data is global or you want correct metres without choosing a projection. Avoid doing metric work on `geometry` in EPSG:4326 (the result is in degrees) or in EPSG:3857 (distances inflated by roughly `1/cos(latitude)`; at 52.4° N that is ≈ 1.64×, areas ≈ 2.7×).\n\nWorked comparison (verifiable): two points on the equator, one degree of longitude apart.\n\n```sql\nSELECT ST_Distance('SRID=4326;POINT(0 0)'::geometry,\n                   'SRID=4326;POINT(1 0)'::geometry);      -- 1            (degrees)\n\nSELECT ST_Distance('SRID=4326;POINT(0 0)'::geography,\n                   'SRID=4326;POINT(1 0)'::geography);     -- ≈ 111319.49  (metres)\n```\n\nThe geography result equals `a · π/180` with the WGS 84 semi-major axis `a = 6 378 137 m`: `6378137 × 0.0174532925 ≈ 111 319.49 m`. (Along a meridian from latitude 0° to 1°, the result is ≈ 110 574 m, because the ellipsoid's radius of curvature differs by direction.)\n\nFor reference, the spherical (haversine) formula used by simple spherical functions is\n\n```\nd = 2R · asin( sqrt( sin²(Δφ/2) + cos φ₁ · cos φ₂ · sin²(Δλ/2) ) )\n```\n\nwith φ = latitude, λ = longitude in radians, and `R ≈ 6 371 008.8 m` (mean Earth radius). PostGIS `geography` functions use a spheroidal solution (GeographicLib) by default for `ST_Distance`, so results differ slightly (up to ~0.3%) from a spherical calculation.\n\n","lineStart":66,"lineEnd":99,"html":"<h2>2.3 <code>geometry</code> vs <code>geography</code></h2>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 2.3 `geometry` vs `geography`\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Aspect</th>\n<th><code>geometry</code></th>\n<th><code>geography</code></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Model</td>\n<td>Planar (Cartesian)</td>\n<td>Ellipsoidal (angular coordinates on the WGS 84 spheroid by default)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Coordinates</td>\n<td>Any CRS (projected or geographic)</td>\n<td>Longitude/latitude only (SRID 4326 default)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Distance/length units</td>\n<td>CRS units (<strong>degrees</strong> if SRID is 4326!)</td>\n<td><strong>Metres</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Area units</td>\n<td>CRS units²</td>\n<td>m²</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Function coverage</td>\n<td>Very large</td>\n<td>Smaller subset (e.g. <code>ST_Distance</code>, <code>ST_DWithin</code>, <code>ST_Area</code>, <code>ST_Length</code>, <code>ST_Intersects</code>, <code>ST_Buffer</code>, <code>ST_Covers</code>, …)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Speed</td>\n<td>Faster (planar math)</td>\n<td>Slower (spheroidal math)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Best for</td>\n<td>Local/regional work in a suitable projected CRS; heavy analysis; tiling</td>\n<td>Global datasets, &quot;how many metres between two lon/lat points&quot; with minimal CRS thinking</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> for a regional dataset, store <code>geometry</code> in a <strong>local projected CRS</strong> (e.g. EPSG:28992 in the Netherlands, a UTM zone elsewhere) and do metric work there. Use <code>geography</code> when data is global or you want correct metres without choosing a projection. Avoid doing metric work on <code>geometry</code> in EPSG:4326 (the result is in degrees) or in EPSG:3857 (distances inflated by roughly <code>1/cos(latitude)</code>; at 52.4° N that is ≈ 1.64×, areas ≈ 2.7×).</p>\n<p>Worked comparison (verifiable): two points on the equator, one degree of longitude apart.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT ST_Distance(&#39;SRID=4326;POINT(0 0)&#39;::geometry,\n                   &#39;SRID=4326;POINT(1 0)&#39;::geometry);      -- 1            (degrees)\n\nSELECT ST_Distance(&#39;SRID=4326;POINT(0 0)&#39;::geography,\n                   &#39;SRID=4326;POINT(1 0)&#39;::geography);     -- ≈ 111319.49  (metres)</code></pre><p>The geography result equals <code>a · π/180</code> with the WGS 84 semi-major axis <code>a = 6 378 137 m</code>: <code>6378137 × 0.0174532925 ≈ 111 319.49 m</code>. (Along a meridian from latitude 0° to 1°, the result is ≈ 110 574 m, because the ellipsoid&#39;s radius of curvature differs by direction.)</p>\n<p>For reference, the spherical (haversine) formula used by simple spherical functions is</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">d = 2R · asin( sqrt( sin²(Δφ/2) + cos φ₁ · cos φ₂ · sin²(Δλ/2) ) )</code></pre><p>with φ = latitude, λ = longitude in radians, and <code>R ≈ 6 371 008.8 m</code> (mean Earth radius). PostGIS <code>geography</code> functions use a spheroidal solution (GeographicLib) by default for <code>ST_Distance</code>, so results differ slightly (up to ~0.3%) from a spherical calculation.</p>\n","sha256":"1543420ba5737c8d58257581afa362a06105a7ae79914d76f29415dd93a95e9c"},{"key":"2.4","id":"2-4","title":"2.4 Spatial relationships and DE-9IM","anchor":"24-spatial-relationships-and-de-9im","depth":3,"start":7659,"topics":["relationships"],"end":9028,"markdown":"### 2.4 Spatial relationships and DE-9IM\n\nThe DE-9IM matrix is read row-wise: rows = Interior, Boundary, Exterior of geometry **A**; columns = Interior, Boundary, Exterior of **B**. Each cell holds the dimension of the intersection: `F` (empty), `0` (point), `1` (line), `2` (area). `T` means \"any non-empty\".\n\n| Predicate | Pattern (`ST_Relate` mask) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `ST_Intersects(A,B)` | not disjoint (any of `T********`, `*T*******`, `***T*****`, `****T****`) | True if they share *any* point, boundary included |\n| `ST_Contains(A,B)` | `T*****FF*` | No point of B lies in A's exterior **and** B touches A's *interior*. A point **on** A's boundary is *not* contained |\n| `ST_Covers(A,B)` | `T*****FF*` or `*T****FF*` or `***T**FF*` or `****T*FF*` | Like contains, but boundary contact is allowed |\n| `ST_Within(A,B)` | `T*F**F***` | `ST_Contains` with arguments swapped |\n| `ST_Touches(A,B)` | `FT*******`, `F**T*****`, or `F***T****` | Boundaries meet, interiors do not |\n\nTwo matrices you will meet in the worked example (A = polygon, B = point):\n\n```\nPoint strictly inside the polygon      ST_Relate = 0F2FF1FF2\nPoint exactly on the polygon boundary  ST_Relate = FF20F1FF2\n```\n\nThe second matrix has `F` in the (Interior, Interior) cell, which is why `ST_Contains` is **false** for a boundary point while `ST_Intersects` and `ST_Covers` are **true**.\n\n","lineStart":100,"lineEnd":120,"html":"<h2>2.4 Spatial relationships and DE-9IM</h2>\n<p>The DE-9IM matrix is read row-wise: rows = Interior, Boundary, Exterior of geometry <strong>A</strong>; columns = Interior, Boundary, Exterior of <strong>B</strong>. Each cell holds the dimension of the intersection: <code>F</code> (empty), <code>0</code> (point), <code>1</code> (line), <code>2</code> (area). <code>T</code> means &quot;any non-empty&quot;.</p>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 2.4 Spatial relationships and DE-9IM\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Predicate</th>\n<th>Pattern (<code>ST_Relate</code> mask)</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>ST_Intersects(A,B)</code></td>\n<td>not disjoint (any of <code>T********</code>, <code>*T*******</code>, <code>***T*****</code>, <code>****T****</code>)</td>\n<td>True if they share <em>any</em> point, boundary included</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ST_Contains(A,B)</code></td>\n<td><code>T*****FF*</code></td>\n<td>No point of B lies in A&#39;s exterior <strong>and</strong> B touches A&#39;s <em>interior</em>. A point <strong>on</strong> A&#39;s boundary is <em>not</em> contained</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ST_Covers(A,B)</code></td>\n<td><code>T*****FF*</code> or <code>*T****FF*</code> or <code>***T**FF*</code> or <code>****T*FF*</code></td>\n<td>Like contains, but boundary contact is allowed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ST_Within(A,B)</code></td>\n<td><code>T*F**F***</code></td>\n<td><code>ST_Contains</code> with arguments swapped</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ST_Touches(A,B)</code></td>\n<td><code>FT*******</code>, <code>F**T*****</code>, or <code>F***T****</code></td>\n<td>Boundaries meet, interiors do not</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p>Two matrices you will meet in the worked example (A = polygon, B = point):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Point strictly inside the polygon      ST_Relate = 0F2FF1FF2\nPoint exactly on the polygon boundary  ST_Relate = FF20F1FF2</code></pre><p>The second matrix has <code>F</code> in the (Interior, Interior) cell, which is why <code>ST_Contains</code> is <strong>false</strong> for a boundary point while <code>ST_Intersects</code> and <code>ST_Covers</code> are <strong>true</strong>.</p>\n","sha256":"cbaacc236f6c007189c168c830bd54041c39200d0a7f1c059a8ad5cf48419ad2"},{"key":"2.5","id":"2-5","title":"2.5 Why spatial indexes exist: bbox filtering","anchor":"25-why-spatial-indexes-exist-bbox-filtering","depth":3,"start":9028,"topics":["indexes"],"end":9651,"markdown":"### 2.5 Why spatial indexes exist: bbox filtering\n\nTesting every pair of geometries is O(n·m) with expensive exact tests. A spatial index stores each feature's bbox in a tree. A query window is compared against node bboxes, so whole subtrees are skipped. Two bboxes overlap if and only if\n\n```\na.xmin ≤ b.xmax  AND  a.xmax ≥ b.xmin  AND  a.ymin ≤ b.ymax  AND  a.ymax ≥ b.ymin\n```\n\nThis is exactly what the PostGIS operator `&&` computes. Because bboxes over-approximate shapes, the index can return **false positives** (diagonal lines and L-shaped polygons have large, mostly empty bboxes) — hence the *refine* step.\n\n---\n\n","lineStart":121,"lineEnd":132,"html":"<h2>2.5 Why spatial indexes exist: bbox filtering</h2>\n<p>Testing every pair of geometries is O(n·m) with expensive exact tests. A spatial index stores each feature&#39;s bbox in a tree. A query window is compared against node bboxes, so whole subtrees are skipped. Two bboxes overlap if and only if</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">a.xmin ≤ b.xmax  AND  a.xmax ≥ b.xmin  AND  a.ymin ≤ b.ymax  AND  a.ymax ≥ b.ymin</code></pre><p>This is exactly what the PostGIS operator <code>&amp;&amp;</code> computes. Because bboxes over-approximate shapes, the index can return <strong>false positives</strong> (diagonal lines and L-shaped polygons have large, mostly empty bboxes) — hence the <em>refine</em> step.</p>\n<hr>\n","sha256":"8b4eecb3a28973dc4f75afb8d676e0807e2410f8d793a57fcc23d4502f54e210"},{"key":"3","id":"3","title":"3. Visual Explanations","anchor":"3-visual-explanations","depth":2,"start":9651,"topics":["foundations"],"end":9678,"markdown":"## 3. Visual Explanations\n\n","lineStart":133,"lineEnd":134,"html":"<h2>3. Visual Explanations</h2>\n","sha256":"3aad34e03150c9d568e2393f089e87b41aea3ceae58132d6e78f0fdb43f1286d"},{"key":"3.1","id":"3-1","title":"3.1 Conceptual overview: the Phase 4 landscape","anchor":"31-conceptual-overview-the-phase-4-landscape","depth":3,"start":9678,"topics":["foundations"],"end":11058,"markdown":"### 3.1 Conceptual overview: the Phase 4 landscape\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    SDB[\"Spatial database\"]\n    SDB --> TYPES[\"Data types\"]\n    SDB --> INDEX[\"Indexing and planning\"]\n    SDB --> SQLP[\"Spatial SQL patterns\"]\n    SDB --> NET[\"Network analysis\"]\n    SDB --> MODEL[\"Data modeling\"]\n    SDB --> ALT[\"Alternative engines\"]\n\n    TYPES --> T1[\"geometry (planar)\"]\n    TYPES --> T2[\"geography (ellipsoidal)\"]\n    TYPES --> T3[\"raster, topology\"]\n\n    INDEX --> I1[\"GiST bbox index\"]\n    INDEX --> I2[\"Filter and refine\"]\n    INDEX --> I3[\"EXPLAIN ANALYZE, ANALYZE stats\"]\n\n    SQLP --> S1[\"KNN with the distance operator\"]\n    SQLP --> S2[\"ST_DWithin vs ST_Distance\"]\n    SQLP --> S3[\"Clustering: DBSCAN, KMeans, grids\"]\n    SQLP --> S4[\"Tiling: ST_TileEnvelope, ST_AsMVT\"]\n\n    NET --> N1[\"pgRouting: Dijkstra, A*, service areas\"]\n    MODEL --> M1[\"Valid-time versioning\"]\n    MODEL --> M2[\"Topology-aware schemas\"]\n    MODEL --> M3[\"Quality constraints\"]\n    ALT --> A1[\"SpatiaLite / GeoPackage\"]\n    ALT --> A2[\"DuckDB spatial\"]\n    ALT --> A3[\"Oracle Spatial / SQL Server\"]\n```\n\n**How to read it:** the centre node is the capability; each branch is a skill area in this phase. Types determine *what is stored*, indexing/planning determines *how fast it can be queried*, SQL patterns are *how you ask*, and modeling decides *whether the data stays trustworthy over time*.\n\n","lineStart":135,"lineEnd":170,"html":"<h2>3.1 Conceptual overview: the Phase 4 landscape</h2>\n<figure class=\"source-diagram\"><a href=\"assets/figures/phase4-3-1.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/figures/phase4-3-1.svg\" alt=\"3.1 Conceptual overview: the Phase 4 landscape — source diagram; explanation follows.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption>Source diagram · <a href=\"assets/figures/phase4-3-1.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Enlarge ↗</a></figcaption></figure><details class=\"source-code\"><summary>Mermaid diagram definition</summary><pre><code class=\"language-mermaid\">flowchart TD\n    SDB[&quot;Spatial database&quot;]\n    SDB --&gt; TYPES[&quot;Data types&quot;]\n    SDB --&gt; INDEX[&quot;Indexing and planning&quot;]\n    SDB --&gt; SQLP[&quot;Spatial SQL patterns&quot;]\n    SDB --&gt; NET[&quot;Network analysis&quot;]\n    SDB --&gt; MODEL[&quot;Data modeling&quot;]\n    SDB --&gt; ALT[&quot;Alternative engines&quot;]\n\n    TYPES --&gt; T1[&quot;geometry (planar)&quot;]\n    TYPES --&gt; T2[&quot;geography (ellipsoidal)&quot;]\n    TYPES --&gt; T3[&quot;raster, topology&quot;]\n\n    INDEX --&gt; I1[&quot;GiST bbox index&quot;]\n    INDEX --&gt; I2[&quot;Filter and refine&quot;]\n    INDEX --&gt; I3[&quot;EXPLAIN ANALYZE, ANALYZE stats&quot;]\n\n    SQLP --&gt; S1[&quot;KNN with the distance operator&quot;]\n    SQLP --&gt; S2[&quot;ST_DWithin vs ST_Distance&quot;]\n    SQLP --&gt; S3[&quot;Clustering: DBSCAN, KMeans, grids&quot;]\n    SQLP --&gt; S4[&quot;Tiling: ST_TileEnvelope, ST_AsMVT&quot;]\n\n    NET --&gt; N1[&quot;pgRouting: Dijkstra, A*, service areas&quot;]\n    MODEL --&gt; M1[&quot;Valid-time versioning&quot;]\n    MODEL --&gt; M2[&quot;Topology-aware schemas&quot;]\n    MODEL --&gt; M3[&quot;Quality constraints&quot;]\n    ALT --&gt; A1[&quot;SpatiaLite / GeoPackage&quot;]\n    ALT --&gt; A2[&quot;DuckDB spatial&quot;]\n    ALT --&gt; A3[&quot;Oracle Spatial / SQL Server&quot;]</code></pre></details><p><strong>How to read it:</strong> the centre node is the capability; each branch is a skill area in this phase. Types determine <em>what is stored</em>, indexing/planning determines <em>how fast it can be queried</em>, SQL patterns are <em>how you ask</em>, and modeling decides <em>whether the data stays trustworthy over time</em>.</p>\n","sha256":"9ead64dddfd2975920e6735e9c5be0ffe5ac565fc9d21954cfa188a468ef1e74"},{"key":"3.2","id":"3-2","title":"3.2 Workflow: filter-and-refine for a spatial predicate","anchor":"32-workflow-filter-and-refine-for-a-spatial-predicate","depth":3,"start":11058,"topics":["indexes"],"end":11707,"markdown":"### 3.2 Workflow: filter-and-refine for a spatial predicate\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    Q[\"Query: ST_DWithin(geom, point, 2000)\"] --> P[\"Planner: pick index scan vs seq scan using column statistics\"]\n    P --> F[\"Filter: GiST index finds rows whose bbox overlaps the point bbox expanded by 2000\"]\n    F --> C[\"Candidate rows, may include false positives\"]\n    C --> R[\"Refine: exact distance test on full geometry\"]\n    R --> OUT[\"Result rows\"]\n```\n\n**Interpretation:** the index never answers \"is it within 2000 m\"; it answers \"could it be\". The cost of the refine step grows with vertex count, which is why large polygons are subdivided (§4.4).\n\n","lineStart":171,"lineEnd":183,"html":"<h2>3.2 Workflow: filter-and-refine for a spatial predicate</h2>\n<figure class=\"source-diagram\"><a href=\"assets/figures/phase4-3-2.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/figures/phase4-3-2.svg\" alt=\"3.2 Workflow: filter-and-refine for a spatial predicate — source diagram; explanation follows.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption>Source diagram · <a href=\"assets/figures/phase4-3-2.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Enlarge ↗</a></figcaption></figure><details class=\"source-code\"><summary>Mermaid diagram definition</summary><pre><code class=\"language-mermaid\">flowchart LR\n    Q[&quot;Query: ST_DWithin(geom, point, 2000)&quot;] --&gt; P[&quot;Planner: pick index scan vs seq scan using column statistics&quot;]\n    P --&gt; F[&quot;Filter: GiST index finds rows whose bbox overlaps the point bbox expanded by 2000&quot;]\n    F --&gt; C[&quot;Candidate rows, may include false positives&quot;]\n    C --&gt; R[&quot;Refine: exact distance test on full geometry&quot;]\n    R --&gt; OUT[&quot;Result rows&quot;]</code></pre></details><p><strong>Interpretation:</strong> the index never answers &quot;is it within 2000 m&quot;; it answers &quot;could it be&quot;. The cost of the refine step grows with vertex count, which is why large polygons are subdivided (§4.4).</p>\n","sha256":"816ba3daac98148b8e1b8f1ecf5515ebe41f748d697a0ad9b7d17f85fd6c1342"},{"key":"3.3","id":"3-3","title":"3.3 Sequence: serving a vector tile from PostGIS","anchor":"33-sequence-serving-a-vector-tile-from-postgis","depth":3,"start":11707,"topics":["tiles"],"end":12326,"markdown":"### 3.3 Sequence: serving a vector tile from PostGIS\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    participant Browser as Web map client\n    participant Srv as Tile server\n    participant DB as PostGIS\n    Browser->>Srv: GET /tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.mvt\n    Srv->>DB: SELECT tile_fn(z, x, y)\n    DB->>DB: ST_TileEnvelope(z,x,y) gives bounds in EPSG:3857\n    DB->>DB: bbox filter using GiST index\n    DB->>DB: ST_AsMVTGeom clips and quantizes to tile grid\n    DB->>DB: ST_AsMVT aggregates rows into a protobuf tile\n    DB-->>Srv: bytea (Mapbox Vector Tile)\n    Srv-->>Browser: 200 OK, application/vnd.mapbox-vector-tile\n```\n\n","lineStart":184,"lineEnd":201,"html":"<h2>3.3 Sequence: serving a vector tile from PostGIS</h2>\n<figure class=\"source-diagram\"><a href=\"assets/figures/phase4-3-3.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/figures/phase4-3-3.svg\" alt=\"3.3 Sequence: serving a vector tile from PostGIS — source diagram; explanation follows.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption>Source diagram · <a href=\"assets/figures/phase4-3-3.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Enlarge ↗</a></figcaption></figure><details class=\"source-code\"><summary>Mermaid diagram definition</summary><pre><code class=\"language-mermaid\">sequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    participant Browser as Web map client\n    participant Srv as Tile server\n    participant DB as PostGIS\n    Browser-&gt;&gt;Srv: GET /tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.mvt\n    Srv-&gt;&gt;DB: SELECT tile_fn(z, x, y)\n    DB-&gt;&gt;DB: ST_TileEnvelope(z,x,y) gives bounds in EPSG:3857\n    DB-&gt;&gt;DB: bbox filter using GiST index\n    DB-&gt;&gt;DB: ST_AsMVTGeom clips and quantizes to tile grid\n    DB-&gt;&gt;DB: ST_AsMVT aggregates rows into a protobuf tile\n    DB--&gt;&gt;Srv: bytea (Mapbox Vector Tile)\n    Srv--&gt;&gt;Browser: 200 OK, application/vnd.mapbox-vector-tile</code></pre></details>","sha256":"7556866fd72ca04556f5a4b76f52e57556ebc0182ff1a04a7337d8d21a55c4dd"},{"key":"3.4","id":"3-4","title":"3.4 Data model: valid-time versioned features (used in §4.8 and the capstone)","anchor":"34-data-model-valid-time-versioned-features-used-in-48-and-the-capstone","depth":3,"start":12326,"topics":["registry"],"end":13067,"markdown":"### 3.4 Data model: valid-time versioned features (used in §4.8 and the capstone)\n\n```mermaid\nerDiagram\n    STATION ||--o{ STATION_VERSION : \"has versions\"\n    DISTRICT ||--o{ STATION_VERSION : \"spatially contains\"\n    STATION {\n        int station_id PK\n        text code\n    }\n    STATION_VERSION {\n        int version_id PK\n        int station_id FK\n        text name\n        geometry geom \"Point in EPSG 28992\"\n        tstzrange valid_during \"valid time\"\n    }\n    DISTRICT {\n        int district_id PK\n        text name\n        geometry geom \"Polygon in EPSG 28992\"\n    }\n```\n\nThe relationship between `DISTRICT` and `STATION_VERSION` is **derived spatially** (via `ST_Covers`), not by a foreign key — a common spatial-modeling idiom.\n\n","lineStart":202,"lineEnd":227,"html":"<h2>3.4 Data model: valid-time versioned features (used in §4.8 and the capstone)</h2>\n<figure class=\"source-diagram\"><a href=\"assets/figures/phase4-3-4.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/figures/phase4-3-4.svg\" alt=\"3.4 Data model: valid-time versioned features (used in §4.8 and the capstone) — source diagram; explanation follows.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption>Source diagram · <a href=\"assets/figures/phase4-3-4.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Enlarge ↗</a></figcaption></figure><details class=\"source-code\"><summary>Mermaid diagram definition</summary><pre><code class=\"language-mermaid\">erDiagram\n    STATION ||--o{ STATION_VERSION : &quot;has versions&quot;\n    DISTRICT ||--o{ STATION_VERSION : &quot;spatially contains&quot;\n    STATION {\n        int station_id PK\n        text code\n    }\n    STATION_VERSION {\n        int version_id PK\n        int station_id FK\n        text name\n        geometry geom &quot;Point in EPSG 28992&quot;\n        tstzrange valid_during &quot;valid time&quot;\n    }\n    DISTRICT {\n        int district_id PK\n        text name\n        geometry geom &quot;Polygon in EPSG 28992&quot;\n    }</code></pre></details><p>The relationship between <code>DISTRICT</code> and <code>STATION_VERSION</code> is <strong>derived spatially</strong> (via <code>ST_Covers</code>), not by a foreign key — a common spatial-modeling idiom.</p>\n","sha256":"86d2aa8624e4d6fa679fdf2b16fe85b7c9c9dfcf3c92ef8ece4aec3ddd367b94"},{"key":"3.5","id":"3-5","title":"3.5 Choosing an engine","anchor":"35-choosing-an-engine","depth":3,"start":13067,"topics":["engines"],"end":13744,"markdown":"### 3.5 Choosing an engine\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    START[\"New spatial workload\"] --> Q1{\"Multi-user transactional writes?\"}\n    Q1 -- Yes --> Q2{\"Existing enterprise standard?\"}\n    Q2 -- \"Oracle shop\" --> ORA[\"Oracle Spatial\"]\n    Q2 -- \"SQL Server shop\" --> MSS[\"SQL Server spatial\"]\n    Q2 -- \"No constraint\" --> PG[\"PostgreSQL + PostGIS\"]\n    Q1 -- No --> Q3{\"Analytical scans over big files, Parquet, GeoPackage?\"}\n    Q3 -- Yes --> DUCK[\"DuckDB spatial\"]\n    Q3 -- \"Embedded or single file, mobile, offline\" --> SQL[\"SpatiaLite or GeoPackage\"]\n    PG --> Q4{\"Need road routing?\"}\n    Q4 -- Yes --> PGR[\"Add pgRouting\"]\n    Q4 -- No --> DONE[\"PostGIS only\"]\n```\n\n---\n","lineStart":228,"lineEnd":245,"html":"<h2>3.5 Choosing an engine</h2>\n<figure class=\"source-diagram\"><a href=\"assets/figures/phase4-3-5.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/figures/phase4-3-5.svg\" alt=\"3.5 Choosing an engine — source diagram; explanation follows.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption>Source diagram · <a href=\"assets/figures/phase4-3-5.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Enlarge ↗</a></figcaption></figure><details class=\"source-code\"><summary>Mermaid diagram definition</summary><pre><code class=\"language-mermaid\">flowchart TD\n    START[&quot;New spatial workload&quot;] --&gt; Q1{&quot;Multi-user transactional writes?&quot;}\n    Q1 -- Yes --&gt; Q2{&quot;Existing enterprise standard?&quot;}\n    Q2 -- &quot;Oracle shop&quot; --&gt; ORA[&quot;Oracle Spatial&quot;]\n    Q2 -- &quot;SQL Server shop&quot; --&gt; MSS[&quot;SQL Server spatial&quot;]\n    Q2 -- &quot;No constraint&quot; --&gt; PG[&quot;PostgreSQL + PostGIS&quot;]\n    Q1 -- No --&gt; Q3{&quot;Analytical scans over big files, Parquet, GeoPackage?&quot;}\n    Q3 -- Yes --&gt; DUCK[&quot;DuckDB spatial&quot;]\n    Q3 -- &quot;Embedded or single file, mobile, offline&quot; --&gt; SQL[&quot;SpatiaLite or GeoPackage&quot;]\n    PG --&gt; Q4{&quot;Need road routing?&quot;}\n    Q4 -- Yes --&gt; PGR[&quot;Add pgRouting&quot;]\n    Q4 -- No --&gt; DONE[&quot;PostGIS only&quot;]</code></pre></details><hr>\n","sha256":"800aba98a43e565795af874ef31dc8d9bcb16067244e1c601cc3d770e5392f2a"},{"key":"4","id":"4","title":"4. Technical Deep Dive","anchor":"4-technical-deep-dive","depth":2,"start":13744,"topics":["foundations"],"end":13771,"markdown":"## 4. Technical Deep Dive\n\n","lineStart":246,"lineEnd":247,"html":"<h2>4. Technical Deep Dive</h2>\n","sha256":"e690dacfd4969c92cda2ae8f2dc0f11d79e74269e95dff3eefa35a955d938867"},{"key":"4.1","id":"4-1","title":"4.1 PostGIS spatial types and storage","anchor":"41-postgis-spatial-types-and-storage","depth":3,"start":13771,"topics":["foundations"],"end":15356,"markdown":"### 4.1 PostGIS spatial types and storage\n\n```sql\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS postgis;\nSELECT postgis_full_version();           -- confirms PostGIS, GEOS, PROJ versions\n\n-- Typmod constrains geometry type AND SRID\nCREATE TABLE poi (\n    poi_id  integer PRIMARY KEY,\n    name    text NOT NULL,\n    geom    geometry(Point, 28992) NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- Geography column (lon/lat, metres)\nCREATE TABLE poi_geog (\n    poi_id integer PRIMARY KEY,\n    geog   geography(Point, 4326) NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- Constructing values\nSELECT ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(120000, 487000), 28992);        -- build a point, tag it with an SRID\nSELECT ST_GeomFromText('POINT(120000 487000)', 28992);          -- from WKT\nSELECT ST_GeomFromEWKT('SRID=28992;POINT(120000 487000)');      -- from EWKT\nSELECT ST_AsText(geom), ST_AsEWKT(geom), ST_SRID(geom) FROM poi;\n```\n\nImportant distinction: **`ST_SetSRID` only relabels** the coordinates (no math). **`ST_Transform` reprojects** them (requires a correct source SRID).\n\n```sql\n-- Wrong: claims lon/lat numbers are RD New metres\nSELECT ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(4.9, 52.37), 28992);\n-- Right: label as 4326, then reproject\nSELECT ST_Transform(ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(4.9, 52.37), 4326), 28992);\n```\n\nUseful catalog objects: `spatial_ref_sys` (CRS definitions), views `geometry_columns` / `geography_columns` (registered spatial columns with type, SRID, dimension).\n\nOther types: `raster` (via the `postgis_raster` extension in PostGIS 3+), `topogeometry` (via `postgis_topology`, §4.8), `box2d`/`box3d` (bboxes), and 3D/measured coordinates (`PointZ`, `PointM`, `PointZM`).\n\n","lineStart":248,"lineEnd":286,"html":"<h2>4.1 PostGIS spatial types and storage</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS postgis;\nSELECT postgis_full_version();           -- confirms PostGIS, GEOS, PROJ versions\n\n-- Typmod constrains geometry type AND SRID\nCREATE TABLE poi (\n    poi_id  integer PRIMARY KEY,\n    name    text NOT NULL,\n    geom    geometry(Point, 28992) NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- Geography column (lon/lat, metres)\nCREATE TABLE poi_geog (\n    poi_id integer PRIMARY KEY,\n    geog   geography(Point, 4326) NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- Constructing values\nSELECT ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(120000, 487000), 28992);        -- build a point, tag it with an SRID\nSELECT ST_GeomFromText(&#39;POINT(120000 487000)&#39;, 28992);          -- from WKT\nSELECT ST_GeomFromEWKT(&#39;SRID=28992;POINT(120000 487000)&#39;);      -- from EWKT\nSELECT ST_AsText(geom), ST_AsEWKT(geom), ST_SRID(geom) FROM poi;</code></pre><p>Important distinction: <strong><code>ST_SetSRID</code> only relabels</strong> the coordinates (no math). <strong><code>ST_Transform</code> reprojects</strong> them (requires a correct source SRID).</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">-- Wrong: claims lon/lat numbers are RD New metres\nSELECT ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(4.9, 52.37), 28992);\n-- Right: label as 4326, then reproject\nSELECT ST_Transform(ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(4.9, 52.37), 4326), 28992);</code></pre><p>Useful catalog objects: <code>spatial_ref_sys</code> (CRS definitions), views <code>geometry_columns</code> / <code>geography_columns</code> (registered spatial columns with type, SRID, dimension).</p>\n<p>Other types: <code>raster</code> (via the <code>postgis_raster</code> extension in PostGIS 3+), <code>topogeometry</code> (via <code>postgis_topology</code>, §4.8), <code>box2d</code>/<code>box3d</code> (bboxes), and 3D/measured coordinates (<code>PointZ</code>, <code>PointM</code>, <code>PointZM</code>).</p>\n","sha256":"0566efb44a0175b76f66d9376d76232656a5ff260de1b3682dda500e30a18e36"},{"key":"4.2","id":"4-2","title":"4.2 GiST and other spatial indexes","anchor":"42-gist-and-other-spatial-indexes","depth":3,"start":15356,"topics":["indexes"],"end":17688,"markdown":"### 4.2 GiST and other spatial indexes\n\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX poi_geom_gix ON poi USING gist (geom);\nANALYZE poi;                                  -- refresh statistics after bulk loads\n```\n\n| Index | Structure | Choose when |\n|---|---|---|\n| **GiST** (default) | R-tree-like tree of bboxes | General purpose: polygons, lines, points, mixed workloads, KNN ordering |\n| **SP-GiST** | Space-partitioning tree (quadtree/k-d) | Mostly points, highly non-overlapping data |\n| **BRIN** | Min/max bbox per block range | Huge, **physically ordered** (e.g. by space or time) append-mostly tables; tiny index, coarse pruning |\n| **GiST n-D** (`gist_geometry_ops_nd`) | N-dimensional tree | 3D/4D queries using `&&&` |\n\nPostGIS **index-assisted** operators:\n\n| Operator | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `a && b` | bboxes overlap (2D) |\n| `a &&& b` | n-D bboxes overlap |\n| `a @ b` / `a ~ b` | bbox of a is contained in / contains bbox of b |\n| `a <-> b` | distance, usable in `ORDER BY` for KNN |\n| `a <#> b` | bbox-to-bbox distance, usable in `ORDER BY` |\n\nIndex-aware functions: `ST_Intersects`, `ST_Contains`, `ST_Within`, `ST_Covers`, `ST_CoveredBy`, `ST_Overlaps`, `ST_Touches`, `ST_Crosses`, `ST_Equals`, `ST_DWithin`, `ST_DFullyWithin` (PostGIS 3+ inlines the `&&` test automatically through planner support functions). **Not** index-aware: `ST_Distance(...) < x`, `ST_Area(...) > x`, `ST_Buffer(...)` used inside a predicate on the indexed column, and expressions that wrap the indexed column (e.g. `ST_Transform(geom, 3857)`) unless you build a matching **expression index**.\n\n```sql\n-- Expression index: lets queries filtering on the geography cast use an index\nCREATE INDEX poi_geom_geog_gix ON poi USING gist ((geom::geography));  -- only valid if geom is in a geodetic SRID\n\n-- Generated column alternative (store a 3857 copy for tiling)\nALTER TABLE poi ADD COLUMN geom_3857 geometry(Point, 3857)\n    GENERATED ALWAYS AS (ST_Transform(geom, 3857)) STORED;\nCREATE INDEX poi_geom3857_gix ON poi USING gist (geom_3857);\n```\n\nMaintenance notes: run `ANALYZE` after bulk loads; `VACUUM` regularly; on PostgreSQL 14+ with PostGIS 3.1+, GiST builds use a faster sorted-build strategy. `CLUSTER poi USING poi_geom_gix;` physically reorders the table by the index (one-time, takes an `ACCESS EXCLUSIVE` lock) to improve locality for window queries.\n\n","lineStart":287,"lineEnd":324,"html":"<h2>4.2 GiST and other spatial indexes</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CREATE INDEX poi_geom_gix ON poi USING gist (geom);\nANALYZE poi;                                  -- refresh statistics after bulk loads</code></pre><div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 4.2 GiST and other spatial indexes\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Index</th>\n<th>Structure</th>\n<th>Choose when</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>GiST</strong> (default)</td>\n<td>R-tree-like tree of bboxes</td>\n<td>General purpose: polygons, lines, points, mixed workloads, KNN ordering</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>SP-GiST</strong></td>\n<td>Space-partitioning tree (quadtree/k-d)</td>\n<td>Mostly points, highly non-overlapping data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>BRIN</strong></td>\n<td>Min/max bbox per block range</td>\n<td>Huge, <strong>physically ordered</strong> (e.g. by space or time) append-mostly tables; tiny index, coarse pruning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>GiST n-D</strong> (<code>gist_geometry_ops_nd</code>)</td>\n<td>N-dimensional tree</td>\n<td>3D/4D queries using <code>&amp;&amp;&amp;</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p>PostGIS <strong>index-assisted</strong> operators:</p>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 4.2 GiST and other spatial indexes\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Operator</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>a &amp;&amp; b</code></td>\n<td>bboxes overlap (2D)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>a &amp;&amp;&amp; b</code></td>\n<td>n-D bboxes overlap</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>a @ b</code> / <code>a ~ b</code></td>\n<td>bbox of a is contained in / contains bbox of b</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>a &lt;-&gt; b</code></td>\n<td>distance, usable in <code>ORDER BY</code> for KNN</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>a &lt;#&gt; b</code></td>\n<td>bbox-to-bbox distance, usable in <code>ORDER BY</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p>Index-aware functions: <code>ST_Intersects</code>, <code>ST_Contains</code>, <code>ST_Within</code>, <code>ST_Covers</code>, <code>ST_CoveredBy</code>, <code>ST_Overlaps</code>, <code>ST_Touches</code>, <code>ST_Crosses</code>, <code>ST_Equals</code>, <code>ST_DWithin</code>, <code>ST_DFullyWithin</code> (PostGIS 3+ inlines the <code>&amp;&amp;</code> test automatically through planner support functions). <strong>Not</strong> index-aware: <code>ST_Distance(...) &lt; x</code>, <code>ST_Area(...) &gt; x</code>, <code>ST_Buffer(...)</code> used inside a predicate on the indexed column, and expressions that wrap the indexed column (e.g. <code>ST_Transform(geom, 3857)</code>) unless you build a matching <strong>expression index</strong>.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">-- Expression index: lets queries filtering on the geography cast use an index\nCREATE INDEX poi_geom_geog_gix ON poi USING gist ((geom::geography));  -- only valid if geom is in a geodetic SRID\n\n-- Generated column alternative (store a 3857 copy for tiling)\nALTER TABLE poi ADD COLUMN geom_3857 geometry(Point, 3857)\n    GENERATED ALWAYS AS (ST_Transform(geom, 3857)) STORED;\nCREATE INDEX poi_geom3857_gix ON poi USING gist (geom_3857);</code></pre><p>Maintenance notes: run <code>ANALYZE</code> after bulk loads; <code>VACUUM</code> regularly; on PostgreSQL 14+ with PostGIS 3.1+, GiST builds use a faster sorted-build strategy. <code>CLUSTER poi USING poi_geom_gix;</code> physically reorders the table by the index (one-time, takes an <code>ACCESS EXCLUSIVE</code> lock) to improve locality for window queries.</p>\n","sha256":"cc7228f6f7c2b25cd93b944dc19cc26084a4ca5c73f70885370cf12c6d55bac3"},{"key":"4.3","id":"4-3","title":"4.3 The `ST_*` function families","anchor":"43-the-st_-function-families","depth":3,"start":17688,"topics":["relationships"],"end":19565,"markdown":"### 4.3 The `ST_*` function families\n\n| Family | Examples | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Constructors | `ST_MakePoint`, `ST_MakeEnvelope`, `ST_GeomFromText`, `ST_GeomFromGeoJSON`, `ST_Collect` | `ST_MakeEnvelope(xmin,ymin,xmax,ymax,srid)` builds a rectangle |\n| Accessors | `ST_SRID`, `ST_GeometryType`, `ST_NPoints`, `ST_X`, `ST_Y`, `ST_Envelope`, `ST_Dump` | `ST_Dump` explodes multi-geometries into rows |\n| Relationships | `ST_Intersects`, `ST_Contains`, `ST_Within`, `ST_Covers`, `ST_Touches`, `ST_Relate`, `ST_DWithin` | Predicates return `boolean`; see §2.4 |\n| Measurement | `ST_Distance`, `ST_Length`, `ST_Area`, `ST_Perimeter`, `ST_Azimuth` | Units follow the CRS (geometry) or metres (geography) |\n| Processing | `ST_Buffer`, `ST_Union`, `ST_Intersection`, `ST_Difference`, `ST_Simplify`, `ST_Subdivide`, `ST_Centroid`, `ST_PointOnSurface`, `ST_ConvexHull` | `ST_Centroid` of a concave polygon may lie *outside* it; use `ST_PointOnSurface` for a guaranteed interior point |\n| Validation/repair | `ST_IsValid`, `ST_IsValidReason`, `ST_MakeValid`, `ST_IsEmpty` | Many functions raise errors or give wrong results on invalid input |\n| Reprojection | `ST_Transform`, `ST_SetSRID` | See §4.1 |\n| Output | `ST_AsText`, `ST_AsGeoJSON`, `ST_AsMVT`, `ST_AsMVTGeom`, `ST_AsBinary` | |\n| Linear referencing | `ST_LineLocatePoint`, `ST_LineInterpolatePoint`, `ST_LineSubstring` | Used to snap points onto network edges (§4.6) |\n\nAnnotated predicate example, using the polygon/point pair from the worked example (§5):\n\n```sql\nSELECT ST_Contains(d.geom, i.geom) AS contains,     -- false when the point is on the boundary\n       ST_Covers(d.geom, i.geom)   AS covers,       -- true\n       ST_Intersects(d.geom, i.geom) AS intersects, -- true\n       ST_Relate(d.geom, i.geom)   AS de9im         -- FF20F1FF2\nFROM district d, incident i\nWHERE d.name = 'West' AND i.incident_id = 2;\n```\n\n","lineStart":325,"lineEnd":349,"html":"<h2>4.3 The <code>ST_*</code> function families</h2>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 4.3 The `ST_*` function families\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Family</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Constructors</td>\n<td><code>ST_MakePoint</code>, <code>ST_MakeEnvelope</code>, <code>ST_GeomFromText</code>, <code>ST_GeomFromGeoJSON</code>, <code>ST_Collect</code></td>\n<td><code>ST_MakeEnvelope(xmin,ymin,xmax,ymax,srid)</code> builds a rectangle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Accessors</td>\n<td><code>ST_SRID</code>, <code>ST_GeometryType</code>, <code>ST_NPoints</code>, <code>ST_X</code>, <code>ST_Y</code>, <code>ST_Envelope</code>, <code>ST_Dump</code></td>\n<td><code>ST_Dump</code> explodes multi-geometries into rows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Relationships</td>\n<td><code>ST_Intersects</code>, <code>ST_Contains</code>, <code>ST_Within</code>, <code>ST_Covers</code>, <code>ST_Touches</code>, <code>ST_Relate</code>, <code>ST_DWithin</code></td>\n<td>Predicates return <code>boolean</code>; see §2.4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Measurement</td>\n<td><code>ST_Distance</code>, <code>ST_Length</code>, <code>ST_Area</code>, <code>ST_Perimeter</code>, <code>ST_Azimuth</code></td>\n<td>Units follow the CRS (geometry) or metres (geography)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Processing</td>\n<td><code>ST_Buffer</code>, <code>ST_Union</code>, <code>ST_Intersection</code>, <code>ST_Difference</code>, <code>ST_Simplify</code>, <code>ST_Subdivide</code>, <code>ST_Centroid</code>, <code>ST_PointOnSurface</code>, <code>ST_ConvexHull</code></td>\n<td><code>ST_Centroid</code> of a concave polygon may lie <em>outside</em> it; use <code>ST_PointOnSurface</code> for a guaranteed interior point</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Validation/repair</td>\n<td><code>ST_IsValid</code>, <code>ST_IsValidReason</code>, <code>ST_MakeValid</code>, <code>ST_IsEmpty</code></td>\n<td>Many functions raise errors or give wrong results on invalid input</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reprojection</td>\n<td><code>ST_Transform</code>, <code>ST_SetSRID</code></td>\n<td>See §4.1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Output</td>\n<td><code>ST_AsText</code>, <code>ST_AsGeoJSON</code>, <code>ST_AsMVT</code>, <code>ST_AsMVTGeom</code>, <code>ST_AsBinary</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Linear referencing</td>\n<td><code>ST_LineLocatePoint</code>, <code>ST_LineInterpolatePoint</code>, <code>ST_LineSubstring</code></td>\n<td>Used to snap points onto network edges (§4.6)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p>Annotated predicate example, using the polygon/point pair from the worked example (§5):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT ST_Contains(d.geom, i.geom) AS contains,     -- false when the point is on the boundary\n       ST_Covers(d.geom, i.geom)   AS covers,       -- true\n       ST_Intersects(d.geom, i.geom) AS intersects, -- true\n       ST_Relate(d.geom, i.geom)   AS de9im         -- FF20F1FF2\nFROM district d, incident i\nWHERE d.name = &#39;West&#39; AND i.incident_id = 2;</code></pre>","sha256":"0a5c078039dfd9c35519a3bd66693066a4f7e1df6749aceeef7336e30cc573e8"},{"key":"4.4","id":"4-4","title":"4.4 Query planning for spatial queries","anchor":"44-query-planning-for-spatial-queries","depth":3,"start":19565,"topics":["indexes"],"end":22464,"markdown":"### 4.4 Query planning for spatial queries\n\n**Execution model.** For `WHERE ST_Intersects(geom, :window)` PostgreSQL's planner rewrites the call to `geom && :window AND <exact test>`. The GiST scan evaluates the `&&` (the *Index Cond*), and the exact test is applied to each candidate (the *Filter* / *Recheck*).\n\n**Reading a plan.** (Illustrative output — your costs and timings will differ; on tiny tables the planner correctly prefers a sequential scan. For learning only, `SET enable_seqscan = off;` forces index use in the current session.)\n\n```text\nEXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)\nSELECT * FROM incident WHERE ST_DWithin(geom, ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(120000,487000),28992), 2000);\n\n Index Scan using incident_geom_gix on incident  (cost=0.14..8.16 rows=1 width=...) (actual time=0.03..0.04 rows=2 loops=1)\n   Index Cond: (geom && '0102...'::geometry)            <-- filter: bbox expanded by 2000\n   Filter: st_dwithin(geom, '0101...'::geometry, 2000)  <-- refine: exact distance\n   Rows Removed by Filter: 0\n   Buffers: shared hit=3\n```\n\nWhat to look for:\n\n| Signal | Meaning | Typical action |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Seq Scan` on a large table with a spatial predicate | Index missing, not usable (wrapped column, `ST_Distance < x`), SRIDs differ, or stale statistics | Add/rewrite index; run `ANALYZE` |\n| Large `Rows Removed by Filter` | Bboxes are poor approximations (long diagonal lines, large polygons) | `ST_Subdivide`, split features, simplify |\n| Huge `Buffers: read` for few rows | Poor physical locality or toasted large geometries | `CLUSTER`, subdivide, tune memory |\n| Nested loop with inner `Index Scan` | Standard spatial-join plan (outer = smaller table) | Ensure the inner table has a GiST index |\n| Estimates far from actual rows | Stale or insufficient statistics | `ANALYZE`; raise `default_statistics_target` for the column |\n\n**Statistics.** `ANALYZE` builds a 2D (and n-D) histogram of feature bboxes. The planner uses it to estimate how many rows a window will touch and therefore whether an index scan beats a sequential scan.\n\n**Large geometries.** A single country polygon with millions of vertices makes every refine test expensive and is **TOASTed** (stored out-of-line and de-toasted on every access). `ST_Subdivide` splits geometries into pieces with at most N vertices (minimum 5), so each piece gets a tight bbox:\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE region_sub AS\nSELECT region_id, ST_Subdivide(geom, 255) AS geom\nFROM region;\nCREATE INDEX region_sub_gix ON region_sub USING gist (geom);\n```\n\nQuery against the pieces and de-duplicate by `region_id`: `SELECT DISTINCT region_id FROM region_sub WHERE ST_Intersects(geom, :pt);`.\n\n**Other tuning levers:** `random_page_cost` (≈1.1 on SSDs), `work_mem` for sorts/hash joins, `max_parallel_workers_per_gather` (PostGIS functions are marked `PARALLEL SAFE`), declarative partitioning for very large tables (one GiST index per partition).\n\n","lineStart":350,"lineEnd":391,"html":"<h2>4.4 Query planning for spatial queries</h2>\n<p><strong>Execution model.</strong> For <code>WHERE ST_Intersects(geom, :window)</code> PostgreSQL&#39;s planner rewrites the call to <code>geom &amp;&amp; :window AND &lt;exact test&gt;</code>. The GiST scan evaluates the <code>&amp;&amp;</code> (the <em>Index Cond</em>), and the exact test is applied to each candidate (the <em>Filter</em> / <em>Recheck</em>).</p>\n<p><strong>Reading a plan.</strong> (Illustrative output — your costs and timings will differ; on tiny tables the planner correctly prefers a sequential scan. For learning only, <code>SET enable_seqscan = off;</code> forces index use in the current session.)</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)\nSELECT * FROM incident WHERE ST_DWithin(geom, ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(120000,487000),28992), 2000);\n\n Index Scan using incident_geom_gix on incident  (cost=0.14..8.16 rows=1 width=...) (actual time=0.03..0.04 rows=2 loops=1)\n   Index Cond: (geom &amp;&amp; &#39;0102...&#39;::geometry)            &lt;-- filter: bbox expanded by 2000\n   Filter: st_dwithin(geom, &#39;0101...&#39;::geometry, 2000)  &lt;-- refine: exact distance\n   Rows Removed by Filter: 0\n   Buffers: shared hit=3</code></pre><p>What to look for:</p>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 4.4 Query planning for spatial queries\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Signal</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Typical action</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Seq Scan</code> on a large table with a spatial predicate</td>\n<td>Index missing, not usable (wrapped column, <code>ST_Distance &lt; x</code>), SRIDs differ, or stale statistics</td>\n<td>Add/rewrite index; run <code>ANALYZE</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Large <code>Rows Removed by Filter</code></td>\n<td>Bboxes are poor approximations (long diagonal lines, large polygons)</td>\n<td><code>ST_Subdivide</code>, split features, simplify</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Huge <code>Buffers: read</code> for few rows</td>\n<td>Poor physical locality or toasted large geometries</td>\n<td><code>CLUSTER</code>, subdivide, tune memory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nested loop with inner <code>Index Scan</code></td>\n<td>Standard spatial-join plan (outer = smaller table)</td>\n<td>Ensure the inner table has a GiST index</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Estimates far from actual rows</td>\n<td>Stale or insufficient statistics</td>\n<td><code>ANALYZE</code>; raise <code>default_statistics_target</code> for the column</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p><strong>Statistics.</strong> <code>ANALYZE</code> builds a 2D (and n-D) histogram of feature bboxes. The planner uses it to estimate how many rows a window will touch and therefore whether an index scan beats a sequential scan.</p>\n<p><strong>Large geometries.</strong> A single country polygon with millions of vertices makes every refine test expensive and is <strong>TOASTed</strong> (stored out-of-line and de-toasted on every access). <code>ST_Subdivide</code> splits geometries into pieces with at most N vertices (minimum 5), so each piece gets a tight bbox:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CREATE TABLE region_sub AS\nSELECT region_id, ST_Subdivide(geom, 255) AS geom\nFROM region;\nCREATE INDEX region_sub_gix ON region_sub USING gist (geom);</code></pre><p>Query against the pieces and de-duplicate by <code>region_id</code>: <code>SELECT DISTINCT region_id FROM region_sub WHERE ST_Intersects(geom, :pt);</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Other tuning levers:</strong> <code>random_page_cost</code> (≈1.1 on SSDs), <code>work_mem</code> for sorts/hash joins, <code>max_parallel_workers_per_gather</code> (PostGIS functions are marked <code>PARALLEL SAFE</code>), declarative partitioning for very large tables (one GiST index per partition).</p>\n","sha256":"b977c48515bb180b0e346f7fe1cb69b8e07c438030eb3bdcca3c1147c0f7503a"},{"key":"4.5","id":"4-5","title":"4.5 Spatial SQL patterns","anchor":"45-spatial-sql-patterns","depth":3,"start":22464,"topics":["queries"],"end":22494,"markdown":"### 4.5 Spatial SQL patterns\n\n","lineStart":392,"lineEnd":393,"html":"<h2>4.5 Spatial SQL patterns</h2>\n","sha256":"7b37e6d9ae32a54061d9ee50154cced7ee8d53180faf30c30abb2d636bad13c9"},{"key":"4.5.1","id":"4-5-1","title":"4.5.1 Nearest neighbour (KNN)","anchor":"451-nearest-neighbour-knn","depth":4,"start":22494,"topics":["queries"],"end":24272,"markdown":"#### 4.5.1 Nearest neighbour (KNN)\n\nThe `<->` operator in `ORDER BY ... LIMIT k` lets the GiST index return rows in increasing distance order without scanning the whole table.\n\n```sql\n-- The 3 stations nearest to a location (single query point)\nSELECT station_id, name,\n       ST_Distance(geom, ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(121000, 488000), 28992)) AS dist_m\nFROM fire_station\nORDER BY geom <-> ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(121000, 488000), 28992)\nLIMIT 3;\n\n-- Nearest station for EVERY incident: LATERAL gives one KNN probe per outer row\nSELECT i.incident_id, s.name, ST_Distance(i.geom, s.geom) AS dist_m\nFROM incident i\nCROSS JOIN LATERAL (\n    SELECT name, geom\n    FROM fire_station\n    ORDER BY geom <-> i.geom\n    LIMIT 1\n) s;\n```\n\nBehaviour and caveats:\n\n* For `geometry`, `<->` returns the true 2D planar distance between geometries (PostgreSQL 9.5+ / PostGIS 2.2+); for `geography` it is a spherical distance in metres (very slightly different from the spheroidal `ST_Distance`). `<#>` is bbox-to-bbox distance only.\n* The index ordering is only used when one side of `<->` is a **constant or an outer-row value** and the expression appears in `ORDER BY`.\n* A selective `WHERE` on another column is applied *after* index ordering and may force the index to walk many rows. For \"nearest 5 *open* stations\", consider a partial index (`WHERE status = 'open'`) or bound the search with `ST_DWithin` first.\n* Compute KNN on `geometry` in a **projected CRS** if you need metric correctness; `geometry` in EPSG:4326 ranks neighbours by *degrees*, which skews east–west vs north–south at high latitude.\n* Ties: equal distances have undefined relative order. Add a deterministic tiebreaker (`ORDER BY geom <-> p, station_id`) only if you can accept losing pure index ordering for ties.\n\n","lineStart":394,"lineEnd":424,"html":"<h2>4.5.1 Nearest neighbour (KNN)</h2>\n<p>The <code>&lt;-&gt;</code> operator in <code>ORDER BY ... LIMIT k</code> lets the GiST index return rows in increasing distance order without scanning the whole table.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">-- The 3 stations nearest to a location (single query point)\nSELECT station_id, name,\n       ST_Distance(geom, ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(121000, 488000), 28992)) AS dist_m\nFROM fire_station\nORDER BY geom &lt;-&gt; ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(121000, 488000), 28992)\nLIMIT 3;\n\n-- Nearest station for EVERY incident: LATERAL gives one KNN probe per outer row\nSELECT i.incident_id, s.name, ST_Distance(i.geom, s.geom) AS dist_m\nFROM incident i\nCROSS JOIN LATERAL (\n    SELECT name, geom\n    FROM fire_station\n    ORDER BY geom &lt;-&gt; i.geom\n    LIMIT 1\n) s;</code></pre><p>Behaviour and caveats:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>For <code>geometry</code>, <code>&lt;-&gt;</code> returns the true 2D planar distance between geometries (PostgreSQL 9.5+ / PostGIS 2.2+); for <code>geography</code> it is a spherical distance in metres (very slightly different from the spheroidal <code>ST_Distance</code>). <code>&lt;#&gt;</code> is bbox-to-bbox distance only.</li>\n<li>The index ordering is only used when one side of <code>&lt;-&gt;</code> is a <strong>constant or an outer-row value</strong> and the expression appears in <code>ORDER BY</code>.</li>\n<li>A selective <code>WHERE</code> on another column is applied <em>after</em> index ordering and may force the index to walk many rows. For &quot;nearest 5 <em>open</em> stations&quot;, consider a partial index (<code>WHERE status = &#39;open&#39;</code>) or bound the search with <code>ST_DWithin</code> first.</li>\n<li>Compute KNN on <code>geometry</code> in a <strong>projected CRS</strong> if you need metric correctness; <code>geometry</code> in EPSG:4326 ranks neighbours by <em>degrees</em>, which skews east–west vs north–south at high latitude.</li>\n<li>Ties: equal distances have undefined relative order. Add a deterministic tiebreaker (<code>ORDER BY geom &lt;-&gt; p, station_id</code>) only if you can accept losing pure index ordering for ties.</li>\n</ul>\n","sha256":"06873478172603201cebbd8158f4d2378754f62e2e30a0e4c640b76f66265155"},{"key":"4.5.2","id":"4-5-2","title":"4.5.2 `ST_DWithin` vs `ST_Distance`","anchor":"452-st_dwithin-vs-st_distance","depth":4,"start":24272,"topics":["queries"],"end":24916,"markdown":"#### 4.5.2 `ST_DWithin` vs `ST_Distance`\n\n```sql\n-- BAD: computes distance for every row; cannot use the index\nSELECT * FROM incident\nWHERE ST_Distance(geom, :p) < 2000;\n\n-- GOOD: bbox expanded by 2000 uses the index, then exact distance recheck\nSELECT * FROM incident\nWHERE ST_DWithin(geom, :p, 2000);\n```\n\n* `ST_DWithin(a, b, d)` is true when `distance(a,b) <= d` (**inclusive**).\n* Units: the CRS units for `geometry`; **metres** for `geography`. With `geometry` in EPSG:4326, `ST_DWithin(a, b, 1000)` means 1000 *degrees* — a classic bug.\n* Use `ST_Distance` for *reporting* a distance, `ST_DWithin` for *filtering*, `<->` for *ordering*.\n\n","lineStart":425,"lineEnd":440,"html":"<h2>4.5.2 <code>ST_DWithin</code> vs <code>ST_Distance</code></h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">-- BAD: computes distance for every row; cannot use the index\nSELECT * FROM incident\nWHERE ST_Distance(geom, :p) &lt; 2000;\n\n-- GOOD: bbox expanded by 2000 uses the index, then exact distance recheck\nSELECT * FROM incident\nWHERE ST_DWithin(geom, :p, 2000);</code></pre><ul>\n<li><code>ST_DWithin(a, b, d)</code> is true when <code>distance(a,b) &lt;= d</code> (<strong>inclusive</strong>).</li>\n<li>Units: the CRS units for <code>geometry</code>; <strong>metres</strong> for <code>geography</code>. With <code>geometry</code> in EPSG:4326, <code>ST_DWithin(a, b, 1000)</code> means 1000 <em>degrees</em> — a classic bug.</li>\n<li>Use <code>ST_Distance</code> for <em>reporting</em> a distance, <code>ST_DWithin</code> for <em>filtering</em>, <code>&lt;-&gt;</code> for <em>ordering</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n","sha256":"67ed6c63d36a34100c30d910d8e4d7422428b2b2bb1adb12cfb19b5ebf470f9d"},{"key":"4.5.3","id":"4-5-3","title":"4.5.3 Clustering","anchor":"453-clustering","depth":4,"start":24916,"topics":["queries"],"end":26129,"markdown":"#### 4.5.3 Clustering\n\n| Need | Tool | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Density-based clusters, noise detection | `ST_ClusterDBSCAN(geom, eps, minpoints) OVER ()` | `eps` in CRS units; returns `NULL` for noise points |\n| Fixed number of clusters | `ST_ClusterKMeans(geom, k) OVER ()` | Centroid-based; sensitive to initial conditions and outliers |\n| Connected groups | `ST_ClusterIntersecting(geom)`, `ST_ClusterWithin(geom, d)` (aggregates) | Return arrays of geometries |\n| Aggregation to a grid for maps | `ST_SquareGrid`, `ST_HexagonGrid` (PostGIS 3.1+), `ST_SnapToGrid` | Fast \"heat-map\" style counts |\n| Physical table ordering | `CLUSTER table USING gist_index` | Different meaning: storage layout, not analysis |\n\n```sql\nSELECT incident_id,\n       ST_ClusterDBSCAN(geom, eps := 2000, minpoints := 2) OVER () AS cluster_id\nFROM incident;\n\n-- Hexagon-binned counts (cell size = 1000 CRS units)\nSELECT h.i, h.j, h.geom, count(*) AS n\nFROM ST_HexagonGrid(1000, (SELECT ST_Extent(geom) FROM incident)) AS h\nJOIN incident i ON ST_Intersects(h.geom, i.geom)\nGROUP BY h.i, h.j, h.geom;\n```\n\n(Note: `ST_Extent` returns a `box2d`; cast with `::geometry` if your version of the grid function requires a geometry argument.)\n\n","lineStart":441,"lineEnd":464,"html":"<h2>4.5.3 Clustering</h2>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 4.5.3 Clustering\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>Tool</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Density-based clusters, noise detection</td>\n<td><code>ST_ClusterDBSCAN(geom, eps, minpoints) OVER ()</code></td>\n<td><code>eps</code> in CRS units; returns <code>NULL</code> for noise points</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fixed number of clusters</td>\n<td><code>ST_ClusterKMeans(geom, k) OVER ()</code></td>\n<td>Centroid-based; sensitive to initial conditions and outliers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Connected groups</td>\n<td><code>ST_ClusterIntersecting(geom)</code>, <code>ST_ClusterWithin(geom, d)</code> (aggregates)</td>\n<td>Return arrays of geometries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Aggregation to a grid for maps</td>\n<td><code>ST_SquareGrid</code>, <code>ST_HexagonGrid</code> (PostGIS 3.1+), <code>ST_SnapToGrid</code></td>\n<td>Fast &quot;heat-map&quot; style counts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Physical table ordering</td>\n<td><code>CLUSTER table USING gist_index</code></td>\n<td>Different meaning: storage layout, not analysis</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT incident_id,\n       ST_ClusterDBSCAN(geom, eps := 2000, minpoints := 2) OVER () AS cluster_id\nFROM incident;\n\n-- Hexagon-binned counts (cell size = 1000 CRS units)\nSELECT h.i, h.j, h.geom, count(*) AS n\nFROM ST_HexagonGrid(1000, (SELECT ST_Extent(geom) FROM incident)) AS h\nJOIN incident i ON ST_Intersects(h.geom, i.geom)\nGROUP BY h.i, h.j, h.geom;</code></pre><p>(Note: <code>ST_Extent</code> returns a <code>box2d</code>; cast with <code>::geometry</code> if your version of the grid function requires a geometry argument.)</p>\n","sha256":"e1510d54e9be37e315fc0bef6eb79143172761da31ed71e17f1481c0780e8865"},{"key":"4.5.4","id":"4-5-4","title":"4.5.4 Tiling queries (vector tiles)","anchor":"454-tiling-queries-vector-tiles","depth":4,"start":26129,"topics":["tiles"],"end":28115,"markdown":"#### 4.5.4 Tiling queries (vector tiles)\n\nWeb map tiles use the XYZ scheme in EPSG:3857: at zoom `z` the world is divided into `2^z × 2^z` tiles, `y = 0` is the northern row, and the Web Mercator extent is ±20 037 508.342789244 m on each axis. Tile edge length is `40 075 016.68557849 / 2^z` metres (in projected units).\n\nLon/lat to tile indices (verify with a calculator or in code):\n\n```\nn = 2^z\nx = floor( (lon + 180) / 360 · n )\ny = floor( (1 − asinh(tan(lat)) / π) / 2 · n )        (lat in radians)\n```\n\nPostGIS functions:\n\n| Function | Role |\n|---|---|\n| `ST_TileEnvelope(z, x, y)` | Tile bounds as an EPSG:3857 polygon (PostGIS 3.0+) |\n| `ST_AsMVTGeom(geom, bounds, extent, buffer, clip)` | Transforms geometry to tile-local integer coordinates (default extent 4096) and clips |\n| `ST_AsMVT(row, layer_name, extent, geom_column)` | Aggregates rows into one protobuf tile (`bytea`) |\n\nExpected values you can check: `ST_TileEnvelope(0,0,0)` spans `(-20037508.34, -20037508.34)` to `(20037508.34, 20037508.34)`; `ST_TileEnvelope(1,1,0)` (east-north quadrant) spans `(0, 0)` to `(20037508.34, 20037508.34)`.\n\n```sql\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION incident_mvt(z integer, x integer, y integer)\nRETURNS bytea\nLANGUAGE sql STABLE PARALLEL SAFE AS $$\n    WITH bounds AS (\n        SELECT ST_TileEnvelope(z, x, y) AS b3857\n    ),\n    mvtgeom AS (\n        SELECT i.incident_id,\n               ST_AsMVTGeom(ST_Transform(i.geom, 3857), b.b3857, 4096, 64, true) AS geom\n        FROM incident i, bounds b\n        -- Transform the TILE (cheap) into the data CRS so the GiST index on i.geom is used\n        WHERE i.geom && ST_Transform(b.b3857, 28992)\n    )\n    SELECT ST_AsMVT(mvtgeom.*, 'incidents', 4096, 'geom') FROM mvtgeom;\n$$;\n```\n\nTile servers such as `pg_tileserv` and Martin expose functions like this over HTTP. Strategy for production: precompute simplified/aggregated layers for low zooms, apply `ST_Simplify`/`ST_RemoveRepeatedPoints` or attribute filters, and cap features per tile.\n\n","lineStart":465,"lineEnd":506,"html":"<h2>4.5.4 Tiling queries (vector tiles)</h2>\n<p>Web map tiles use the XYZ scheme in EPSG:3857: at zoom <code>z</code> the world is divided into <code>2^z × 2^z</code> tiles, <code>y = 0</code> is the northern row, and the Web Mercator extent is ±20 037 508.342789244 m on each axis. Tile edge length is <code>40 075 016.68557849 / 2^z</code> metres (in projected units).</p>\n<p>Lon/lat to tile indices (verify with a calculator or in code):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">n = 2^z\nx = floor( (lon + 180) / 360 · n )\ny = floor( (1 − asinh(tan(lat)) / π) / 2 · n )        (lat in radians)</code></pre><p>PostGIS functions:</p>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 4.5.4 Tiling queries (vector tiles)\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Function</th>\n<th>Role</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>ST_TileEnvelope(z, x, y)</code></td>\n<td>Tile bounds as an EPSG:3857 polygon (PostGIS 3.0+)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ST_AsMVTGeom(geom, bounds, extent, buffer, clip)</code></td>\n<td>Transforms geometry to tile-local integer coordinates (default extent 4096) and clips</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ST_AsMVT(row, layer_name, extent, geom_column)</code></td>\n<td>Aggregates rows into one protobuf tile (<code>bytea</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p>Expected values you can check: <code>ST_TileEnvelope(0,0,0)</code> spans <code>(-20037508.34, -20037508.34)</code> to <code>(20037508.34, 20037508.34)</code>; <code>ST_TileEnvelope(1,1,0)</code> (east-north quadrant) spans <code>(0, 0)</code> to <code>(20037508.34, 20037508.34)</code>.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION incident_mvt(z integer, x integer, y integer)\nRETURNS bytea\nLANGUAGE sql STABLE PARALLEL SAFE AS $$\n    WITH bounds AS (\n        SELECT ST_TileEnvelope(z, x, y) AS b3857\n    ),\n    mvtgeom AS (\n        SELECT i.incident_id,\n               ST_AsMVTGeom(ST_Transform(i.geom, 3857), b.b3857, 4096, 64, true) AS geom\n        FROM incident i, bounds b\n        -- Transform the TILE (cheap) into the data CRS so the GiST index on i.geom is used\n        WHERE i.geom &amp;&amp; ST_Transform(b.b3857, 28992)\n    )\n    SELECT ST_AsMVT(mvtgeom.*, &#39;incidents&#39;, 4096, &#39;geom&#39;) FROM mvtgeom;\n$$;</code></pre><p>Tile servers such as <code>pg_tileserv</code> and Martin expose functions like this over HTTP. Strategy for production: precompute simplified/aggregated layers for low zooms, apply <code>ST_Simplify</code>/<code>ST_RemoveRepeatedPoints</code> or attribute filters, and cap features per tile.</p>\n","sha256":"8c8b7e7d7fc2e56d91e05dc8e485344b00f336d6c55e8fd4afc06699e58a0ea0"},{"key":"4.6","id":"4-6","title":"4.6 pgRouting for network analysis","anchor":"46-pgrouting-for-network-analysis","depth":3,"start":28115,"topics":["routing"],"end":32772,"markdown":"### 4.6 pgRouting for network analysis\n\n**pgRouting** extends PostGIS with graph algorithms. A routable network is a table of **edges** (road segments) between **vertices** (nodes), each edge having a `cost` and optionally a `reverse_cost`.\n\n| Concept | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Edge query | SQL string returning `id, source, target, cost, reverse_cost` (plus `x1,y1,x2,y2` for A\\*) |\n| `directed` | If true, `cost` applies source→target and `reverse_cost` target→source; a negative value (by convention −1) means the edge cannot be traversed in that direction. If false, the graph is undirected |\n| Cost | Any additive non-negative quantity: length in metres, travel time in seconds, a weighted blend |\n\nCommon functions (verify exact signatures and output columns in your version; they differ between 3.x and 4.x):\n\n| Function | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `pgr_dijkstra` | Shortest path(s), one-to-one / one-to-many / many-to-many |\n| `pgr_dijkstraCost` | Only the aggregate costs, no path geometry |\n| `pgr_aStar` | Heuristic search; needs coordinates; faster on large networks |\n| `pgr_KSP` | K shortest alternative paths |\n| `pgr_drivingDistance` | **Service area / isochrone** nodes reachable within a cost budget |\n| `pgr_connectedComponents` | Detect disconnected sub-networks (data-quality check) |\n| `pgr_contraction` | Contraction hierarchies preprocessing for large graphs |\n\nDijkstra's algorithm maintains the best known cost `d(v)` to each vertex and repeatedly settles the unsettled vertex with the smallest `d`, relaxing `d(w) = min(d(w), d(v) + cost(v,w))`. Complexity with a binary heap is `O((V + E) log V)`.\n\n**Toy graph (abstract costs, not a map).** Edge costs are in arbitrary units and every edge is two-way (`cost = reverse_cost`). Adjacency list (`neighbour(cost)`), followed by the authoritative edge table:\n\n```text\n1: 2(4)  3(2)\n2: 1(4)  3(1)  4(5)\n3: 1(2)  2(1)  4(8)  5(10)\n4: 2(5)  3(8)  5(2)  6(6)\n5: 3(10) 4(2)  6(3)\n6: 4(6)  5(3)\n```\n\n| edge id | source | target | cost | reverse_cost |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 1 | 2 | 4 | 4 |\n| 2 | 1 | 3 | 2 | 2 |\n| 3 | 2 | 3 | 1 | 1 |\n| 4 | 2 | 4 | 5 | 5 |\n| 5 | 3 | 4 | 8 | 8 |\n| 6 | 3 | 5 | 10 | 10 |\n| 7 | 4 | 5 | 2 | 2 |\n| 8 | 4 | 6 | 6 | 6 |\n| 9 | 5 | 6 | 3 | 3 |\n\n```sql\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS pgrouting CASCADE;     -- pulls in postgis if absent\n\nCREATE TABLE toy_edge (\n    id int PRIMARY KEY, source int, target int,\n    cost float8, reverse_cost float8\n);\nINSERT INTO toy_edge VALUES\n (1,1,2,4,4),(2,1,3,2,2),(3,2,3,1,1),(4,2,4,5,5),(5,3,4,8,8),\n (6,3,5,10,10),(7,4,5,2,2),(8,4,6,6,6),(9,5,6,3,3);\n\n-- Shortest path from vertex 1 to vertex 6\nSELECT * FROM pgr_dijkstra(\n  'SELECT id, source, target, cost, reverse_cost FROM toy_edge', 1, 6, true);\n```\n\n**Expected result (verify by hand).** The cheapest route is `1 → 3 → 2 → 4 → 5 → 6` using edges 2, 3, 4, 7, 9 with total cost **13**. Note that the greedy first hop `1 → 3` (cost 2) leads to `2` at cost 3, which beats the direct edge `1 → 2` (cost 4).\n\n| path_seq | node | edge | cost | agg_cost |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 1 | 2 | 2 | 0 |\n| 2 | 3 | 3 | 1 | 2 |\n| 3 | 2 | 4 | 5 | 3 |\n| 4 | 4 | 7 | 2 | 8 |\n| 5 | 5 | 9 | 3 | 10 |\n| 6 | 6 | −1 | 0 | 13 |\n\n(Your version may add columns such as `seq`, `start_vid`, `end_vid`.)\n\nMany-to-many costs and a service area:\n\n```sql\nSELECT start_vid, end_vid, agg_cost\nFROM pgr_dijkstraCost(\n  'SELECT id, source, target, cost, reverse_cost FROM toy_edge',\n  ARRAY[1,6], ARRAY[3,4,5], true)\nORDER BY start_vid, end_vid;\n-- (1,3,2) (1,4,8) (1,5,10) (6,3,11) (6,4,5) (6,5,3)\n\nSELECT node, agg_cost\nFROM pgr_drivingDistance(\n  'SELECT id, source, target, cost, reverse_cost FROM toy_edge', 1, 8, true)\nORDER BY agg_cost, node;\n-- nodes 1 (0), 3 (2), 2 (3), 4 (8)    -- node 5 costs 10, outside the budget of 8\n```\n\n**From roads to a graph (real data).** Typical pipeline: import road lines (OSM via `osm2pgrouting`, or your authority data via `ogr2ogr`/`COPY`) → **node** the lines at intersections (e.g. `ST_Node` over a collected set of lines, or the tool's own noding) → assign integer `source`/`target` vertex ids (modern pgRouting favours building a vertices table with `pgr_extractVertices`; the older `pgr_createTopology` is deprecated and removed in recent major versions) → compute `cost = ST_Length(geom)` in metres (projected CRS or `geom::geography`) or travel time → encode one-way streets with `reverse_cost = -1` → run `pgr_connectedComponents` to find isolated pieces. To route between arbitrary coordinates, **snap** each point to its nearest edge/vertex using the KNN pattern (`ORDER BY geom <-> point LIMIT 1`) and, for mid-edge snapping, `ST_LineLocatePoint`.\n\n","lineStart":507,"lineEnd":601,"html":"<h2>4.6 pgRouting for network analysis</h2>\n<p><strong>pgRouting</strong> extends PostGIS with graph algorithms. A routable network is a table of <strong>edges</strong> (road segments) between <strong>vertices</strong> (nodes), each edge having a <code>cost</code> and optionally a <code>reverse_cost</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 4.6 pgRouting for network analysis\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Edge query</td>\n<td>SQL string returning <code>id, source, target, cost, reverse_cost</code> (plus <code>x1,y1,x2,y2</code> for A*)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>directed</code></td>\n<td>If true, <code>cost</code> applies source→target and <code>reverse_cost</code> target→source; a negative value (by convention −1) means the edge cannot be traversed in that direction. If false, the graph is undirected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cost</td>\n<td>Any additive non-negative quantity: length in metres, travel time in seconds, a weighted blend</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p>Common functions (verify exact signatures and output columns in your version; they differ between 3.x and 4.x):</p>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 4.6 pgRouting for network analysis\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Function</th>\n<th>Purpose</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>pgr_dijkstra</code></td>\n<td>Shortest path(s), one-to-one / one-to-many / many-to-many</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>pgr_dijkstraCost</code></td>\n<td>Only the aggregate costs, no path geometry</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>pgr_aStar</code></td>\n<td>Heuristic search; needs coordinates; faster on large networks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>pgr_KSP</code></td>\n<td>K shortest alternative paths</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>pgr_drivingDistance</code></td>\n<td><strong>Service area / isochrone</strong> nodes reachable within a cost budget</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>pgr_connectedComponents</code></td>\n<td>Detect disconnected sub-networks (data-quality check)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>pgr_contraction</code></td>\n<td>Contraction hierarchies preprocessing for large graphs</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p>Dijkstra&#39;s algorithm maintains the best known cost <code>d(v)</code> to each vertex and repeatedly settles the unsettled vertex with the smallest <code>d</code>, relaxing <code>d(w) = min(d(w), d(v) + cost(v,w))</code>. Complexity with a binary heap is <code>O((V + E) log V)</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Toy graph (abstract costs, not a map).</strong> Edge costs are in arbitrary units and every edge is two-way (<code>cost = reverse_cost</code>). Adjacency list (<code>neighbour(cost)</code>), followed by the authoritative edge table:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">1: 2(4)  3(2)\n2: 1(4)  3(1)  4(5)\n3: 1(2)  2(1)  4(8)  5(10)\n4: 2(5)  3(8)  5(2)  6(6)\n5: 3(10) 4(2)  6(3)\n6: 4(6)  5(3)</code></pre><div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 4.6 pgRouting for network analysis\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>edge id</th>\n<th>source</th>\n<th>target</th>\n<th>cost</th>\n<th>reverse_cost</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>10</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS pgrouting CASCADE;     -- pulls in postgis if absent\n\nCREATE TABLE toy_edge (\n    id int PRIMARY KEY, source int, target int,\n    cost float8, reverse_cost float8\n);\nINSERT INTO toy_edge VALUES\n (1,1,2,4,4),(2,1,3,2,2),(3,2,3,1,1),(4,2,4,5,5),(5,3,4,8,8),\n (6,3,5,10,10),(7,4,5,2,2),(8,4,6,6,6),(9,5,6,3,3);\n\n-- Shortest path from vertex 1 to vertex 6\nSELECT * FROM pgr_dijkstra(\n  &#39;SELECT id, source, target, cost, reverse_cost FROM toy_edge&#39;, 1, 6, true);</code></pre><p><strong>Expected result (verify by hand).</strong> The cheapest route is <code>1 → 3 → 2 → 4 → 5 → 6</code> using edges 2, 3, 4, 7, 9 with total cost <strong>13</strong>. Note that the greedy first hop <code>1 → 3</code> (cost 2) leads to <code>2</code> at cost 3, which beats the direct edge <code>1 → 2</code> (cost 4).</p>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 4.6 pgRouting for network analysis\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>path_seq</th>\n<th>node</th>\n<th>edge</th>\n<th>cost</th>\n<th>agg_cost</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>10</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>−1</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>13</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p>(Your version may add columns such as <code>seq</code>, <code>start_vid</code>, <code>end_vid</code>.)</p>\n<p>Many-to-many costs and a service area:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT start_vid, end_vid, agg_cost\nFROM pgr_dijkstraCost(\n  &#39;SELECT id, source, target, cost, reverse_cost FROM toy_edge&#39;,\n  ARRAY[1,6], ARRAY[3,4,5], true)\nORDER BY start_vid, end_vid;\n-- (1,3,2) (1,4,8) (1,5,10) (6,3,11) (6,4,5) (6,5,3)\n\nSELECT node, agg_cost\nFROM pgr_drivingDistance(\n  &#39;SELECT id, source, target, cost, reverse_cost FROM toy_edge&#39;, 1, 8, true)\nORDER BY agg_cost, node;\n-- nodes 1 (0), 3 (2), 2 (3), 4 (8)    -- node 5 costs 10, outside the budget of 8</code></pre><p><strong>From roads to a graph (real data).</strong> Typical pipeline: import road lines (OSM via <code>osm2pgrouting</code>, or your authority data via <code>ogr2ogr</code>/<code>COPY</code>) → <strong>node</strong> the lines at intersections (e.g. <code>ST_Node</code> over a collected set of lines, or the tool&#39;s own noding) → assign integer <code>source</code>/<code>target</code> vertex ids (modern pgRouting favours building a vertices table with <code>pgr_extractVertices</code>; the older <code>pgr_createTopology</code> is deprecated and removed in recent major versions) → compute <code>cost = ST_Length(geom)</code> in metres (projected CRS or <code>geom::geography</code>) or travel time → encode one-way streets with <code>reverse_cost = -1</code> → run <code>pgr_connectedComponents</code> to find isolated pieces. To route between arbitrary coordinates, <strong>snap</strong> each point to its nearest edge/vertex using the KNN pattern (<code>ORDER BY geom &lt;-&gt; point LIMIT 1</code>) and, for mid-edge snapping, <code>ST_LineLocatePoint</code>.</p>\n","sha256":"befb49e95efb6ea0d8f8f48c085fb58cc23dec5ca31b1f0e3d245f0c4a7cb05e"},{"key":"4.7","id":"4-7","title":"4.7 Alternatives to PostGIS","anchor":"47-alternatives-to-postgis","depth":3,"start":32772,"topics":["engines"],"end":35647,"markdown":"### 4.7 Alternatives to PostGIS\n\n| Engine | Spatial types & index | Strengths | Limitations / cautions |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **SpatiaLite** (SQLite extension; related: **GeoPackage**, an OGC SQLite container) | Geometry blobs; R\\*Tree virtual-table index created with `CreateSpatialIndex` (GeoPackage uses `rtree_<table>_<geom>` tables) | Single file, zero admin, embedded/mobile/offline, GDAL-friendly | Single-writer concurrency model; smaller function set; no geography type; index must be queried explicitly in SpatiaLite |\n| **DuckDB + `spatial`** | `GEOMETRY` type (planar), `ST_*` functions, R-tree indexes (check your version's supported query shapes), `ST_Read` for GDAL formats, GeoParquet interchange | Columnar, vectorised analytics over large files/Parquet; excellent for ETL and one-off analysis | Not a multi-user transactional server; no `geography` type (some sphere/spheroid helper functions exist); fewer planning/index features than PostGIS |\n| **Oracle Spatial** | `SDO_GEOMETRY`; metadata in `USER_SDO_GEOM_METADATA`; `MDSYS.SPATIAL_INDEX_V2` (R-tree); operators `SDO_RELATE`, `SDO_WITHIN_DISTANCE`, `SDO_NN` | Enterprise integration, geodetic and projected support, network and topology data models | Licensing/edition dependent; different SQL idioms; metadata must be registered before indexing |\n| **SQL Server spatial** | `geometry` and `geography`; **grid-tessellation** spatial index (not an R-tree); methods like `STIntersects`, `STDistance`, `STBuffer` | Tight Microsoft stack integration, ellipsoidal `geography` | Fewer analytical functions; index requires a `BOUNDING_BOX` for `geometry`; method-call syntax |\n\nIllustrative equivalents of \"stations within 2000 m of (120000, 487000)\":\n\n```sql\n-- PostGIS\nSELECT station_id FROM fire_station\nWHERE ST_DWithin(geom, ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(120000,487000),28992), 2000);\n\n-- SpatiaLite (index probed explicitly through the SpatialIndex virtual table)\nSELECT s.name FROM station s\nWHERE s.ROWID IN (SELECT ROWID FROM SpatialIndex\n                  WHERE f_table_name = 'station'\n                    AND search_frame = BuildMbr(118000,485000,122000,489000, 28992))\n  AND ST_Distance(s.geom, MakePoint(120000,487000,28992)) <= 2000;\n\n-- DuckDB spatial (INSTALL spatial; LOAD spatial;)\nSELECT id FROM station\nWHERE ST_DWithin(geom, ST_Point(120000, 487000), 2000);\n\n-- Oracle Spatial (index and metadata required; bounds below are illustrative)\nSELECT id FROM station s\nWHERE SDO_WITHIN_DISTANCE(s.geom,\n        SDO_GEOMETRY(2001, 28992, SDO_POINT_TYPE(120000,487000,NULL), NULL, NULL),\n        'distance=2000 unit=METER') = 'TRUE';\n\n-- SQL Server\nSELECT id FROM dbo.station\nWHERE geom.STDistance(geometry::Point(120000, 487000, 28992)) <= 2000;\n```\n\nTip: the *concepts* (bbox index, filter-and-refine, SRID discipline, validity) transfer across engines; only syntax and operational details change.\n\n","lineStart":602,"lineEnd":641,"html":"<h2>4.7 Alternatives to PostGIS</h2>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 4.7 Alternatives to PostGIS\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Engine</th>\n<th>Spatial types &amp; index</th>\n<th>Strengths</th>\n<th>Limitations / cautions</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>SpatiaLite</strong> (SQLite extension; related: <strong>GeoPackage</strong>, an OGC SQLite container)</td>\n<td>Geometry blobs; R*Tree virtual-table index created with <code>CreateSpatialIndex</code> (GeoPackage uses <code>rtree_&lt;table&gt;_&lt;geom&gt;</code> tables)</td>\n<td>Single file, zero admin, embedded/mobile/offline, GDAL-friendly</td>\n<td>Single-writer concurrency model; smaller function set; no geography type; index must be queried explicitly in SpatiaLite</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>DuckDB + <code>spatial</code></strong></td>\n<td><code>GEOMETRY</code> type (planar), <code>ST_*</code> functions, R-tree indexes (check your version&#39;s supported query shapes), <code>ST_Read</code> for GDAL formats, GeoParquet interchange</td>\n<td>Columnar, vectorised analytics over large files/Parquet; excellent for ETL and one-off analysis</td>\n<td>Not a multi-user transactional server; no <code>geography</code> type (some sphere/spheroid helper functions exist); fewer planning/index features than PostGIS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Oracle Spatial</strong></td>\n<td><code>SDO_GEOMETRY</code>; metadata in <code>USER_SDO_GEOM_METADATA</code>; <code>MDSYS.SPATIAL_INDEX_V2</code> (R-tree); operators <code>SDO_RELATE</code>, <code>SDO_WITHIN_DISTANCE</code>, <code>SDO_NN</code></td>\n<td>Enterprise integration, geodetic and projected support, network and topology data models</td>\n<td>Licensing/edition dependent; different SQL idioms; metadata must be registered before indexing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>SQL Server spatial</strong></td>\n<td><code>geometry</code> and <code>geography</code>; <strong>grid-tessellation</strong> spatial index (not an R-tree); methods like <code>STIntersects</code>, <code>STDistance</code>, <code>STBuffer</code></td>\n<td>Tight Microsoft stack integration, ellipsoidal <code>geography</code></td>\n<td>Fewer analytical functions; index requires a <code>BOUNDING_BOX</code> for <code>geometry</code>; method-call syntax</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p>Illustrative equivalents of &quot;stations within 2000 m of (120000, 487000)&quot;:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">-- PostGIS\nSELECT station_id FROM fire_station\nWHERE ST_DWithin(geom, ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(120000,487000),28992), 2000);\n\n-- SpatiaLite (index probed explicitly through the SpatialIndex virtual table)\nSELECT s.name FROM station s\nWHERE s.ROWID IN (SELECT ROWID FROM SpatialIndex\n                  WHERE f_table_name = &#39;station&#39;\n                    AND search_frame = BuildMbr(118000,485000,122000,489000, 28992))\n  AND ST_Distance(s.geom, MakePoint(120000,487000,28992)) &lt;= 2000;\n\n-- DuckDB spatial (INSTALL spatial; LOAD spatial;)\nSELECT id FROM station\nWHERE ST_DWithin(geom, ST_Point(120000, 487000), 2000);\n\n-- Oracle Spatial (index and metadata required; bounds below are illustrative)\nSELECT id FROM station s\nWHERE SDO_WITHIN_DISTANCE(s.geom,\n        SDO_GEOMETRY(2001, 28992, SDO_POINT_TYPE(120000,487000,NULL), NULL, NULL),\n        &#39;distance=2000 unit=METER&#39;) = &#39;TRUE&#39;;\n\n-- SQL Server\nSELECT id FROM dbo.station\nWHERE geom.STDistance(geometry::Point(120000, 487000, 28992)) &lt;= 2000;</code></pre><p>Tip: the <em>concepts</em> (bbox index, filter-and-refine, SRID discipline, validity) transfer across engines; only syntax and operational details change.</p>\n","sha256":"2e1c115df614e6d0b74e91f7f7ccddb95a0d11f172144036c437d09123199213"},{"key":"4.8","id":"4-8","title":"4.8 Data modeling","anchor":"48-data-modeling","depth":3,"start":35647,"topics":["modeling"],"end":35670,"markdown":"### 4.8 Data modeling\n\n","lineStart":642,"lineEnd":643,"html":"<h2>4.8 Data modeling</h2>\n","sha256":"fa2570251541330c4ed4603684a71476a09d391fbcb3807163925e90fd828d5c"},{"key":"4.8.1","id":"4-8-1","title":"4.8.1 Schema design basics","anchor":"481-schema-design-basics","depth":4,"start":35670,"topics":["modeling"],"end":36258,"markdown":"#### 4.8.1 Schema design basics\n\n* **One geometry type and one SRID per column** (typmod enforces it). Store in a single authoritative CRS; derive other CRSs with generated columns, views, or on-the-fly `ST_Transform`.\n* **Separate layers by geometry type** instead of mixing points and polygons in one column.\n* Use **surrogate integer keys** plus a stable **business key** (e.g. `station_code`).\n* Partition very large tables (by region, tile, or time) and index each partition.\n* Keep derived data (simplified geometries, tile layers) in **materialized views** with a refresh policy.\n\n","lineStart":644,"lineEnd":651,"html":"<h2>4.8.1 Schema design basics</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>One geometry type and one SRID per column</strong> (typmod enforces it). Store in a single authoritative CRS; derive other CRSs with generated columns, views, or on-the-fly <code>ST_Transform</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Separate layers by geometry type</strong> instead of mixing points and polygons in one column.</li>\n<li>Use <strong>surrogate integer keys</strong> plus a stable <strong>business key</strong> (e.g. <code>station_code</code>).</li>\n<li>Partition very large tables (by region, tile, or time) and index each partition.</li>\n<li>Keep derived data (simplified geometries, tile layers) in <strong>materialized views</strong> with a refresh policy.</li>\n</ul>\n","sha256":"710f0259732ab2255e503aa7bb693e4aed5433a5dd966ea4932f81147ee2fc01"},{"key":"4.8.2","id":"4-8-2","title":"4.8.2 Data-quality constraints","anchor":"482-data-quality-constraints","depth":4,"start":36258,"topics":["modeling"],"end":37399,"markdown":"#### 4.8.2 Data-quality constraints\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE district (\n    district_id integer PRIMARY KEY,\n    name        text NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n    geom        geometry(Polygon, 28992) NOT NULL,           -- type + SRID enforced\n    CONSTRAINT district_valid CHECK (ST_IsValid(geom)),     -- no self-intersections etc.\n    CONSTRAINT district_nonempty CHECK (NOT ST_IsEmpty(geom)),\n    CONSTRAINT district_area CHECK (ST_Area(geom) BETWEEN 1 AND 1e10),    -- sanity bounds in m²\n    CONSTRAINT district_extent CHECK (geom && ST_MakeEnvelope(0, 289000, 300000, 629000, 28992)) -- illustrative national extent\n);\n```\n\nOther tools: `ST_MakeValid` in an ingestion step (not inside a `CHECK`, which should reject rather than repair silently); `ST_SnapToGrid`/`ST_ReducePrecision` to fix near-duplicate vertices; `ST_NDims` to enforce 2D; point-uniqueness via `EXCLUDE USING gist (geom WITH &&)` (for points, bbox overlap means identical location). Audit existing data with queries rather than constraints when rules are expensive or need tolerance:\n\n```sql\nSELECT district_id, ST_IsValidReason(geom) FROM district WHERE NOT ST_IsValid(geom);\n```\n\n","lineStart":652,"lineEnd":671,"html":"<h2>4.8.2 Data-quality constraints</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CREATE TABLE district (\n    district_id integer PRIMARY KEY,\n    name        text NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n    geom        geometry(Polygon, 28992) NOT NULL,           -- type + SRID enforced\n    CONSTRAINT district_valid CHECK (ST_IsValid(geom)),     -- no self-intersections etc.\n    CONSTRAINT district_nonempty CHECK (NOT ST_IsEmpty(geom)),\n    CONSTRAINT district_area CHECK (ST_Area(geom) BETWEEN 1 AND 1e10),    -- sanity bounds in m²\n    CONSTRAINT district_extent CHECK (geom &amp;&amp; ST_MakeEnvelope(0, 289000, 300000, 629000, 28992)) -- illustrative national extent\n);</code></pre><p>Other tools: <code>ST_MakeValid</code> in an ingestion step (not inside a <code>CHECK</code>, which should reject rather than repair silently); <code>ST_SnapToGrid</code>/<code>ST_ReducePrecision</code> to fix near-duplicate vertices; <code>ST_NDims</code> to enforce 2D; point-uniqueness via <code>EXCLUDE USING gist (geom WITH &amp;&amp;)</code> (for points, bbox overlap means identical location). Audit existing data with queries rather than constraints when rules are expensive or need tolerance:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT district_id, ST_IsValidReason(geom) FROM district WHERE NOT ST_IsValid(geom);</code></pre>","sha256":"bd7194eca559f714c9f5863234395a9e5df03c9a07e91c021379d90a77931ebc"},{"key":"4.8.3","id":"4-8-3","title":"4.8.3 Versioning and temporal data","anchor":"483-versioning-and-temporal-data","depth":4,"start":37399,"topics":["registry"],"end":38922,"markdown":"#### 4.8.3 Versioning and temporal data\n\nThree different ideas are often conflated:\n\n| Concept | Question answered | Typical implementation |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Valid time** | \"When was this true in the real world?\" | `tstzrange` column on each version row |\n| **Transaction (system) time** | \"When did the database know it?\" | History table + trigger, or extension-based system versioning |\n| **Bitemporal** | Both | Two range columns; more complex queries |\n\nA robust valid-time pattern in PostgreSQL uses a **range column plus an exclusion constraint** so the database itself forbids two overlapping versions of the same feature:\n\n```sql\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS btree_gist;     -- allows \"=\" on scalar columns inside a GiST index\n\nCREATE TABLE station_version (\n    version_id   bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n    station_id   integer NOT NULL,\n    name         text NOT NULL,\n    geom         geometry(Point, 28992) NOT NULL,\n    valid_during tstzrange NOT NULL,\n    CONSTRAINT no_overlapping_versions\n        EXCLUDE USING gist (station_id WITH =, valid_during WITH &&)\n);\nCREATE INDEX station_version_geom_gix ON station_version USING gist (geom);\n\n-- As-of query: what existed at a given instant?\nSELECT * FROM station_version WHERE valid_during @> timestamptz '2026-06-01 00:00+00';\n```\n\nRange conventions: use half-open ranges `[start, end)` so that consecutive versions touch without overlapping; `NULL` as the upper bound means \"still current\". Always store timestamps as `timestamptz`.\n\n","lineStart":672,"lineEnd":703,"html":"<h2>4.8.3 Versioning and temporal data</h2>\n<p>Three different ideas are often conflated:</p>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 4.8.3 Versioning and temporal data\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Question answered</th>\n<th>Typical implementation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Valid time</strong></td>\n<td>&quot;When was this true in the real world?&quot;</td>\n<td><code>tstzrange</code> column on each version row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Transaction (system) time</strong></td>\n<td>&quot;When did the database know it?&quot;</td>\n<td>History table + trigger, or extension-based system versioning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Bitemporal</strong></td>\n<td>Both</td>\n<td>Two range columns; more complex queries</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p>A robust valid-time pattern in PostgreSQL uses a <strong>range column plus an exclusion constraint</strong> so the database itself forbids two overlapping versions of the same feature:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS btree_gist;     -- allows &quot;=&quot; on scalar columns inside a GiST index\n\nCREATE TABLE station_version (\n    version_id   bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n    station_id   integer NOT NULL,\n    name         text NOT NULL,\n    geom         geometry(Point, 28992) NOT NULL,\n    valid_during tstzrange NOT NULL,\n    CONSTRAINT no_overlapping_versions\n        EXCLUDE USING gist (station_id WITH =, valid_during WITH &amp;&amp;)\n);\nCREATE INDEX station_version_geom_gix ON station_version USING gist (geom);\n\n-- As-of query: what existed at a given instant?\nSELECT * FROM station_version WHERE valid_during @&gt; timestamptz &#39;2026-06-01 00:00+00&#39;;</code></pre><p>Range conventions: use half-open ranges <code>[start, end)</code> so that consecutive versions touch without overlapping; <code>NULL</code> as the upper bound means &quot;still current&quot;. Always store timestamps as <code>timestamptz</code>.</p>\n","sha256":"89f10c68cd87139d2b4c2d1c9f1c861ddfdebfb389799b9c7cd00801e5045318"},{"key":"4.8.4","id":"4-8-4","title":"4.8.4 Topology-aware schemas","anchor":"484-topology-aware-schemas","depth":4,"start":38922,"topics":["modeling"],"end":40611,"markdown":"#### 4.8.4 Topology-aware schemas\n\n\"Topology-aware\" can mean two things:\n\n1. **Topological data model** (`postgis_topology` extension): nodes, edges, and faces stored once, features reference them as `TopoGeometry` values. Shared boundaries cannot drift apart because there is only one copy. Typical API: `CreateTopology`, `AddTopoGeometryColumn`, `ST_CreateTopoGeo`, `ValidateTopology`. Powerful but heavier to load, query, and maintain.\n2. **Simple features + enforced topological rules** (more common): keep ordinary polygons and verify rules with queries/triggers — *no overlaps*, *no gaps*, *must be covered by*, *lines must end at nodes*. PostGIS also provides coverage functions (e.g. `ST_CoverageUnion`, `ST_CoverageInvalidEdges`, `ST_CoverageSimplify`, available in recent PostGIS/GEOS versions — verify).\n\nOverlap check for a polygon layer (parcels, districts):\n\n```sql\nSELECT a.district_id AS a_id, b.district_id AS b_id,\n       ST_Area(ST_Intersection(a.geom, b.geom)) AS overlap_m2\nFROM district a\nJOIN district b\n  ON a.district_id < b.district_id            -- each pair once\n AND ST_Intersects(a.geom, b.geom)             -- index-assisted\nWHERE ST_Area(ST_Intersection(a.geom, b.geom)) > 0.01;   -- ignore boundary-only contact\n```\n\nGap check (holes in the dissolved coverage): `SELECT ST_NumInteriorRings(g) FROM (SELECT (ST_Dump(ST_Union(geom))).geom AS g FROM district) t;` — any interior ring in the union is a gap (or an intentional enclave).\n\nBecause `ST_Contains`/`ST_Overlaps` are functions, not operators, they cannot appear in an `EXCLUDE` constraint; enforce \"no overlapping polygons\" with a `BEFORE INSERT OR UPDATE` trigger or by periodic audit queries.\n\n---\n","lineStart":704,"lineEnd":727,"html":"<h2>4.8.4 Topology-aware schemas</h2>\n<p>&quot;Topology-aware&quot; can mean two things:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Topological data model</strong> (<code>postgis_topology</code> extension): nodes, edges, and faces stored once, features reference them as <code>TopoGeometry</code> values. Shared boundaries cannot drift apart because there is only one copy. Typical API: <code>CreateTopology</code>, <code>AddTopoGeometryColumn</code>, <code>ST_CreateTopoGeo</code>, <code>ValidateTopology</code>. Powerful but heavier to load, query, and maintain.</li>\n<li><strong>Simple features + enforced topological rules</strong> (more common): keep ordinary polygons and verify rules with queries/triggers — <em>no overlaps</em>, <em>no gaps</em>, <em>must be covered by</em>, <em>lines must end at nodes</em>. PostGIS also provides coverage functions (e.g. <code>ST_CoverageUnion</code>, <code>ST_CoverageInvalidEdges</code>, <code>ST_CoverageSimplify</code>, available in recent PostGIS/GEOS versions — verify).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Overlap check for a polygon layer (parcels, districts):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT a.district_id AS a_id, b.district_id AS b_id,\n       ST_Area(ST_Intersection(a.geom, b.geom)) AS overlap_m2\nFROM district a\nJOIN district b\n  ON a.district_id &lt; b.district_id            -- each pair once\n AND ST_Intersects(a.geom, b.geom)             -- index-assisted\nWHERE ST_Area(ST_Intersection(a.geom, b.geom)) &gt; 0.01;   -- ignore boundary-only contact</code></pre><p>Gap check (holes in the dissolved coverage): <code>SELECT ST_NumInteriorRings(g) FROM (SELECT (ST_Dump(ST_Union(geom))).geom AS g FROM district) t;</code> — any interior ring in the union is a gap (or an intentional enclave).</p>\n<p>Because <code>ST_Contains</code>/<code>ST_Overlaps</code> are functions, not operators, they cannot appear in an <code>EXCLUDE</code> constraint; enforce &quot;no overlapping polygons&quot; with a <code>BEFORE INSERT OR UPDATE</code> trigger or by periodic audit queries.</p>\n<hr>\n","sha256":"5f6d6f129d83a4d6be9b8ba7e47faaf89a6aec730d512eec48bdfc8903e58a1d"},{"key":"5","id":"5","title":"5. Worked GIS Example — Emergency Response Analysis","anchor":"5-worked-gis-example--emergency-response-analysis","depth":2,"start":40611,"topics":["relationships"],"end":41117,"markdown":"## 5. Worked GIS Example — Emergency Response Analysis\n\n**Scenario.** A city fire service wants to (1) find the nearest station for each incident, (2) count incidents within a 2 km response radius of each station, (3) report incidents per district, (4) detect incident hot spots, and (5) serve incidents as vector tiles. The data below is **synthetic**: coordinates are plain numbers in **EPSG:28992 (metres)**, chosen so every result can be computed by hand with Pythagoras. They are not real locations.\n\n","lineStart":728,"lineEnd":731,"html":"<h2>5. Worked GIS Example — Emergency Response Analysis</h2>\n<p><strong>Scenario.</strong> A city fire service wants to (1) find the nearest station for each incident, (2) count incidents within a 2 km response radius of each station, (3) report incidents per district, (4) detect incident hot spots, and (5) serve incidents as vector tiles. The data below is <strong>synthetic</strong>: coordinates are plain numbers in <strong>EPSG:28992 (metres)</strong>, chosen so every result can be computed by hand with Pythagoras. They are not real locations.</p>\n","sha256":"46bfc57d0b0daa8f4016098c88487feece258e22d03c5f5f9135982c7c95e17f"},{"key":"5.1","id":"5-1","title":"5.1 Schematic layout (not to scale)","anchor":"51-schematic-layout-not-to-scale","depth":3,"start":41117,"topics":["relationships"],"end":42431,"markdown":"### 5.1 Schematic layout (not to scale)\n\n```text\n y=491000 +-------------------------+-------------------------+\n          |                         |                         |\n y=490000 |  S3                     |                         |\n          |                         |                         |\n y=489000 |              I3         |                         |\n          |                         |                         |\n y=487300 |   I1                    |                         |\n y=487000 |  S1                     I2           S2           |\n          |       District West     |      District East      |\n y=486000 +-------------------------+-------------------------+\n       x=119000                  x=122000                  x=125000\n\n S = fire station   I = incident   | at x=122000 is the shared district boundary\n Exact coordinates are in the table below.\n```\n\n| Feature | x (m) | y (m) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| S1 | 120000 | 487000 | inside West |\n| S2 | 123000 | 487000 | inside East |\n| S3 | 120000 | 490000 | inside West |\n| I1 | 120400 | 487300 | |\n| I2 | 122000 | 487000 | **exactly on the West/East boundary** |\n| I3 | 121400 | 489000 | |\n| District West | 119000–122000 | 486000–491000 | 3000 m × 5000 m = 15 km² |\n| District East | 122000–125000 | 486000–491000 | 15 km² |\n\n","lineStart":732,"lineEnd":761,"html":"<h2>5.1 Schematic layout (not to scale)</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\"> y=491000 +-------------------------+-------------------------+\n          |                         |                         |\n y=490000 |  S3                     |                         |\n          |                         |                         |\n y=489000 |              I3         |                         |\n          |                         |                         |\n y=487300 |   I1                    |                         |\n y=487000 |  S1                     I2           S2           |\n          |       District West     |      District East      |\n y=486000 +-------------------------+-------------------------+\n       x=119000                  x=122000                  x=125000\n\n S = fire station   I = incident   | at x=122000 is the shared district boundary\n Exact coordinates are in the table below.</code></pre><div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 5.1 Schematic layout (not to scale)\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>x (m)</th>\n<th>y (m)</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>S1</td>\n<td>120000</td>\n<td>487000</td>\n<td>inside West</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>S2</td>\n<td>123000</td>\n<td>487000</td>\n<td>inside East</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>S3</td>\n<td>120000</td>\n<td>490000</td>\n<td>inside West</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I1</td>\n<td>120400</td>\n<td>487300</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I2</td>\n<td>122000</td>\n<td>487000</td>\n<td><strong>exactly on the West/East boundary</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I3</td>\n<td>121400</td>\n<td>489000</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>District West</td>\n<td>119000–122000</td>\n<td>486000–491000</td>\n<td>3000 m × 5000 m = 15 km²</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>District East</td>\n<td>122000–125000</td>\n<td>486000–491000</td>\n<td>15 km²</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>","sha256":"98c3befef0026fbc1c42a5a35955e376d6ca02e75608aa8be8103a984b150b0a"},{"key":"5.2","id":"5-2","title":"5.2 Step 1 — Schema, data, indexes","anchor":"52-step-1--schema-data-indexes","depth":3,"start":42431,"topics":["relationships"],"end":43754,"markdown":"### 5.2 Step 1 — Schema, data, indexes\n\n```sql\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS postgis;\n\nCREATE TABLE fire_station (\n    station_id integer PRIMARY KEY,\n    name       text NOT NULL,\n    geom       geometry(Point, 28992) NOT NULL\n);\nCREATE TABLE incident (\n    incident_id integer PRIMARY KEY,\n    geom        geometry(Point, 28992) NOT NULL\n);\nCREATE TABLE district (\n    district_id integer PRIMARY KEY,\n    name        text NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n    geom        geometry(Polygon, 28992) NOT NULL,\n    CONSTRAINT district_valid CHECK (ST_IsValid(geom))\n);\n\nINSERT INTO fire_station VALUES\n (1,'S1',ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(120000,487000),28992)),\n (2,'S2',ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(123000,487000),28992)),\n (3,'S3',ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(120000,490000),28992));\n\nINSERT INTO incident VALUES\n (1,ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(120400,487300),28992)),\n (2,ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(122000,487000),28992)),\n (3,ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(121400,489000),28992));\n\nINSERT INTO district VALUES\n (1,'West',ST_MakeEnvelope(119000,486000,122000,491000,28992)),\n (2,'East',ST_MakeEnvelope(122000,486000,125000,491000,28992));\n\nCREATE INDEX fire_station_geom_gix ON fire_station USING gist (geom);\nCREATE INDEX incident_geom_gix     ON incident     USING gist (geom);\nCREATE INDEX district_geom_gix     ON district     USING gist (geom);\nANALYZE;\n```\n\n","lineStart":762,"lineEnd":802,"html":"<h2>5.2 Step 1 — Schema, data, indexes</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS postgis;\n\nCREATE TABLE fire_station (\n    station_id integer PRIMARY KEY,\n    name       text NOT NULL,\n    geom       geometry(Point, 28992) NOT NULL\n);\nCREATE TABLE incident (\n    incident_id integer PRIMARY KEY,\n    geom        geometry(Point, 28992) NOT NULL\n);\nCREATE TABLE district (\n    district_id integer PRIMARY KEY,\n    name        text NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n    geom        geometry(Polygon, 28992) NOT NULL,\n    CONSTRAINT district_valid CHECK (ST_IsValid(geom))\n);\n\nINSERT INTO fire_station VALUES\n (1,&#39;S1&#39;,ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(120000,487000),28992)),\n (2,&#39;S2&#39;,ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(123000,487000),28992)),\n (3,&#39;S3&#39;,ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(120000,490000),28992));\n\nINSERT INTO incident VALUES\n (1,ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(120400,487300),28992)),\n (2,ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(122000,487000),28992)),\n (3,ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(121400,489000),28992));\n\nINSERT INTO district VALUES\n (1,&#39;West&#39;,ST_MakeEnvelope(119000,486000,122000,491000,28992)),\n (2,&#39;East&#39;,ST_MakeEnvelope(122000,486000,125000,491000,28992));\n\nCREATE INDEX fire_station_geom_gix ON fire_station USING gist (geom);\nCREATE INDEX incident_geom_gix     ON incident     USING gist (geom);\nCREATE INDEX district_geom_gix     ON district     USING gist (geom);\nANALYZE;</code></pre>","sha256":"4c7675ab6a26838b49395c02ea41c80f50a7bd0a9d4e3e7796fa1e2667b3de47"},{"key":"5.3","id":"5-3","title":"5.3 Step 2 — Validate the load","anchor":"53-step-2--validate-the-load","depth":3,"start":43754,"topics":["relationships"],"end":44140,"markdown":"### 5.3 Step 2 — Validate the load\n\n```sql\nSELECT f_table_name, f_geometry_column, srid, type\nFROM geometry_columns\nWHERE f_table_name IN ('fire_station','incident','district')\nORDER BY f_table_name;\n-- district|geom|28992|POLYGON ; fire_station|geom|28992|POINT ; incident|geom|28992|POINT\n\nSELECT name, ST_Area(geom) FROM district ORDER BY name;\n-- East|15000000 ; West|15000000\n```\n\n","lineStart":803,"lineEnd":815,"html":"<h2>5.3 Step 2 — Validate the load</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT f_table_name, f_geometry_column, srid, type\nFROM geometry_columns\nWHERE f_table_name IN (&#39;fire_station&#39;,&#39;incident&#39;,&#39;district&#39;)\nORDER BY f_table_name;\n-- district|geom|28992|POLYGON ; fire_station|geom|28992|POINT ; incident|geom|28992|POINT\n\nSELECT name, ST_Area(geom) FROM district ORDER BY name;\n-- East|15000000 ; West|15000000</code></pre>","sha256":"09c23b56581a58fc6198ce193da607fc0cb0d11d2b24548b3ccd8fd30c91ff12"},{"key":"5.4","id":"5-4","title":"5.4 Step 3 — Nearest station per incident (KNN)","anchor":"54-step-3--nearest-station-per-incident-knn","depth":3,"start":44140,"topics":["queries"],"end":44740,"markdown":"### 5.4 Step 3 — Nearest station per incident (KNN)\n\n```sql\nSELECT i.incident_id, s.name,\n       round(ST_Distance(i.geom, s.geom)::numeric, 2) AS dist_m\nFROM incident i\nCROSS JOIN LATERAL (\n    SELECT name, geom FROM fire_station ORDER BY geom <-> i.geom LIMIT 1\n) s\nORDER BY i.incident_id;\n```\n\n**Expected result and hand check**\n\n| incident_id | name | dist_m | Hand calculation |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | S1 | 500.00 | √(400² + 300²) = 500 |\n| 2 | S2 | 1000.00 | S2 is 1000 m east; S1 is 2000 m west |\n| 3 | S3 | 1720.47 | √(1400² + 1000²) = √2 960 000 ≈ 1720.47 (S1: ≈ 2441.31, S2: ≈ 2561.25) |\n\n","lineStart":816,"lineEnd":835,"html":"<h2>5.4 Step 3 — Nearest station per incident (KNN)</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT i.incident_id, s.name,\n       round(ST_Distance(i.geom, s.geom)::numeric, 2) AS dist_m\nFROM incident i\nCROSS JOIN LATERAL (\n    SELECT name, geom FROM fire_station ORDER BY geom &lt;-&gt; i.geom LIMIT 1\n) s\nORDER BY i.incident_id;</code></pre><p><strong>Expected result and hand check</strong></p>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 5.4 Step 3 — Nearest station per incident (KNN)\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>incident_id</th>\n<th>name</th>\n<th>dist_m</th>\n<th>Hand calculation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>S1</td>\n<td>500.00</td>\n<td>√(400² + 300²) = 500</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>S2</td>\n<td>1000.00</td>\n<td>S2 is 1000 m east; S1 is 2000 m west</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>S3</td>\n<td>1720.47</td>\n<td>√(1400² + 1000²) = √2 960 000 ≈ 1720.47 (S1: ≈ 2441.31, S2: ≈ 2561.25)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>","sha256":"de1a7e90b93c9d3f8027e34cbf07fa19ef1ae2e06ecf647b040515f8a903cf16"},{"key":"5.5","id":"5-5","title":"5.5 Step 4 — Response radius with `ST_DWithin` (inclusive)","anchor":"55-step-4--response-radius-with-st_dwithin-inclusive","depth":3,"start":44740,"topics":["queries"],"end":45229,"markdown":"### 5.5 Step 4 — Response radius with `ST_DWithin` (inclusive)\n\n```sql\nSELECT s.name, count(i.incident_id) AS incidents_within_2km\nFROM fire_station s\nLEFT JOIN incident i ON ST_DWithin(s.geom, i.geom, 2000)\nGROUP BY s.name\nORDER BY s.name;\n```\n\n**Expected:** `S1 | 2`, `S2 | 1`, `S3 | 1`.\n\nHand check: S1–I1 = 500 ✔, S1–I2 = **exactly 2000** ✔ (inclusive), S1–I3 ≈ 2441.31 ✘. S2–I2 = 1000 ✔, S2–I1 ≈ 2617.25 ✘, S2–I3 ≈ 2561.25 ✘. S3–I3 ≈ 1720.47 ✔, S3–I1 ≈ 2729.47 ✘, S3–I2 ≈ 3605.55 ✘.\n\n","lineStart":836,"lineEnd":849,"html":"<h2>5.5 Step 4 — Response radius with <code>ST_DWithin</code> (inclusive)</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT s.name, count(i.incident_id) AS incidents_within_2km\nFROM fire_station s\nLEFT JOIN incident i ON ST_DWithin(s.geom, i.geom, 2000)\nGROUP BY s.name\nORDER BY s.name;</code></pre><p><strong>Expected:</strong> <code>S1 | 2</code>, <code>S2 | 1</code>, <code>S3 | 1</code>.</p>\n<p>Hand check: S1–I1 = 500 ✔, S1–I2 = <strong>exactly 2000</strong> ✔ (inclusive), S1–I3 ≈ 2441.31 ✘. S2–I2 = 1000 ✔, S2–I1 ≈ 2617.25 ✘, S2–I3 ≈ 2561.25 ✘. S3–I3 ≈ 1720.47 ✔, S3–I1 ≈ 2729.47 ✘, S3–I2 ≈ 3605.55 ✘.</p>\n","sha256":"bad5195188bf0c3a20c5699d1a73a13b44a6a0d359aaf5ed04c36f6ab1fcc9d2"},{"key":"5.6","id":"5-6","title":"5.6 Step 5 — Incidents per district: boundary behaviour","anchor":"56-step-5--incidents-per-district-boundary-behaviour","depth":3,"start":45229,"topics":["relationships"],"end":46257,"markdown":"### 5.6 Step 5 — Incidents per district: boundary behaviour\n\n```sql\n-- Any contact counts\nSELECT d.name, count(i.incident_id) AS n_intersects\nFROM district d LEFT JOIN incident i ON ST_Intersects(d.geom, i.geom)\nGROUP BY d.name ORDER BY d.name;\n-- East|1 ; West|3        (I2 lies on the shared boundary, so it intersects BOTH)\n\n-- Strictly interior only\nSELECT d.name, count(i.incident_id) AS n_contains\nFROM district d LEFT JOIN incident i ON ST_Contains(d.geom, i.geom)\nGROUP BY d.name ORDER BY d.name;\n-- East|0 ; West|2        (I2 is in neither interior)\n\nSELECT d.name, ST_Relate(d.geom, i.geom)\nFROM district d, incident i WHERE i.incident_id = 2 ORDER BY d.name;\n-- East|FF20F1FF2 ; West|FF20F1FF2\n```\n\nLesson: the sum of `ST_Intersects` counts is 4 but there are only 3 incidents (double counting), while `ST_Contains` counts sum to 2 (one incident lost). Assigning each point to exactly one polygon requires a deterministic tiebreak rule, for example `DISTINCT ON (incident_id) ... ORDER BY incident_id, district_id`.\n\n","lineStart":850,"lineEnd":871,"html":"<h2>5.6 Step 5 — Incidents per district: boundary behaviour</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">-- Any contact counts\nSELECT d.name, count(i.incident_id) AS n_intersects\nFROM district d LEFT JOIN incident i ON ST_Intersects(d.geom, i.geom)\nGROUP BY d.name ORDER BY d.name;\n-- East|1 ; West|3        (I2 lies on the shared boundary, so it intersects BOTH)\n\n-- Strictly interior only\nSELECT d.name, count(i.incident_id) AS n_contains\nFROM district d LEFT JOIN incident i ON ST_Contains(d.geom, i.geom)\nGROUP BY d.name ORDER BY d.name;\n-- East|0 ; West|2        (I2 is in neither interior)\n\nSELECT d.name, ST_Relate(d.geom, i.geom)\nFROM district d, incident i WHERE i.incident_id = 2 ORDER BY d.name;\n-- East|FF20F1FF2 ; West|FF20F1FF2</code></pre><p>Lesson: the sum of <code>ST_Intersects</code> counts is 4 but there are only 3 incidents (double counting), while <code>ST_Contains</code> counts sum to 2 (one incident lost). Assigning each point to exactly one polygon requires a deterministic tiebreak rule, for example <code>DISTINCT ON (incident_id) ... ORDER BY incident_id, district_id</code>.</p>\n","sha256":"f0ee46cedea767e683e52e0212ef00f61df467226ecd564883b92760104db757"},{"key":"5.7","id":"5-7","title":"5.7 Step 6 — Hot spots with DBSCAN","anchor":"57-step-6--hot-spots-with-dbscan","depth":3,"start":46257,"topics":["queries"],"end":46802,"markdown":"### 5.7 Step 6 — Hot spots with DBSCAN\n\n```sql\nSELECT incident_id,\n       ST_ClusterDBSCAN(geom, eps := 2000, minpoints := 2) OVER () AS cluster_id\nFROM incident ORDER BY incident_id;\n```\n\nPairwise distances: I1–I2 ≈ 1627.88, I1–I3 ≈ 1972.31, I2–I3 ≈ 2088.06. With `eps = 2000`, I1 is within reach of both others, so all three form **one cluster** (`cluster_id = 0` for all rows) even though I2–I3 alone exceed `eps` (density-reachability is transitive through I1). With `eps = 1000` no pair qualifies and every `cluster_id` is `NULL` (noise).\n\n","lineStart":872,"lineEnd":881,"html":"<h2>5.7 Step 6 — Hot spots with DBSCAN</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT incident_id,\n       ST_ClusterDBSCAN(geom, eps := 2000, minpoints := 2) OVER () AS cluster_id\nFROM incident ORDER BY incident_id;</code></pre><p>Pairwise distances: I1–I2 ≈ 1627.88, I1–I3 ≈ 1972.31, I2–I3 ≈ 2088.06. With <code>eps = 2000</code>, I1 is within reach of both others, so all three form <strong>one cluster</strong> (<code>cluster_id = 0</code> for all rows) even though I2–I3 alone exceed <code>eps</code> (density-reachability is transitive through I1). With <code>eps = 1000</code> no pair qualifies and every <code>cluster_id</code> is <code>NULL</code> (noise).</p>\n","sha256":"696d9e75c200bdbd62350d4319c7ed9c2919d664bfae02faa1aa014f435342fa"},{"key":"5.8","id":"5-8","title":"5.8 Step 7 — Check the plan","anchor":"58-step-7--check-the-plan","depth":3,"start":46802,"topics":["indexes"],"end":47279,"markdown":"### 5.8 Step 7 — Check the plan\n\n```sql\nSET enable_seqscan = off;      -- learning only: tables are tiny\nEXPLAIN SELECT * FROM incident WHERE ST_DWithin(geom, ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(120000,487000),28992), 2000);\nRESET enable_seqscan;\n```\n\nYou should see `Index Scan using incident_geom_gix` with an `Index Cond` containing `&&`. Without `enable_seqscan = off`, a sequential scan on 3 rows is the correct planner choice — index benefits appear at thousands of rows and above.\n\n","lineStart":882,"lineEnd":891,"html":"<h2>5.8 Step 7 — Check the plan</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SET enable_seqscan = off;      -- learning only: tables are tiny\nEXPLAIN SELECT * FROM incident WHERE ST_DWithin(geom, ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(120000,487000),28992), 2000);\nRESET enable_seqscan;</code></pre><p>You should see <code>Index Scan using incident_geom_gix</code> with an <code>Index Cond</code> containing <code>&amp;&amp;</code>. Without <code>enable_seqscan = off</code>, a sequential scan on 3 rows is the correct planner choice — index benefits appear at thousands of rows and above.</p>\n","sha256":"9a902e305fb35f3cc294b9cd5b9b6006efe2a5c1b48f3cfd2db65481a635b2d6"},{"key":"5.9","id":"5-9","title":"5.9 Step 8 — Vector tile","anchor":"59-step-8--vector-tile","depth":3,"start":47279,"topics":["tiles"],"end":47958,"markdown":"### 5.9 Step 8 — Vector tile\n\nCreate `incident_mvt(z,x,y)` from §4.5.4. Because the incidents are regional data in EPSG:28992, request tiles that cover them; transforming the bounds of a very low-zoom (whole-world) tile into a regional CRS can fail or give meaningless bounds, which is why production services usually store tile-served data in EPSG:4326/3857 or restrict minimum zoom.\n\nLocating the tile for a lon/lat (Amsterdam city centre ≈ 4.9° E, 52.37° N) at zoom 10 with the formulas of §4.5.4:\n\n```\nn = 2^10 = 1024\nx = floor((4.9 + 180) / 360 × 1024) = floor(525.9) = 525\ny = floor((1 − asinh(tan(52.37°)) / π) / 2 × 1024) ≈ floor(336.5) = 336\n→ tile 10/525/336\n```\n\n---\n\n","lineStart":892,"lineEnd":906,"html":"<h2>5.9 Step 8 — Vector tile</h2>\n<p>Create <code>incident_mvt(z,x,y)</code> from §4.5.4. Because the incidents are regional data in EPSG:28992, request tiles that cover them; transforming the bounds of a very low-zoom (whole-world) tile into a regional CRS can fail or give meaningless bounds, which is why production services usually store tile-served data in EPSG:4326/3857 or restrict minimum zoom.</p>\n<p>Locating the tile for a lon/lat (Amsterdam city centre ≈ 4.9° E, 52.37° N) at zoom 10 with the formulas of §4.5.4:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">n = 2^10 = 1024\nx = floor((4.9 + 180) / 360 × 1024) = floor(525.9) = 525\ny = floor((1 − asinh(tan(52.37°)) / π) / 2 × 1024) ≈ floor(336.5) = 336\n→ tile 10/525/336</code></pre><hr>\n","sha256":"e3a1cc5112a0c68007e828113da3a1809182a3ec54577bfbe069a15e4be5c650"},{"key":"6","id":"6","title":"6. Common Pitfalls and Best Practices","anchor":"6-common-pitfalls-and-best-practices","depth":2,"start":47958,"topics":["modeling"],"end":51307,"markdown":"## 6. Common Pitfalls and Best Practices\n\n| # | Pitfall | Symptom | Best practice |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Axis order confusion** (lat/lon vs lon/lat) | Points land in the wrong hemisphere or at sea | PostGIS is **x = lon, y = lat**. `ST_MakePoint(lon, lat)`. Check GeoJSON (always lon,lat) vs EPSG:4326 definitions in other tools |\n| 2 | **`ST_SetSRID` used instead of `ST_Transform`** | Coordinates are labeled correctly but are numerically wrong | `SetSRID` relabels; `Transform` reprojects. Verify with a known point |\n| 3 | **SRID mismatch** | Error \"Operation on mixed SRID geometries\"; or empty results when SRID `0` is mixed in | Enforce typmod SRIDs; transform at the query boundary, not on the indexed column |\n| 4 | **Metric operations in EPSG:4326 or EPSG:3857** | Distances in degrees; 3857 distances/areas inflated (≈ 1.64× linear at 52° N) | Use a local projected CRS, or `geography` |\n| 5 | **`ST_Distance(...) < d` in `WHERE`** | Sequential scan, slow joins | `ST_DWithin` for filtering; `<->` for ordering |\n| 6 | **Wrapping the indexed column** (`ST_Transform(geom, 3857) && x`) | Index not used | Transform the *constant*, or create an expression index / generated column |\n| 7 | **Missing `ANALYZE` after bulk load** | Bad plans, wrong row estimates | `ANALYZE` after loads; consider autovacuum tuning |\n| 8 | **Invalid geometries** | `TopologyException`, wrong areas, failing `ST_Union` | `CHECK (ST_IsValid(geom))` for clean data; `ST_MakeValid` in the ingestion pipeline; log what was repaired |\n| 9 | **Huge polygons** | Slow joins, large `Rows Removed by Filter` | `ST_Subdivide`; keep simplified copies for display |\n| 10 | **Boundary semantics** | Points on edges missed or double counted | Choose `ST_Covers` vs `ST_Contains` deliberately; define a tiebreak rule (§5.6) |\n| 11 | **Geography limits** | Missing functions, slower queries; antimeridian/pole edge cases | Prefer `geometry` + projected CRS for heavy analysis; cast to `geography` for specific distance/area calls |\n| 12 | **KNN with extra filters** | Index walks many rows | Partial indexes, bound with `ST_DWithin`, or restructure the query |\n| 13 | **Floating-point equality** | `ST_Equals` fails on \"same\" points after reprojection | Snap with `ST_SnapToGrid`/`ST_ReducePrecision`, compare with tolerance (`ST_DWithin(a,b,0.001)`) |\n| 14 | **Empty / NULL geometries** | `NULL` predicates, empty results | `NOT NULL` + `NOT ST_IsEmpty` constraints where appropriate |\n| 15 | **Routing graph quality** | No route found, absurd detours | Check `pgr_connectedComponents`, one-way encoding (`reverse_cost = -1`), non-negative costs, unit consistency |\n| 16 | **Temporal overlaps** | Two \"current\" versions of one feature | Range type + `EXCLUDE USING gist (id WITH =, range WITH &&)` |\n| 17 | **Cross-engine assumptions** | Function behaves differently in DuckDB/SQL Server/Oracle | Re-test semantics (e.g. boundary rules, units, validity handling) per engine |\n\n**Validation habits.** (1) Reconcile row counts after every load. (2) Assert `ST_SRID`, `GeometryType`, validity, and extent. (3) Spot-check ten random features in QGIS. (4) Keep a regression suite of small hand-verifiable datasets like §5 and run it after upgrades — GEOS/PROJ upgrades can change edge-case results. (5) Use `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` on every query you intend to ship.\n\n---\n","lineStart":907,"lineEnd":931,"html":"<h2>6. Common Pitfalls and Best Practices</h2>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 6. Common Pitfalls and Best Practices\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Pitfall</th>\n<th>Symptom</th>\n<th>Best practice</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><strong>Axis order confusion</strong> (lat/lon vs lon/lat)</td>\n<td>Points land in the wrong hemisphere or at sea</td>\n<td>PostGIS is <strong>x = lon, y = lat</strong>. <code>ST_MakePoint(lon, lat)</code>. Check GeoJSON (always lon,lat) vs EPSG:4326 definitions in other tools</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><strong><code>ST_SetSRID</code> used instead of <code>ST_Transform</code></strong></td>\n<td>Coordinates are labeled correctly but are numerically wrong</td>\n<td><code>SetSRID</code> relabels; <code>Transform</code> reprojects. Verify with a known point</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><strong>SRID mismatch</strong></td>\n<td>Error &quot;Operation on mixed SRID geometries&quot;; or empty results when SRID <code>0</code> is mixed in</td>\n<td>Enforce typmod SRIDs; transform at the query boundary, not on the indexed column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><strong>Metric operations in EPSG:4326 or EPSG:3857</strong></td>\n<td>Distances in degrees; 3857 distances/areas inflated (≈ 1.64× linear at 52° N)</td>\n<td>Use a local projected CRS, or <code>geography</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><strong><code>ST_Distance(...) &lt; d</code> in <code>WHERE</code></strong></td>\n<td>Sequential scan, slow joins</td>\n<td><code>ST_DWithin</code> for filtering; <code>&lt;-&gt;</code> for ordering</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td><strong>Wrapping the indexed column</strong> (<code>ST_Transform(geom, 3857) &amp;&amp; x</code>)</td>\n<td>Index not used</td>\n<td>Transform the <em>constant</em>, or create an expression index / generated column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td><strong>Missing <code>ANALYZE</code> after bulk load</strong></td>\n<td>Bad plans, wrong row estimates</td>\n<td><code>ANALYZE</code> after loads; consider autovacuum tuning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td><strong>Invalid geometries</strong></td>\n<td><code>TopologyException</code>, wrong areas, failing <code>ST_Union</code></td>\n<td><code>CHECK (ST_IsValid(geom))</code> for clean data; <code>ST_MakeValid</code> in the ingestion pipeline; log what was repaired</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td><strong>Huge polygons</strong></td>\n<td>Slow joins, large <code>Rows Removed by Filter</code></td>\n<td><code>ST_Subdivide</code>; keep simplified copies for display</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td><strong>Boundary semantics</strong></td>\n<td>Points on edges missed or double counted</td>\n<td>Choose <code>ST_Covers</code> vs <code>ST_Contains</code> deliberately; define a tiebreak rule (§5.6)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11</td>\n<td><strong>Geography limits</strong></td>\n<td>Missing functions, slower queries; antimeridian/pole edge cases</td>\n<td>Prefer <code>geometry</code> + projected CRS for heavy analysis; cast to <code>geography</code> for specific distance/area calls</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12</td>\n<td><strong>KNN with extra filters</strong></td>\n<td>Index walks many rows</td>\n<td>Partial indexes, bound with <code>ST_DWithin</code>, or restructure the query</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>13</td>\n<td><strong>Floating-point equality</strong></td>\n<td><code>ST_Equals</code> fails on &quot;same&quot; points after reprojection</td>\n<td>Snap with <code>ST_SnapToGrid</code>/<code>ST_ReducePrecision</code>, compare with tolerance (<code>ST_DWithin(a,b,0.001)</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>14</td>\n<td><strong>Empty / NULL geometries</strong></td>\n<td><code>NULL</code> predicates, empty results</td>\n<td><code>NOT NULL</code> + <code>NOT ST_IsEmpty</code> constraints where appropriate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>15</td>\n<td><strong>Routing graph quality</strong></td>\n<td>No route found, absurd detours</td>\n<td>Check <code>pgr_connectedComponents</code>, one-way encoding (<code>reverse_cost = -1</code>), non-negative costs, unit consistency</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>16</td>\n<td><strong>Temporal overlaps</strong></td>\n<td>Two &quot;current&quot; versions of one feature</td>\n<td>Range type + <code>EXCLUDE USING gist (id WITH =, range WITH &amp;&amp;)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>17</td>\n<td><strong>Cross-engine assumptions</strong></td>\n<td>Function behaves differently in DuckDB/SQL Server/Oracle</td>\n<td>Re-test semantics (e.g. boundary rules, units, validity handling) per engine</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p><strong>Validation habits.</strong> (1) Reconcile row counts after every load. (2) Assert <code>ST_SRID</code>, <code>GeometryType</code>, validity, and extent. (3) Spot-check ten random features in QGIS. (4) Keep a regression suite of small hand-verifiable datasets like §5 and run it after upgrades — GEOS/PROJ upgrades can change edge-case results. (5) Use <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code> on every query you intend to ship.</p>\n<hr>\n","sha256":"b09b72d432dea902c5492cee960b6cba3e43f299cddad49d6b115d94db05a21c"},{"key":"7","id":"7","title":"7. Progressive Exercises","anchor":"7-progressive-exercises","depth":2,"start":51307,"topics":["queries"],"end":51595,"markdown":"## 7. Progressive Exercises\n\nThe four exercises build on one another and reuse the synthetic dataset from §5 (EPSG:28992, metres). Work in a scratch database. Do **not** look at §8 until you have attempted each exercise and checked your results against the stated success criteria.\n\n---\n\n","lineStart":932,"lineEnd":937,"html":"<h2>7. Progressive Exercises</h2>\n<p>The four exercises build on one another and reuse the synthetic dataset from §5 (EPSG:28992, metres). Work in a scratch database. Do <strong>not</strong> look at §8 until you have attempted each exercise and checked your results against the stated success criteria.</p>\n<hr>\n","sha256":"3587a0ca80d6b1e60ef33209c8a1ffc891818e9e1dd90bada429cfeb08ec1777"},{"key":"exercise-1","id":"exercise-1","title":"Exercise 1 — Beginner: Foundations, SRIDs, and the geometry/geography difference","anchor":"exercise-1--beginner-foundations-srids-and-the-geometrygeography-difference","depth":3,"start":51595,"topics":["foundations"],"end":53021,"markdown":"### Exercise 1 — Beginner: Foundations, SRIDs, and the geometry/geography difference\n\n**Task.** Build the schema from scratch using only the coordinate table in §5.1 (if you already ran §5, drop the tables first). Then:\n\n1. Create `fire_station`, `incident`, and `district` with typmod geometry columns (type + SRID 28992), a `CHECK (ST_IsValid(geom))` on `district`, and a GiST index on each geometry column. Run `ANALYZE`.\n2. Query `geometry_columns` to confirm type and SRID of each table.\n3. Prove the typmod works: try to insert a point labeled EPSG:4326 into `incident` and capture the error message.\n4. Compute `ST_Distance` between `POINT(0 0)` and `POINT(1 0)` as `geometry` (SRID 4326) **and** as `geography`; explain the unit difference.\n5. Use `ST_Relate` to show the DE-9IM string for incident 2 against each district.\n\n**Prerequisites.** PostGIS installed; §2 and §4.1–4.3.\n\n**Sample input.** Coordinates in §5.1.\n\n**Expected deliverable.** One SQL script plus a short text file containing your query outputs for steps 2–5.\n\n**Success criteria.**\n* `geometry_columns` shows three rows with SRID 28992 and the correct types (`POINT`, `POINT`, `POLYGON`).\n* The wrong-SRID insert is **rejected**.\n* The geometry distance is `1` and the geography distance is ≈ `111319.49`.\n* Both `ST_Relate` strings equal `FF20F1FF2`.\n\n**Hint.** `ST_MakeEnvelope(xmin, ymin, xmax, ymax, srid)` builds each district polygon.\n\n---\n\n","lineStart":938,"lineEnd":963,"html":"<h2>Exercise 1 — Beginner: Foundations, SRIDs, and the geometry/geography difference</h2>\n<p><strong>Task.</strong> Build the schema from scratch using only the coordinate table in §5.1 (if you already ran §5, drop the tables first). Then:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Create <code>fire_station</code>, <code>incident</code>, and <code>district</code> with typmod geometry columns (type + SRID 28992), a <code>CHECK (ST_IsValid(geom))</code> on <code>district</code>, and a GiST index on each geometry column. Run <code>ANALYZE</code>.</li>\n<li>Query <code>geometry_columns</code> to confirm type and SRID of each table.</li>\n<li>Prove the typmod works: try to insert a point labeled EPSG:4326 into <code>incident</code> and capture the error message.</li>\n<li>Compute <code>ST_Distance</code> between <code>POINT(0 0)</code> and <code>POINT(1 0)</code> as <code>geometry</code> (SRID 4326) <strong>and</strong> as <code>geography</code>; explain the unit difference.</li>\n<li>Use <code>ST_Relate</code> to show the DE-9IM string for incident 2 against each district.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites.</strong> PostGIS installed; §2 and §4.1–4.3.</p>\n<p><strong>Sample input.</strong> Coordinates in §5.1.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected deliverable.</strong> One SQL script plus a short text file containing your query outputs for steps 2–5.</p>\n<p><strong>Success criteria.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>geometry_columns</code> shows three rows with SRID 28992 and the correct types (<code>POINT</code>, <code>POINT</code>, <code>POLYGON</code>).</li>\n<li>The wrong-SRID insert is <strong>rejected</strong>.</li>\n<li>The geometry distance is <code>1</code> and the geography distance is ≈ <code>111319.49</code>.</li>\n<li>Both <code>ST_Relate</code> strings equal <code>FF20F1FF2</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hint.</strong> <code>ST_MakeEnvelope(xmin, ymin, xmax, ymax, srid)</code> builds each district polygon.</p>\n<hr>\n","sha256":"f3b7a62ba929a8b616e54e8cd47f337c35be6dae7bbc9727a96dfd0f3adcd308"},{"key":"exercise-2","id":"exercise-2","title":"Exercise 2 — Intermediate: Index-aware spatial SQL","anchor":"exercise-2--intermediate-index-aware-spatial-sql","depth":3,"start":53021,"topics":["queries"],"end":54458,"markdown":"### Exercise 2 — Intermediate: Index-aware spatial SQL\n\n**Task.** Using your Exercise 1 database:\n\n1. A colleague wrote `SELECT … FROM incident i, fire_station s WHERE ST_Distance(i.geom, s.geom) < 2000;`. Rewrite it so the GiST index can be used, and show with `EXPLAIN` (with `enable_seqscan = off`) that an index scan with an `&&` condition appears.\n2. For each incident return its **two** nearest stations with rank and distance (KNN with `LATERAL`).\n3. Assign each incident to **exactly one** district, using `ST_Covers` and the deterministic rule \"if several districts match, choose the smallest `district_id`\".\n4. Run `ST_ClusterDBSCAN` with `eps = 1000` and then `eps = 2000` (`minpoints = 2`) and explain the difference.\n\n**Prerequisites.** Exercise 1; §4.4–4.5, §5.6.\n\n**Sample input.** Your three tables.\n\n**Expected deliverable.** A SQL script with four labeled queries, the `EXPLAIN` output for query 1, and a two-sentence explanation for query 4.\n\n**Success criteria.**\n* Query 1 returns 4 station/incident pairs (S1–I1, S1–I2, S2–I2, S3–I3) and the plan references the GiST index.\n* Query 2 returns six rows with the ranks/distances given in §8.2.\n* Query 3 returns three rows, each incident assigned once; incident 2 goes to `West`.\n* Query 4 returns all `NULL` for `eps = 1000` and all `0` for `eps = 2000`.\n\n**Hint.** For query 3 use `SELECT DISTINCT ON (i.incident_id) …  ORDER BY i.incident_id, d.district_id`.\n\n---\n\n","lineStart":964,"lineEnd":988,"html":"<h2>Exercise 2 — Intermediate: Index-aware spatial SQL</h2>\n<p><strong>Task.</strong> Using your Exercise 1 database:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A colleague wrote <code>SELECT … FROM incident i, fire_station s WHERE ST_Distance(i.geom, s.geom) &lt; 2000;</code>. Rewrite it so the GiST index can be used, and show with <code>EXPLAIN</code> (with <code>enable_seqscan = off</code>) that an index scan with an <code>&amp;&amp;</code> condition appears.</li>\n<li>For each incident return its <strong>two</strong> nearest stations with rank and distance (KNN with <code>LATERAL</code>).</li>\n<li>Assign each incident to <strong>exactly one</strong> district, using <code>ST_Covers</code> and the deterministic rule &quot;if several districts match, choose the smallest <code>district_id</code>&quot;.</li>\n<li>Run <code>ST_ClusterDBSCAN</code> with <code>eps = 1000</code> and then <code>eps = 2000</code> (<code>minpoints = 2</code>) and explain the difference.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites.</strong> Exercise 1; §4.4–4.5, §5.6.</p>\n<p><strong>Sample input.</strong> Your three tables.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected deliverable.</strong> A SQL script with four labeled queries, the <code>EXPLAIN</code> output for query 1, and a two-sentence explanation for query 4.</p>\n<p><strong>Success criteria.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Query 1 returns 4 station/incident pairs (S1–I1, S1–I2, S2–I2, S3–I3) and the plan references the GiST index.</li>\n<li>Query 2 returns six rows with the ranks/distances given in §8.2.</li>\n<li>Query 3 returns three rows, each incident assigned once; incident 2 goes to <code>West</code>.</li>\n<li>Query 4 returns all <code>NULL</code> for <code>eps = 1000</code> and all <code>0</code> for <code>eps = 2000</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hint.</strong> For query 3 use <code>SELECT DISTINCT ON (i.incident_id) …  ORDER BY i.incident_id, d.district_id</code>.</p>\n<hr>\n","sha256":"b525473363c19141846812c081a4317a74af1102a360bfa48b44ef14a7d2112f"},{"key":"exercise-3","id":"exercise-3","title":"Exercise 3 — Advanced: Network analysis with pgRouting","anchor":"exercise-3--advanced-network-analysis-with-pgrouting","depth":3,"start":54458,"topics":["routing"],"end":56233,"markdown":"### Exercise 3 — Advanced: Network analysis with pgRouting\n\n**Task.** Extend the analysis from straight-line to **travel-cost** thinking, using the abstract graph from §4.6 (`toy_edge`). Assume this *invented* mapping between the incident data and graph vertices (costs are abstract units, not metres):\n\n| Entity | Graph vertex |\n|---|---|\n| Station S1 | 1 |\n| Station S2 | 6 |\n| Incident I1 | 3 |\n| Incident I2 | 4 |\n| Incident I3 | 5 |\n\n1. Create `toy_edge` and compute, for each incident, the travel cost from each of the two stations with `pgr_dijkstraCost`.\n2. For each incident, pick the station with the lowest travel cost (the *network-nearest* station) and compare it with the Euclidean-nearest of {S1, S2} computed from the §5 coordinates. Explain any disagreement.\n3. Use `pgr_drivingDistance` from S1's vertex with a budget of 8 to find which incidents lie inside S1's service area.\n4. **Stretch:** make edge 9 (between vertices 5 and 6) one-way from 5 to 6 only, recompute the cost from S2's vertex to I3's vertex, and state whether the best station for I3 changes.\n\n**Prerequisites.** Exercises 1–2; §4.6.\n\n**Sample input.** The edge table in §4.6; the mapping above.\n\n**Expected deliverable.** SQL script, a result table for step 1, and a short paragraph for step 2.\n\n**Success criteria.**\n* Costs match: I1 (vertex 3): S1 = 2, S2 = 11; I2 (vertex 4): S1 = 8, S2 = 5; I3 (vertex 5): S1 = 10, S2 = 3.\n* You identify that I3's network-nearest station (S2) differs from its Euclidean-nearest station among {S1, S2} (S1).\n* The service area of S1 (budget 8) covers vertices 1, 3, 2, 4, hence incidents I1 and I2 but not I3.\n* Stretch: the S2→I3 cost becomes 8 and S2 remains the best station.\n\n**Hint.** One-way edges are expressed with `reverse_cost = -1`.\n\n---\n\n","lineStart":989,"lineEnd":1021,"html":"<h2>Exercise 3 — Advanced: Network analysis with pgRouting</h2>\n<p><strong>Task.</strong> Extend the analysis from straight-line to <strong>travel-cost</strong> thinking, using the abstract graph from §4.6 (<code>toy_edge</code>). Assume this <em>invented</em> mapping between the incident data and graph vertices (costs are abstract units, not metres):</p>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in Exercise 3 — Advanced: Network analysis with pgRouting\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Entity</th>\n<th>Graph vertex</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Station S1</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Station S2</td>\n<td>6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Incident I1</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Incident I2</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Incident I3</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><ol>\n<li>Create <code>toy_edge</code> and compute, for each incident, the travel cost from each of the two stations with <code>pgr_dijkstraCost</code>.</li>\n<li>For each incident, pick the station with the lowest travel cost (the <em>network-nearest</em> station) and compare it with the Euclidean-nearest of {S1, S2} computed from the §5 coordinates. Explain any disagreement.</li>\n<li>Use <code>pgr_drivingDistance</code> from S1&#39;s vertex with a budget of 8 to find which incidents lie inside S1&#39;s service area.</li>\n<li><strong>Stretch:</strong> make edge 9 (between vertices 5 and 6) one-way from 5 to 6 only, recompute the cost from S2&#39;s vertex to I3&#39;s vertex, and state whether the best station for I3 changes.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites.</strong> Exercises 1–2; §4.6.</p>\n<p><strong>Sample input.</strong> The edge table in §4.6; the mapping above.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected deliverable.</strong> SQL script, a result table for step 1, and a short paragraph for step 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Success criteria.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Costs match: I1 (vertex 3): S1 = 2, S2 = 11; I2 (vertex 4): S1 = 8, S2 = 5; I3 (vertex 5): S1 = 10, S2 = 3.</li>\n<li>You identify that I3&#39;s network-nearest station (S2) differs from its Euclidean-nearest station among {S1, S2} (S1).</li>\n<li>The service area of S1 (budget 8) covers vertices 1, 3, 2, 4, hence incidents I1 and I2 but not I3.</li>\n<li>Stretch: the S2→I3 cost becomes 8 and S2 remains the best station.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hint.</strong> One-way edges are expressed with <code>reverse_cost = -1</code>.</p>\n<hr>\n","sha256":"b60397b015a71e926b2635adc070dfa093ccb3db78c6af15963aa539a60d545f"},{"key":"exercise-4","id":"exercise-4","title":"Exercise 4 — Applied challenge: A versioned, validated, queryable station registry","anchor":"exercise-4--applied-challenge-a-versioned-validated-queryable-station-registry","depth":3,"start":56233,"topics":["registry"],"end":58810,"markdown":"### Exercise 4 — Applied challenge: A versioned, validated, queryable station registry\n\n**Scenario.** Station S2 is relocated to `(125000, 487000)` effective `2026-01-01 00:00 UTC`. The dispatch analytics team must be able to answer \"which station was nearest to each incident **as of** date X?\" for any past or present date, and the registry must be protected against bad data.\n\n**Task.** Design and implement, in one SQL script:\n\n1. A `station_version` table (valid-time model from §4.8.3) with typmod geometry, an exclusion constraint preventing overlapping versions per station, a GiST index on the geometry, and data-quality checks (`ST_IsValid`, not empty, inside the extent `(0, 289000)–(300000, 629000)`).\n2. Initial data: S1 and S3 current since `2020-01-01`; S2 at `(123000, 487000)` valid `[2020-01-01, 2026-01-01)` and at `(125000, 487000)` valid `[2026-01-01, ∞)`.\n3. A function `relocate_station(p_station_id int, p_new_geom geometry, p_effective timestamptz)` that **atomically** closes the current version and inserts the new one, rejecting an effective date that is not after the current version's start.\n4. A function `nearest_station_asof(p_point geometry, p_asof timestamptz)` returning station id, name, and distance using KNN on the versions valid at `p_asof`.\n5. Evidence of three negative tests: overlapping validity, wrong SRID, and a point outside the extent each fail.\n6. A short written note (5–8 lines) explaining why the as-of KNN filter on `valid_during` may become a performance concern at scale and one mitigation.\n\n**Prerequisites.** Exercises 1–3; §4.5.1, §4.8.\n\n**Sample input.** Incidents I1–I3 from §5; as-of dates `2025-06-01` and `2026-06-01`.\n\n**Expected deliverable.** `registry.sql` (schema, data, functions), a results file with the as-of outputs and the three error messages, and the note from step 6.\n\n**Success criteria.**\n* As of `2025-06-01`: I1 → S1 (500.00), I2 → S2 (1000.00), I3 → S3 (1720.47).\n* As of `2026-06-01`: I1 → S1 (500.00), **I2 → S1 (2000.00)**, I3 → S3 (1720.47).\n* Inserting an overlapping version for station 2 fails with an exclusion-constraint violation (SQLSTATE `23P01`); the other two negative tests fail with check/typmod errors.\n* `relocate_station` leaves exactly one open-ended version per station and no overlaps even if run twice for the same date (the second call must be rejected).\n\n**Hint.** Close the old version with `valid_during = tstzrange(lower(valid_during), p_effective, '[)')`, then insert `tstzrange(p_effective, NULL, '[)')`. You need `btree_gist` for the exclusion constraint.\n\n---\n\n","lineStart":1022,"lineEnd":1050,"html":"<h2>Exercise 4 — Applied challenge: A versioned, validated, queryable station registry</h2>\n<p><strong>Scenario.</strong> Station S2 is relocated to <code>(125000, 487000)</code> effective <code>2026-01-01 00:00 UTC</code>. The dispatch analytics team must be able to answer &quot;which station was nearest to each incident <strong>as of</strong> date X?&quot; for any past or present date, and the registry must be protected against bad data.</p>\n<p><strong>Task.</strong> Design and implement, in one SQL script:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A <code>station_version</code> table (valid-time model from §4.8.3) with typmod geometry, an exclusion constraint preventing overlapping versions per station, a GiST index on the geometry, and data-quality checks (<code>ST_IsValid</code>, not empty, inside the extent <code>(0, 289000)–(300000, 629000)</code>).</li>\n<li>Initial data: S1 and S3 current since <code>2020-01-01</code>; S2 at <code>(123000, 487000)</code> valid <code>[2020-01-01, 2026-01-01)</code> and at <code>(125000, 487000)</code> valid <code>[2026-01-01, ∞)</code>.</li>\n<li>A function <code>relocate_station(p_station_id int, p_new_geom geometry, p_effective timestamptz)</code> that <strong>atomically</strong> closes the current version and inserts the new one, rejecting an effective date that is not after the current version&#39;s start.</li>\n<li>A function <code>nearest_station_asof(p_point geometry, p_asof timestamptz)</code> returning station id, name, and distance using KNN on the versions valid at <code>p_asof</code>.</li>\n<li>Evidence of three negative tests: overlapping validity, wrong SRID, and a point outside the extent each fail.</li>\n<li>A short written note (5–8 lines) explaining why the as-of KNN filter on <code>valid_during</code> may become a performance concern at scale and one mitigation.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites.</strong> Exercises 1–3; §4.5.1, §4.8.</p>\n<p><strong>Sample input.</strong> Incidents I1–I3 from §5; as-of dates <code>2025-06-01</code> and <code>2026-06-01</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected deliverable.</strong> <code>registry.sql</code> (schema, data, functions), a results file with the as-of outputs and the three error messages, and the note from step 6.</p>\n<p><strong>Success criteria.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>As of <code>2025-06-01</code>: I1 → S1 (500.00), I2 → S2 (1000.00), I3 → S3 (1720.47).</li>\n<li>As of <code>2026-06-01</code>: I1 → S1 (500.00), <strong>I2 → S1 (2000.00)</strong>, I3 → S3 (1720.47).</li>\n<li>Inserting an overlapping version for station 2 fails with an exclusion-constraint violation (SQLSTATE <code>23P01</code>); the other two negative tests fail with check/typmod errors.</li>\n<li><code>relocate_station</code> leaves exactly one open-ended version per station and no overlaps even if run twice for the same date (the second call must be rejected).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hint.</strong> Close the old version with <code>valid_during = tstzrange(lower(valid_during), p_effective, &#39;[)&#39;)</code>, then insert <code>tstzrange(p_effective, NULL, &#39;[)&#39;)</code>. You need <code>btree_gist</code> for the exclusion constraint.</p>\n<hr>\n","sha256":"3f798e3c478bfd00823c9dd31aaed50315d1b9b63f6d077b265df2c1f6215a64"},{"key":"8","id":"8","title":"8. Solutions and Explanations","anchor":"8-solutions-and-explanations","depth":2,"start":58810,"topics":["foundations"],"end":58985,"markdown":"## 8. Solutions and Explanations\n\n> Attempt the exercises first. Outputs below were derived by hand from the synthetic coordinates and should be reproducible in PostGIS 3.x.\n\n","lineStart":1051,"lineEnd":1054,"html":"<h2>8. Solutions and Explanations</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Attempt the exercises first. Outputs below were derived by hand from the synthetic coordinates and should be reproducible in PostGIS 3.x.</p>\n</blockquote>\n","sha256":"95ee2d62d65aed6626017ba1dfc5a0c701e7ae7c8727994511b82539d1e0b246"},{"key":"8.1","id":"8-1","title":"8.1 Solution to Exercise 1","anchor":"81-solution-to-exercise-1","depth":3,"start":58985,"topics":["foundations"],"end":61764,"markdown":"### 8.1 Solution to Exercise 1\n\n```sql\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS postgis;\n\nCREATE TABLE fire_station (station_id integer PRIMARY KEY, name text NOT NULL,\n                           geom geometry(Point,28992) NOT NULL);\nCREATE TABLE incident     (incident_id integer PRIMARY KEY,\n                           geom geometry(Point,28992) NOT NULL);\nCREATE TABLE district     (district_id integer PRIMARY KEY, name text NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n                           geom geometry(Polygon,28992) NOT NULL,\n                           CONSTRAINT district_valid CHECK (ST_IsValid(geom)));\n\nINSERT INTO fire_station VALUES\n (1,'S1',ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(120000,487000),28992)),\n (2,'S2',ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(123000,487000),28992)),\n (3,'S3',ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(120000,490000),28992));\nINSERT INTO incident VALUES\n (1,ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(120400,487300),28992)),\n (2,ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(122000,487000),28992)),\n (3,ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(121400,489000),28992));\nINSERT INTO district VALUES\n (1,'West',ST_MakeEnvelope(119000,486000,122000,491000,28992)),\n (2,'East',ST_MakeEnvelope(122000,486000,125000,491000,28992));\n\nCREATE INDEX fire_station_geom_gix ON fire_station USING gist (geom);\nCREATE INDEX incident_geom_gix     ON incident     USING gist (geom);\nCREATE INDEX district_geom_gix     ON district     USING gist (geom);\nANALYZE;\n\n-- 2. Metadata\nSELECT f_table_name, srid, type FROM geometry_columns\nWHERE f_table_name IN ('fire_station','incident','district') ORDER BY 1;\n\n-- 3. Typmod protection (expected: ERROR  Geometry SRID (4326) does not match column SRID (28992))\nINSERT INTO incident VALUES (99, ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(4.9, 52.37), 4326));\n\n-- 4. Units\nSELECT ST_Distance('SRID=4326;POINT(0 0)'::geometry,  'SRID=4326;POINT(1 0)'::geometry)  AS deg,\n       ST_Distance('SRID=4326;POINT(0 0)'::geography, 'SRID=4326;POINT(1 0)'::geography) AS metres;\n\n-- 5. DE-9IM\nSELECT d.name, ST_Relate(d.geom, i.geom)\nFROM district d, incident i WHERE i.incident_id = 2 ORDER BY d.name;\n```\n\n**Expected output.** Step 2: `district POLYGON 28992`, `fire_station POINT 28992`, `incident POINT 28992`. Step 4: `deg = 1`, `metres ≈ 111319.49`. Step 5: `East | FF20F1FF2`, `West | FF20F1FF2`.\n\n**Why.** The typmod makes the column reject a different SRID instead of silently storing inconsistent data. The `geometry` distance is the planar distance in the CRS unit (degrees for EPSG:4326), whereas `geography` computes on the spheroid and returns metres (`6378137 × π/180 ≈ 111319.49` along the equator). `FF20F1FF2` has `F` in the interior/interior cell, confirming the point is on the boundary and in neither interior.\n\n**How to verify.** Row counts (`3, 3, 2`), `ST_AsText` on each table, and checking in QGIS that points fall where the schematic (§5.1) shows them.\n\n","lineStart":1055,"lineEnd":1106,"html":"<h2>8.1 Solution to Exercise 1</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS postgis;\n\nCREATE TABLE fire_station (station_id integer PRIMARY KEY, name text NOT NULL,\n                           geom geometry(Point,28992) NOT NULL);\nCREATE TABLE incident     (incident_id integer PRIMARY KEY,\n                           geom geometry(Point,28992) NOT NULL);\nCREATE TABLE district     (district_id integer PRIMARY KEY, name text NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n                           geom geometry(Polygon,28992) NOT NULL,\n                           CONSTRAINT district_valid CHECK (ST_IsValid(geom)));\n\nINSERT INTO fire_station VALUES\n (1,&#39;S1&#39;,ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(120000,487000),28992)),\n (2,&#39;S2&#39;,ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(123000,487000),28992)),\n (3,&#39;S3&#39;,ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(120000,490000),28992));\nINSERT INTO incident VALUES\n (1,ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(120400,487300),28992)),\n (2,ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(122000,487000),28992)),\n (3,ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(121400,489000),28992));\nINSERT INTO district VALUES\n (1,&#39;West&#39;,ST_MakeEnvelope(119000,486000,122000,491000,28992)),\n (2,&#39;East&#39;,ST_MakeEnvelope(122000,486000,125000,491000,28992));\n\nCREATE INDEX fire_station_geom_gix ON fire_station USING gist (geom);\nCREATE INDEX incident_geom_gix     ON incident     USING gist (geom);\nCREATE INDEX district_geom_gix     ON district     USING gist (geom);\nANALYZE;\n\n-- 2. Metadata\nSELECT f_table_name, srid, type FROM geometry_columns\nWHERE f_table_name IN (&#39;fire_station&#39;,&#39;incident&#39;,&#39;district&#39;) ORDER BY 1;\n\n-- 3. Typmod protection (expected: ERROR  Geometry SRID (4326) does not match column SRID (28992))\nINSERT INTO incident VALUES (99, ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(4.9, 52.37), 4326));\n\n-- 4. Units\nSELECT ST_Distance(&#39;SRID=4326;POINT(0 0)&#39;::geometry,  &#39;SRID=4326;POINT(1 0)&#39;::geometry)  AS deg,\n       ST_Distance(&#39;SRID=4326;POINT(0 0)&#39;::geography, &#39;SRID=4326;POINT(1 0)&#39;::geography) AS metres;\n\n-- 5. DE-9IM\nSELECT d.name, ST_Relate(d.geom, i.geom)\nFROM district d, incident i WHERE i.incident_id = 2 ORDER BY d.name;</code></pre><p><strong>Expected output.</strong> Step 2: <code>district POLYGON 28992</code>, <code>fire_station POINT 28992</code>, <code>incident POINT 28992</code>. Step 4: <code>deg = 1</code>, <code>metres ≈ 111319.49</code>. Step 5: <code>East | FF20F1FF2</code>, <code>West | FF20F1FF2</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Why.</strong> The typmod makes the column reject a different SRID instead of silently storing inconsistent data. The <code>geometry</code> distance is the planar distance in the CRS unit (degrees for EPSG:4326), whereas <code>geography</code> computes on the spheroid and returns metres (<code>6378137 × π/180 ≈ 111319.49</code> along the equator). <code>FF20F1FF2</code> has <code>F</code> in the interior/interior cell, confirming the point is on the boundary and in neither interior.</p>\n<p><strong>How to verify.</strong> Row counts (<code>3, 3, 2</code>), <code>ST_AsText</code> on each table, and checking in QGIS that points fall where the schematic (§5.1) shows them.</p>\n","sha256":"e2a80bb7a65406427ec162f7ffa2da4b0ada399e4f88219e816ab8d64510f477"},{"key":"8.2","id":"8-2","title":"8.2 Solution to Exercise 2","anchor":"82-solution-to-exercise-2","depth":3,"start":61764,"topics":["queries"],"end":64133,"markdown":"### 8.2 Solution to Exercise 2\n\n```sql\n-- 1. Index-friendly rewrite\nSET enable_seqscan = off;\nEXPLAIN\nSELECT s.name, i.incident_id\nFROM incident i JOIN fire_station s ON ST_DWithin(i.geom, s.geom, 2000);\n-- expect: Nested Loop with Index Scan using ..._geom_gix, Index Cond containing &&\nRESET enable_seqscan;\n\nSELECT s.name, i.incident_id\nFROM incident i JOIN fire_station s ON ST_DWithin(i.geom, s.geom, 2000)\nORDER BY s.name, i.incident_id;\n-- S1|1 ; S1|2 ; S2|2 ; S3|3\n\n-- 2. Two nearest stations per incident, with rank\nSELECT i.incident_id,\n       row_number() OVER (PARTITION BY i.incident_id ORDER BY ST_Distance(i.geom, s.geom)) AS rnk,\n       s.name,\n       round(ST_Distance(i.geom, s.geom)::numeric, 2) AS dist_m\nFROM incident i\nCROSS JOIN LATERAL (\n    SELECT name, geom FROM fire_station ORDER BY geom <-> i.geom LIMIT 2\n) s\nORDER BY i.incident_id, rnk;\n\n-- 3. One district per incident (deterministic tiebreak)\nSELECT DISTINCT ON (i.incident_id) i.incident_id, d.name AS district\nFROM incident i\nJOIN district d ON ST_Covers(d.geom, i.geom)\nORDER BY i.incident_id, d.district_id;\n\n-- 4. DBSCAN\nSELECT incident_id, ST_ClusterDBSCAN(geom, eps := 1000, minpoints := 2) OVER () AS c1000,\n                    ST_ClusterDBSCAN(geom, eps := 2000, minpoints := 2) OVER () AS c2000\nFROM incident ORDER BY incident_id;\n```\n\n**Expected results**\n\n| Query | Result |\n|---|---|\n| 2 | I1: rank 1 S1 500.00, rank 2 S2 2617.25 · I2: rank 1 S2 1000.00, rank 2 S1 2000.00 · I3: rank 1 S3 1720.47, rank 2 S1 2441.31 |\n| 3 | I1 West · I2 West (boundary tie broken by smaller `district_id`) · I3 West |\n| 4 | `c1000` = NULL, NULL, NULL; `c2000` = 0, 0, 0 |\n\n**Why.** (1) `ST_DWithin` is expanded to an `&&` index condition plus an exact recheck; `ST_Distance(...) < d` is opaque to the planner. (2) KNN returns the *k* closest rows per outer row; ranks are recomputed from exact distances. (3) A boundary point is covered by both polygons, so an explicit tiebreak makes the assignment deterministic; `ST_Contains` would have assigned it to none. (4) With `eps = 1000` no pair of incidents is that close (closest pair ≈ 1627.88 m), so all are noise; with `eps = 2000` I1 reaches I2 (≈ 1627.88) and I3 (≈ 1972.31), linking all three although I2–I3 (≈ 2088.06) exceeds `eps`.\n\n**How to verify.** Recompute distances with Pythagoras; compare the pair list in query 1 with §5.5.\n\n","lineStart":1107,"lineEnd":1157,"html":"<h2>8.2 Solution to Exercise 2</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">-- 1. Index-friendly rewrite\nSET enable_seqscan = off;\nEXPLAIN\nSELECT s.name, i.incident_id\nFROM incident i JOIN fire_station s ON ST_DWithin(i.geom, s.geom, 2000);\n-- expect: Nested Loop with Index Scan using ..._geom_gix, Index Cond containing &amp;&amp;\nRESET enable_seqscan;\n\nSELECT s.name, i.incident_id\nFROM incident i JOIN fire_station s ON ST_DWithin(i.geom, s.geom, 2000)\nORDER BY s.name, i.incident_id;\n-- S1|1 ; S1|2 ; S2|2 ; S3|3\n\n-- 2. Two nearest stations per incident, with rank\nSELECT i.incident_id,\n       row_number() OVER (PARTITION BY i.incident_id ORDER BY ST_Distance(i.geom, s.geom)) AS rnk,\n       s.name,\n       round(ST_Distance(i.geom, s.geom)::numeric, 2) AS dist_m\nFROM incident i\nCROSS JOIN LATERAL (\n    SELECT name, geom FROM fire_station ORDER BY geom &lt;-&gt; i.geom LIMIT 2\n) s\nORDER BY i.incident_id, rnk;\n\n-- 3. One district per incident (deterministic tiebreak)\nSELECT DISTINCT ON (i.incident_id) i.incident_id, d.name AS district\nFROM incident i\nJOIN district d ON ST_Covers(d.geom, i.geom)\nORDER BY i.incident_id, d.district_id;\n\n-- 4. DBSCAN\nSELECT incident_id, ST_ClusterDBSCAN(geom, eps := 1000, minpoints := 2) OVER () AS c1000,\n                    ST_ClusterDBSCAN(geom, eps := 2000, minpoints := 2) OVER () AS c2000\nFROM incident ORDER BY incident_id;</code></pre><p><strong>Expected results</strong></p>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 8.2 Solution to Exercise 2\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Query</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>I1: rank 1 S1 500.00, rank 2 S2 2617.25 · I2: rank 1 S2 1000.00, rank 2 S1 2000.00 · I3: rank 1 S3 1720.47, rank 2 S1 2441.31</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>I1 West · I2 West (boundary tie broken by smaller <code>district_id</code>) · I3 West</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><code>c1000</code> = NULL, NULL, NULL; <code>c2000</code> = 0, 0, 0</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p><strong>Why.</strong> (1) <code>ST_DWithin</code> is expanded to an <code>&amp;&amp;</code> index condition plus an exact recheck; <code>ST_Distance(...) &lt; d</code> is opaque to the planner. (2) KNN returns the <em>k</em> closest rows per outer row; ranks are recomputed from exact distances. (3) A boundary point is covered by both polygons, so an explicit tiebreak makes the assignment deterministic; <code>ST_Contains</code> would have assigned it to none. (4) With <code>eps = 1000</code> no pair of incidents is that close (closest pair ≈ 1627.88 m), so all are noise; with <code>eps = 2000</code> I1 reaches I2 (≈ 1627.88) and I3 (≈ 1972.31), linking all three although I2–I3 (≈ 2088.06) exceeds <code>eps</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>How to verify.</strong> Recompute distances with Pythagoras; compare the pair list in query 1 with §5.5.</p>\n","sha256":"f3844458ba4955dfe5016391256a0ec19bf91450ae9ed186af3b74df9a7c1dca"},{"key":"8.3","id":"8-3","title":"8.3 Solution to Exercise 3","anchor":"83-solution-to-exercise-3","depth":3,"start":64133,"topics":["routing"],"end":66222,"markdown":"### 8.3 Solution to Exercise 3\n\n```sql\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS pgrouting CASCADE;\nCREATE TABLE toy_edge (id int PRIMARY KEY, source int, target int, cost float8, reverse_cost float8);\nINSERT INTO toy_edge VALUES\n (1,1,2,4,4),(2,1,3,2,2),(3,2,3,1,1),(4,2,4,5,5),(5,3,4,8,8),\n (6,3,5,10,10),(7,4,5,2,2),(8,4,6,6,6),(9,5,6,3,3);\n\n-- 1. Costs from stations (vertices 1 and 6) to incident vertices (3, 4, 5)\nSELECT start_vid, end_vid, agg_cost\nFROM pgr_dijkstraCost('SELECT id, source, target, cost, reverse_cost FROM toy_edge',\n                      ARRAY[1,6], ARRAY[3,4,5], true)\nORDER BY end_vid, start_vid;\n\n-- 3. Service area of S1 with budget 8\nSELECT node, agg_cost\nFROM pgr_drivingDistance('SELECT id, source, target, cost, reverse_cost FROM toy_edge', 1, 8, true)\nORDER BY agg_cost, node;\n\n-- 4. Stretch: edge 9 one-way 5 -> 6\nSELECT end_vid, agg_cost\nFROM pgr_dijkstraCost(\n  'SELECT id, source, target, cost,\n          CASE WHEN id = 9 THEN -1 ELSE reverse_cost END AS reverse_cost\n   FROM toy_edge', 6, 5, true);\n```\n\n**Expected results**\n\n| Incident (vertex) | Cost from S1 (vertex 1) | Cost from S2 (vertex 6) | Network-nearest |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| I1 (3) | 2 | 11 | S1 |\n| I2 (4) | 8 | 5 | S2 |\n| I3 (5) | 10 | 3 | S2 |\n\nService area (budget 8): vertices `1 (0), 3 (2), 2 (3), 4 (8)` → incidents I1 (vertex 3) and I2 (vertex 4) are covered; I3 (vertex 5, cost 10) is not. Stretch: S2→I3 becomes `6→4→5 = 6 + 2 = 8`, so S2 (8) still beats S1 (10).\n\n**Why.** From the §5 coordinates, I3 is Euclidean-closer to S1 (≈ 2441.31) than S2 (≈ 2561.25), but in the invented network S2 reaches I3 through a cheap link (cost 3). Straight-line proximity ignores connectivity, one-way restrictions, and travel cost — the reason network analysis exists. (The mapping is an exercise device; real workflows snap actual coordinates to a noded road network and use lengths or travel times.)\n\n**How to verify.** Hand-run Dijkstra: from vertex 1, settle 1 (0), 3 (2), 2 (3, via 3 rather than the direct edge of 4), 4 (8), 5 (10), 6 (13). From vertex 6: 5 (3), 4 (5, via 5), 2 (10), 3 (11).\n\n","lineStart":1158,"lineEnd":1199,"html":"<h2>8.3 Solution to Exercise 3</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS pgrouting CASCADE;\nCREATE TABLE toy_edge (id int PRIMARY KEY, source int, target int, cost float8, reverse_cost float8);\nINSERT INTO toy_edge VALUES\n (1,1,2,4,4),(2,1,3,2,2),(3,2,3,1,1),(4,2,4,5,5),(5,3,4,8,8),\n (6,3,5,10,10),(7,4,5,2,2),(8,4,6,6,6),(9,5,6,3,3);\n\n-- 1. Costs from stations (vertices 1 and 6) to incident vertices (3, 4, 5)\nSELECT start_vid, end_vid, agg_cost\nFROM pgr_dijkstraCost(&#39;SELECT id, source, target, cost, reverse_cost FROM toy_edge&#39;,\n                      ARRAY[1,6], ARRAY[3,4,5], true)\nORDER BY end_vid, start_vid;\n\n-- 3. Service area of S1 with budget 8\nSELECT node, agg_cost\nFROM pgr_drivingDistance(&#39;SELECT id, source, target, cost, reverse_cost FROM toy_edge&#39;, 1, 8, true)\nORDER BY agg_cost, node;\n\n-- 4. Stretch: edge 9 one-way 5 -&gt; 6\nSELECT end_vid, agg_cost\nFROM pgr_dijkstraCost(\n  &#39;SELECT id, source, target, cost,\n          CASE WHEN id = 9 THEN -1 ELSE reverse_cost END AS reverse_cost\n   FROM toy_edge&#39;, 6, 5, true);</code></pre><p><strong>Expected results</strong></p>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 8.3 Solution to Exercise 3\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Incident (vertex)</th>\n<th>Cost from S1 (vertex 1)</th>\n<th>Cost from S2 (vertex 6)</th>\n<th>Network-nearest</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>I1 (3)</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>S1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I2 (4)</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>S2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I3 (5)</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>S2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p>Service area (budget 8): vertices <code>1 (0), 3 (2), 2 (3), 4 (8)</code> → incidents I1 (vertex 3) and I2 (vertex 4) are covered; I3 (vertex 5, cost 10) is not. Stretch: S2→I3 becomes <code>6→4→5 = 6 + 2 = 8</code>, so S2 (8) still beats S1 (10).</p>\n<p><strong>Why.</strong> From the §5 coordinates, I3 is Euclidean-closer to S1 (≈ 2441.31) than S2 (≈ 2561.25), but in the invented network S2 reaches I3 through a cheap link (cost 3). Straight-line proximity ignores connectivity, one-way restrictions, and travel cost — the reason network analysis exists. (The mapping is an exercise device; real workflows snap actual coordinates to a noded road network and use lengths or travel times.)</p>\n<p><strong>How to verify.</strong> Hand-run Dijkstra: from vertex 1, settle 1 (0), 3 (2), 2 (3, via 3 rather than the direct edge of 4), 4 (8), 5 (10), 6 (13). From vertex 6: 5 (3), 4 (5, via 5), 2 (10), 3 (11).</p>\n","sha256":"e7caabdbe25086fb2f6dcdc0b1ec3768323d49b96e3c1b223c0a497738bd8c2c"},{"key":"8.4","id":"8-4","title":"8.4 Solution to Exercise 4","anchor":"84-solution-to-exercise-4","depth":3,"start":66222,"topics":["registry"],"end":72338,"markdown":"### 8.4 Solution to Exercise 4\n\n```sql\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS postgis;\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS btree_gist;\n\nCREATE TABLE station_version (\n    version_id   bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n    station_id   integer NOT NULL,\n    name         text NOT NULL,\n    geom         geometry(Point, 28992) NOT NULL,\n    valid_during tstzrange NOT NULL,\n    CONSTRAINT sv_valid   CHECK (ST_IsValid(geom)),\n    CONSTRAINT sv_nonempty CHECK (NOT ST_IsEmpty(geom)),\n    CONSTRAINT sv_extent  CHECK (geom && ST_MakeEnvelope(0, 289000, 300000, 629000, 28992)),\n    CONSTRAINT sv_nonempty_range CHECK (NOT isempty(valid_during)),\n    CONSTRAINT no_overlapping_versions\n        EXCLUDE USING gist (station_id WITH =, valid_during WITH &&)\n);\nCREATE INDEX station_version_geom_gix ON station_version USING gist (geom);\nCREATE INDEX station_version_valid_gix ON station_version USING gist (valid_during);\n\nINSERT INTO station_version (station_id, name, geom, valid_during) VALUES\n (1,'S1',ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(120000,487000),28992), tstzrange('2020-01-01 00:00+00', NULL, '[)')),\n (3,'S3',ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(120000,490000),28992), tstzrange('2020-01-01 00:00+00', NULL, '[)')),\n (2,'S2',ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(123000,487000),28992), tstzrange('2020-01-01 00:00+00','2026-01-01 00:00+00','[)')),\n (2,'S2',ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(125000,487000),28992), tstzrange('2026-01-01 00:00+00', NULL, '[)'));\n\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION relocate_station(p_station_id int, p_new_geom geometry, p_effective timestamptz)\nRETURNS void LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$\nDECLARE\n    cur station_version%ROWTYPE;\nBEGIN\n    SELECT * INTO cur FROM station_version\n    WHERE station_id = p_station_id AND upper_inf(valid_during)\n    FOR UPDATE;                                           -- serialise concurrent relocations\n\n    IF NOT FOUND THEN\n        RAISE EXCEPTION 'No open version for station %', p_station_id;\n    END IF;\n    IF p_effective <= lower(cur.valid_during) THEN\n        RAISE EXCEPTION 'Effective date % must be after current version start %',\n                        p_effective, lower(cur.valid_during);\n    END IF;\n\n    UPDATE station_version\n       SET valid_during = tstzrange(lower(valid_during), p_effective, '[)')\n     WHERE version_id = cur.version_id;\n\n    INSERT INTO station_version (station_id, name, geom, valid_during)\n    VALUES (p_station_id, cur.name, p_new_geom, tstzrange(p_effective, NULL, '[)'));\nEND $$;\n\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION nearest_station_asof(p_point geometry, p_asof timestamptz)\nRETURNS TABLE (station_id int, name text, dist_m double precision)\nLANGUAGE sql STABLE AS $$\n    SELECT sv.station_id, sv.name, ST_Distance(sv.geom, p_point)\n    FROM station_version sv\n    WHERE sv.valid_during @> p_asof\n    ORDER BY sv.geom <-> p_point\n    LIMIT 1;\n$$;\n\n-- As-of queries\nSELECT i.incident_id, n.name, round(n.dist_m::numeric, 2) AS dist_m\nFROM incident i, LATERAL nearest_station_asof(i.geom, timestamptz '2025-06-01 00:00+00') n\nORDER BY i.incident_id;\nSELECT i.incident_id, n.name, round(n.dist_m::numeric, 2) AS dist_m\nFROM incident i, LATERAL nearest_station_asof(i.geom, timestamptz '2026-06-01 00:00+00') n\nORDER BY i.incident_id;\n\n-- Negative tests (each must FAIL)\nINSERT INTO station_version (station_id,name,geom,valid_during)         -- overlaps S2's current version\nVALUES (2,'S2',ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(124000,487000),28992), tstzrange('2025-12-01 00:00+00','2026-03-01 00:00+00','[)'));\nINSERT INTO station_version (station_id,name,geom,valid_during)         -- wrong SRID\nVALUES (4,'X',ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(4.9,52.37),4326), tstzrange('2020-01-01 00:00+00', NULL, '[)'));\nINSERT INTO station_version (station_id,name,geom,valid_during)         -- outside the extent\nVALUES (5,'Y',ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(900000,487000),28992), tstzrange('2020-01-01 00:00+00', NULL, '[)'));\n```\n\n**Expected results**\n\n| As-of | I1 | I2 | I3 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 2025-06-01 | S1, 500.00 | S2, 1000.00 | S3, 1720.47 |\n| 2026-06-01 | S1, 500.00 | **S1, 2000.00** | S3, 1720.47 |\n\nNegative tests: the first raises `conflicting key value violates exclusion constraint \"no_overlapping_versions\"` (SQLSTATE `23P01`); the second raises `Geometry SRID (4326) does not match column SRID (28992)`; the third violates `sv_extent` (check-constraint violation, `23514`). Calling `relocate_station(2, <new point>, '2026-01-01 00:00+00')` against this data is rejected by the function, because the effective date is not after the start of the station's open version (which already begins on 2026-01-01).\n\n**Why.** Half-open ranges let one version end exactly where the next begins without overlapping, and the exclusion constraint makes that invariant a database guarantee rather than an application convention. After S2 moves 2 km farther east, I2's distance to S2 grows to 3000 m, so S1 (2000 m) becomes nearest — this is exactly the history-dependent answer an overwritten-in-place `UPDATE` would have destroyed. The extent check uses the `&&` operator so the constraint is cheap and also catches coordinates accidentally entered in the wrong CRS.\n\n**Performance note (step 6).** In `nearest_station_asof`, the `valid_during @> p_asof` filter is applied *after* the GiST KNN ordering returns candidates. If most rows are historical versions, the index scan may walk many non-current rows before finding one valid at the requested date, degrading the query as history accumulates. Mitigations: a **partial index** on current rows (`WHERE upper_inf(valid_during)`) for the very common \"now\" query; a combined GiST index on `(geom, valid_during)` using `btree_gist`/range operator support; or moving closed versions to an **archive partition** so the hot table stays small. Always confirm with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` on realistic data volumes.\n\n**How to verify.** Count open versions per station (`SELECT station_id, count(*) FROM station_version WHERE upper_inf(valid_during) GROUP BY 1` → exactly one each); check there are no overlaps with a self-join on `valid_during && valid_during` (should return zero rows); recompute the two as-of tables by hand with Pythagoras (§5.4).\n\n---\n","lineStart":1200,"lineEnd":1295,"html":"<h2>8.4 Solution to Exercise 4</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS postgis;\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS btree_gist;\n\nCREATE TABLE station_version (\n    version_id   bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n    station_id   integer NOT NULL,\n    name         text NOT NULL,\n    geom         geometry(Point, 28992) NOT NULL,\n    valid_during tstzrange NOT NULL,\n    CONSTRAINT sv_valid   CHECK (ST_IsValid(geom)),\n    CONSTRAINT sv_nonempty CHECK (NOT ST_IsEmpty(geom)),\n    CONSTRAINT sv_extent  CHECK (geom &amp;&amp; ST_MakeEnvelope(0, 289000, 300000, 629000, 28992)),\n    CONSTRAINT sv_nonempty_range CHECK (NOT isempty(valid_during)),\n    CONSTRAINT no_overlapping_versions\n        EXCLUDE USING gist (station_id WITH =, valid_during WITH &amp;&amp;)\n);\nCREATE INDEX station_version_geom_gix ON station_version USING gist (geom);\nCREATE INDEX station_version_valid_gix ON station_version USING gist (valid_during);\n\nINSERT INTO station_version (station_id, name, geom, valid_during) VALUES\n (1,&#39;S1&#39;,ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(120000,487000),28992), tstzrange(&#39;2020-01-01 00:00+00&#39;, NULL, &#39;[)&#39;)),\n (3,&#39;S3&#39;,ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(120000,490000),28992), tstzrange(&#39;2020-01-01 00:00+00&#39;, NULL, &#39;[)&#39;)),\n (2,&#39;S2&#39;,ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(123000,487000),28992), tstzrange(&#39;2020-01-01 00:00+00&#39;,&#39;2026-01-01 00:00+00&#39;,&#39;[)&#39;)),\n (2,&#39;S2&#39;,ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(125000,487000),28992), tstzrange(&#39;2026-01-01 00:00+00&#39;, NULL, &#39;[)&#39;));\n\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION relocate_station(p_station_id int, p_new_geom geometry, p_effective timestamptz)\nRETURNS void LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$\nDECLARE\n    cur station_version%ROWTYPE;\nBEGIN\n    SELECT * INTO cur FROM station_version\n    WHERE station_id = p_station_id AND upper_inf(valid_during)\n    FOR UPDATE;                                           -- serialise concurrent relocations\n\n    IF NOT FOUND THEN\n        RAISE EXCEPTION &#39;No open version for station %&#39;, p_station_id;\n    END IF;\n    IF p_effective &lt;= lower(cur.valid_during) THEN\n        RAISE EXCEPTION &#39;Effective date % must be after current version start %&#39;,\n                        p_effective, lower(cur.valid_during);\n    END IF;\n\n    UPDATE station_version\n       SET valid_during = tstzrange(lower(valid_during), p_effective, &#39;[)&#39;)\n     WHERE version_id = cur.version_id;\n\n    INSERT INTO station_version (station_id, name, geom, valid_during)\n    VALUES (p_station_id, cur.name, p_new_geom, tstzrange(p_effective, NULL, &#39;[)&#39;));\nEND $$;\n\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION nearest_station_asof(p_point geometry, p_asof timestamptz)\nRETURNS TABLE (station_id int, name text, dist_m double precision)\nLANGUAGE sql STABLE AS $$\n    SELECT sv.station_id, sv.name, ST_Distance(sv.geom, p_point)\n    FROM station_version sv\n    WHERE sv.valid_during @&gt; p_asof\n    ORDER BY sv.geom &lt;-&gt; p_point\n    LIMIT 1;\n$$;\n\n-- As-of queries\nSELECT i.incident_id, n.name, round(n.dist_m::numeric, 2) AS dist_m\nFROM incident i, LATERAL nearest_station_asof(i.geom, timestamptz &#39;2025-06-01 00:00+00&#39;) n\nORDER BY i.incident_id;\nSELECT i.incident_id, n.name, round(n.dist_m::numeric, 2) AS dist_m\nFROM incident i, LATERAL nearest_station_asof(i.geom, timestamptz &#39;2026-06-01 00:00+00&#39;) n\nORDER BY i.incident_id;\n\n-- Negative tests (each must FAIL)\nINSERT INTO station_version (station_id,name,geom,valid_during)         -- overlaps S2&#39;s current version\nVALUES (2,&#39;S2&#39;,ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(124000,487000),28992), tstzrange(&#39;2025-12-01 00:00+00&#39;,&#39;2026-03-01 00:00+00&#39;,&#39;[)&#39;));\nINSERT INTO station_version (station_id,name,geom,valid_during)         -- wrong SRID\nVALUES (4,&#39;X&#39;,ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(4.9,52.37),4326), tstzrange(&#39;2020-01-01 00:00+00&#39;, NULL, &#39;[)&#39;));\nINSERT INTO station_version (station_id,name,geom,valid_during)         -- outside the extent\nVALUES (5,&#39;Y&#39;,ST_SetSRID(ST_MakePoint(900000,487000),28992), tstzrange(&#39;2020-01-01 00:00+00&#39;, NULL, &#39;[)&#39;));</code></pre><p><strong>Expected results</strong></p>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 8.4 Solution to Exercise 4\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>As-of</th>\n<th>I1</th>\n<th>I2</th>\n<th>I3</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>2025-06-01</td>\n<td>S1, 500.00</td>\n<td>S2, 1000.00</td>\n<td>S3, 1720.47</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2026-06-01</td>\n<td>S1, 500.00</td>\n<td><strong>S1, 2000.00</strong></td>\n<td>S3, 1720.47</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div><p>Negative tests: the first raises <code>conflicting key value violates exclusion constraint &quot;no_overlapping_versions&quot;</code> (SQLSTATE <code>23P01</code>); the second raises <code>Geometry SRID (4326) does not match column SRID (28992)</code>; the third violates <code>sv_extent</code> (check-constraint violation, <code>23514</code>). Calling <code>relocate_station(2, &lt;new point&gt;, &#39;2026-01-01 00:00+00&#39;)</code> against this data is rejected by the function, because the effective date is not after the start of the station&#39;s open version (which already begins on 2026-01-01).</p>\n<p><strong>Why.</strong> Half-open ranges let one version end exactly where the next begins without overlapping, and the exclusion constraint makes that invariant a database guarantee rather than an application convention. After S2 moves 2 km farther east, I2&#39;s distance to S2 grows to 3000 m, so S1 (2000 m) becomes nearest — this is exactly the history-dependent answer an overwritten-in-place <code>UPDATE</code> would have destroyed. The extent check uses the <code>&amp;&amp;</code> operator so the constraint is cheap and also catches coordinates accidentally entered in the wrong CRS.</p>\n<p><strong>Performance note (step 6).</strong> In <code>nearest_station_asof</code>, the <code>valid_during @&gt; p_asof</code> filter is applied <em>after</em> the GiST KNN ordering returns candidates. If most rows are historical versions, the index scan may walk many non-current rows before finding one valid at the requested date, degrading the query as history accumulates. Mitigations: a <strong>partial index</strong> on current rows (<code>WHERE upper_inf(valid_during)</code>) for the very common &quot;now&quot; query; a combined GiST index on <code>(geom, valid_during)</code> using <code>btree_gist</code>/range operator support; or moving closed versions to an <strong>archive partition</strong> so the hot table stays small. Always confirm with <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code> on realistic data volumes.</p>\n<p><strong>How to verify.</strong> Count open versions per station (<code>SELECT station_id, count(*) FROM station_version WHERE upper_inf(valid_during) GROUP BY 1</code> → exactly one each); check there are no overlaps with a self-join on <code>valid_during &amp;&amp; valid_during</code> (should return zero rows); recompute the two as-of tables by hand with Pythagoras (§5.4).</p>\n<hr>\n","sha256":"17fb06ff9b2bd9cc5e477fcd95e6ac6c989ad47ae4437e87d96a4a7f22d36bf6"},{"key":"9","id":"9","title":"9. Summary, Glossary, and Further Reading","anchor":"9-summary-glossary-and-further-reading","depth":2,"start":72338,"topics":["modeling"],"end":72384,"markdown":"## 9. Summary, Glossary, and Further Reading\n\n","lineStart":1296,"lineEnd":1297,"html":"<h2>9. Summary, Glossary, and Further Reading</h2>\n","sha256":"22915f5478beaff92370282a76c620985a93e26dfd77dcb9ee4693c706d9c9e6"},{"key":"9.1","id":"9-1","title":"9.1 Key takeaways","anchor":"91-key-takeaways","depth":3,"start":72384,"topics":["modeling"],"end":74070,"markdown":"### 9.1 Key takeaways\n\n1. **Types follow the question.** Use `geometry` in a suitable projected CRS for regional, analysis-heavy work; use `geography` when you need metres on a global scale with minimal CRS handling. Never trust units until you have checked the SRID.\n2. **Indexes are bbox filters.** GiST makes spatial predicates fast through *filter-and-refine*. Write predicates the planner can rewrite to `&&`: `ST_DWithin`, `ST_Intersects`, `ST_Contains`, … — not `ST_Distance(...) < d`, and not expressions that wrap the indexed column.\n3. **Read plans, not hunches.** `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)`, fresh statistics (`ANALYZE`), and `ST_Subdivide` for large polygons solve most spatial performance problems.\n4. **Pattern vocabulary.** `ST_DWithin` to filter, `<->` with `ORDER BY … LIMIT` for KNN, `ST_ClusterDBSCAN`/grids for clustering, `ST_TileEnvelope` + `ST_AsMVTGeom` + `ST_AsMVT` for tiles.\n5. **Boundaries matter.** `ST_Contains` vs `ST_Covers` vs `ST_Intersects` differ for boundary contact; encode a deterministic tiebreak when you must assign features to exactly one polygon.\n6. **Networks need graphs, not straight lines.** pgRouting turns noded, costed edges into shortest paths, cost matrices, and service areas; straight-line proximity can rank facilities differently.\n7. **Choose the engine for the workload.** PostGIS for multi-user transactional systems; SpatiaLite/GeoPackage for single-file portability; DuckDB for analytics over files; Oracle/SQL Server when the enterprise already standardises on them.\n8. **Model for trust.** Typmod, `CHECK` constraints, exclusion constraints on valid-time ranges, and topology audits keep spatial data correct over time.\n\n","lineStart":1298,"lineEnd":1308,"html":"<h2>9.1 Key takeaways</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Types follow the question.</strong> Use <code>geometry</code> in a suitable projected CRS for regional, analysis-heavy work; use <code>geography</code> when you need metres on a global scale with minimal CRS handling. Never trust units until you have checked the SRID.</li>\n<li><strong>Indexes are bbox filters.</strong> GiST makes spatial predicates fast through <em>filter-and-refine</em>. Write predicates the planner can rewrite to <code>&amp;&amp;</code>: <code>ST_DWithin</code>, <code>ST_Intersects</code>, <code>ST_Contains</code>, … — not <code>ST_Distance(...) &lt; d</code>, and not expressions that wrap the indexed column.</li>\n<li><strong>Read plans, not hunches.</strong> <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code>, fresh statistics (<code>ANALYZE</code>), and <code>ST_Subdivide</code> for large polygons solve most spatial performance problems.</li>\n<li><strong>Pattern vocabulary.</strong> <code>ST_DWithin</code> to filter, <code>&lt;-&gt;</code> with <code>ORDER BY … LIMIT</code> for KNN, <code>ST_ClusterDBSCAN</code>/grids for clustering, <code>ST_TileEnvelope</code> + <code>ST_AsMVTGeom</code> + <code>ST_AsMVT</code> for tiles.</li>\n<li><strong>Boundaries matter.</strong> <code>ST_Contains</code> vs <code>ST_Covers</code> vs <code>ST_Intersects</code> differ for boundary contact; encode a deterministic tiebreak when you must assign features to exactly one polygon.</li>\n<li><strong>Networks need graphs, not straight lines.</strong> pgRouting turns noded, costed edges into shortest paths, cost matrices, and service areas; straight-line proximity can rank facilities differently.</li>\n<li><strong>Choose the engine for the workload.</strong> PostGIS for multi-user transactional systems; SpatiaLite/GeoPackage for single-file portability; DuckDB for analytics over files; Oracle/SQL Server when the enterprise already standardises on them.</li>\n<li><strong>Model for trust.</strong> Typmod, <code>CHECK</code> constraints, exclusion constraints on valid-time ranges, and topology audits keep spatial data correct over time.</li>\n</ol>\n","sha256":"698ee24a26462104d6f6f46c9ca67396c67c5e8f5c9fd5e980f337d45c01d70b"},{"key":"9.2","id":"9-2","title":"9.2 Glossary","anchor":"92-glossary","depth":3,"start":74070,"topics":["modeling"],"end":76113,"markdown":"### 9.2 Glossary\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Bounding box (MBR)** | Axis-aligned rectangle enclosing a geometry; what spatial indexes store |\n| **BRIN** | Block Range INdex; tiny index storing summaries per block range, effective for physically ordered data |\n| **Covers / Contains** | `Covers` allows boundary contact; `Contains` requires interior contact (see DE-9IM) |\n| **DBSCAN** | Density-Based Spatial Clustering of Applications with Noise; clusters points that are density-reachable within `eps` with at least `minpoints` |\n| **DE-9IM** | 3×3 matrix of interior/boundary/exterior intersection dimensions defining spatial predicates |\n| **Edge / vertex (graph)** | Road segment / intersection or endpoint in a routing network |\n| **EPSG code** | Numeric identifier of a CRS in the EPSG registry (e.g. 4326, 28992, 3857) |\n| **Exclusion constraint** | PostgreSQL constraint ensuring no two rows satisfy given operators simultaneously (e.g. same id and overlapping range) |\n| **Filter-and-refine** | Index-based candidate selection followed by exact geometry testing |\n| **GiST** | Generalized Search Tree; PostGIS's default spatial index access method |\n| **KNN** | k-nearest neighbours search, supported through `ORDER BY geom <-> point LIMIT k` |\n| **MVT** | Mapbox Vector Tile; protobuf-encoded vector tile format |\n| **Noding** | Splitting lines at intersections so they connect at vertices in a routing graph |\n| **R-tree** | Tree index of nested bounding rectangles; GiST spatial indexes are R-tree-like |\n| **SRID** | Spatial Reference ID stored with a PostGIS geometry |\n| **Service area (isochrone)** | Set of locations reachable within a cost budget |\n| **TOAST** | PostgreSQL mechanism for storing large values out-of-line |\n| **Typmod** | Type modifier restricting a column's geometry type, SRID, and dimensions |\n| **Valid time** | Period during which a fact is true in the real world |\n| **XYZ tiles** | Web map tile addressing scheme: zoom `z`, column `x`, row `y` (origin at top-left of the Web Mercator extent) |\n\n","lineStart":1309,"lineEnd":1333,"html":"<h2>9.2 Glossary</h2>\n<div class=\"source-table\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Table in 9.2 Glossary\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Bounding box (MBR)</strong></td>\n<td>Axis-aligned rectangle enclosing a geometry; what spatial indexes store</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>BRIN</strong></td>\n<td>Block Range INdex; tiny index storing summaries per block range, effective for physically ordered data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Covers / Contains</strong></td>\n<td><code>Covers</code> allows boundary contact; <code>Contains</code> requires interior contact (see DE-9IM)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>DBSCAN</strong></td>\n<td>Density-Based Spatial Clustering of Applications with Noise; clusters points that are density-reachable within <code>eps</code> with at least <code>minpoints</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>DE-9IM</strong></td>\n<td>3×3 matrix of interior/boundary/exterior intersection dimensions defining spatial predicates</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Edge / vertex (graph)</strong></td>\n<td>Road segment / intersection or endpoint in a routing network</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>EPSG code</strong></td>\n<td>Numeric identifier of a CRS in the EPSG registry (e.g. 4326, 28992, 3857)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Exclusion constraint</strong></td>\n<td>PostgreSQL constraint ensuring no two rows satisfy given operators simultaneously (e.g. same id and overlapping range)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Filter-and-refine</strong></td>\n<td>Index-based candidate selection followed by exact geometry testing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>GiST</strong></td>\n<td>Generalized Search Tree; PostGIS&#39;s default spatial index access method</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>KNN</strong></td>\n<td>k-nearest neighbours search, supported through <code>ORDER BY geom &lt;-&gt; point LIMIT k</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>MVT</strong></td>\n<td>Mapbox Vector Tile; protobuf-encoded vector tile format</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Noding</strong></td>\n<td>Splitting lines at intersections so they connect at vertices in a routing graph</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>R-tree</strong></td>\n<td>Tree index of nested bounding rectangles; GiST spatial indexes are R-tree-like</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>SRID</strong></td>\n<td>Spatial Reference ID stored with a PostGIS geometry</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Service area (isochrone)</strong></td>\n<td>Set of locations reachable within a cost budget</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>TOAST</strong></td>\n<td>PostgreSQL mechanism for storing large values out-of-line</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Typmod</strong></td>\n<td>Type modifier restricting a column&#39;s geometry type, SRID, and dimensions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Valid time</strong></td>\n<td>Period during which a fact is true in the real world</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>XYZ tiles</strong></td>\n<td>Web map tile addressing scheme: zoom <code>z</code>, column <code>x</code>, row <code>y</code> (origin at top-left of the Web Mercator extent)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>","sha256":"137717e739c81b0a6ec2d605e926204d1c466a150b8786715a7734386d271119"},{"key":"9.3","id":"9-3","title":"9.3 Further reading","anchor":"93-further-reading","depth":3,"start":76113,"topics":["modeling"],"end":77477,"markdown":"### 9.3 Further reading\n\nLinks are given as top-level sites or well-known documentation roots only; navigate from there to the page for your installed version.\n\n* **PostGIS documentation and manual** — `https://postgis.net` (function reference, `geometry`/`geography` FAQ, performance tips, tiling and raster chapters).\n* **PostgreSQL documentation** — `https://www.postgresql.org/docs/` (GiST/SP-GiST/BRIN indexes, `EXPLAIN`, range types, exclusion constraints, partitioning).\n* **pgRouting documentation** — `https://docs.pgrouting.org` (function signatures per version, migration notes from 3.x to 4.x).\n* **DuckDB spatial extension** — `https://duckdb.org` (extension documentation under *Extensions → Spatial*).\n* **SpatiaLite** — `https://www.gaia-gis.it` (SpatiaLite SQL function reference and cookbook).\n* **Oracle Spatial** — `https://docs.oracle.com` (*Spatial and Graph Developer's Guide*).\n* **SQL Server spatial data** — `https://learn.microsoft.com` (search for *Spatial Data (SQL Server)*).\n* **OGC Simple Features** — `https://www.ogc.org` (Simple Feature Access specification; ISO 19125 equivalent).\n* **EPSG Geodetic Parameter Dataset** — `https://epsg.org` (authoritative CRS definitions).\n* **Book:** Regina O. Obe and Leo S. Hsu, *PostGIS in Action* (Manning Publications) — practical, example-driven coverage of PostGIS and related tooling.\n\n","lineStart":1334,"lineEnd":1348,"html":"<h2>9.3 Further reading</h2>\n<p>Links are given as top-level sites or well-known documentation roots only; navigate from there to the page for your installed version.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>PostGIS documentation and manual</strong> — <code>https://postgis.net</code> (function reference, <code>geometry</code>/<code>geography</code> FAQ, performance tips, tiling and raster chapters).</li>\n<li><strong>PostgreSQL documentation</strong> — <code>https://www.postgresql.org/docs/</code> (GiST/SP-GiST/BRIN indexes, <code>EXPLAIN</code>, range types, exclusion constraints, partitioning).</li>\n<li><strong>pgRouting documentation</strong> — <code>https://docs.pgrouting.org</code> (function signatures per version, migration notes from 3.x to 4.x).</li>\n<li><strong>DuckDB spatial extension</strong> — <code>https://duckdb.org</code> (extension documentation under <em>Extensions → Spatial</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>SpatiaLite</strong> — <code>https://www.gaia-gis.it</code> (SpatiaLite SQL function reference and cookbook).</li>\n<li><strong>Oracle Spatial</strong> — <code>https://docs.oracle.com</code> (<em>Spatial and Graph Developer&#39;s Guide</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>SQL Server spatial data</strong> — <code>https://learn.microsoft.com</code> (search for <em>Spatial Data (SQL Server)</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>OGC Simple Features</strong> — <code>https://www.ogc.org</code> (Simple Feature Access specification; ISO 19125 equivalent).</li>\n<li><strong>EPSG Geodetic Parameter Dataset</strong> — <code>https://epsg.org</code> (authoritative CRS definitions).</li>\n<li><strong>Book:</strong> Regina O. Obe and Leo S. Hsu, <em>PostGIS in Action</em> (Manning Publications) — practical, example-driven coverage of PostGIS and related tooling.</li>\n</ul>\n","sha256":"c96f9a06a8d335f137fe89b6ce0d37370aab45e8004262e6c807f90ed822d339"},{"key":"9.4","id":"9-4","title":"9.4 Where to go next","anchor":"94-where-to-go-next","depth":3,"start":77477,"topics":["modeling"],"end":77902,"markdown":"### 9.4 Where to go next\n\n* Port the §5 dataset to DuckDB and SpatiaLite and compare results and syntax.\n* Build a real road graph from an OSM extract with `osm2pgrouting`, snap points with KNN, and compute service areas.\n* Add `pg_tileserv` or Martin in front of `incident_mvt` and render the tiles in a web map.\n* Write an automated spatial-QA script that runs the validity, overlap, and gap queries of §4.8 on every load.\n","lineStart":1349,"lineEnd":1354,"html":"<h2>9.4 Where to go next</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Port the §5 dataset to DuckDB and SpatiaLite and compare results and syntax.</li>\n<li>Build a real road graph from an OSM extract with <code>osm2pgrouting</code>, snap points with KNN, and compute service areas.</li>\n<li>Add <code>pg_tileserv</code> or Martin in front of <code>incident_mvt</code> and render the tiles in a web map.</li>\n<li>Write an automated spatial-QA script that runs the validity, overlap, and gap queries of §4.8 on every load.</li>\n</ul>\n","sha256":"9c1ff9e2e2bb98388fb745c72be037f7c6765681ff6f2c749def0858b7aa76bf"}]};
